#
# Techniques and sub-techniques
#

attack:T0800
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Activate Firmware Update Mode"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may activate firmware update mode on devices to prevent expected response functions from engaging in reaction to an emergency or process malfunction. For example, devices such as protection relays may have an operation mode designed for firmware installation. This mode may halt process monitoring and related functions to allow new firmware to be loaded. A device left in update mode may be placed in an inactive holding state if no firmware is provided to it."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0800" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0107 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0800> ;
	.

attack:T0801
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Monitor Process State"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather information about the physical process state. This information may be used to gain more information about the process itself or used as a trigger for malicious actions. The sources of process state information may vary such as, OPC tags, historian data, specific PLC block information, or network traffic."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0801" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0100 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-25"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0801> ;
	.

attack:T0802
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Automated Collection"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may automate collection of industrial environment information using tools or scripts. This automated collection may leverage native control protocols and tools available in the control systems environment. For example, the OPC protocol may be used to enumerate and gather information. Access to a system or interface with these native protocols may allow collection and enumeration of other attached, communicating servers and devices."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0802" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0100 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0802> ;
	.

attack:T0803
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Block Command Message"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may block a command message from reaching its intended target to prevent command execution. In OT networks, command messages are sent to provide instructions to control system devices. A blocked command message can inhibit response functions from correcting a disruption or unsafe condition. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1691.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0803" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0107 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1691.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-20"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0803> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T0804
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Block Reporting Message"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may block or prevent a reporting message from reaching its intended target. In control systems, reporting messages contain telemetry data (e.g., I/O values) pertaining to the current state of equipment and the industrial process. By blocking these reporting messages, an adversary can potentially hide their actions from an operator. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1691.002."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0804" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0107 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1691.002 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-20"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0804> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T0805
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Block Serial COM"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may block access to serial COM to prevent instructions or configurations from reaching target devices. Serial Communication ports (COM) allow communication with control system devices. Devices can receive command and configuration messages over such serial COM. Devices also use serial COM to send command and reporting messages. Blocking device serial COM may also block command messages and block reporting messages. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1695.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0805" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0107 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1695.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-20"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0805> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T0806
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Brute Force I/O"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may repetitively or successively change I/O point values to perform an action. Brute Force I/O may be achieved by changing either a range of I/O point values or a single point value repeatedly to manipulate a process function. The adversary's goal and the information they have about the target environment will influence which of the options they choose."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0806" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0106 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0806> ;
	.

attack:T0807
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Command-Line Interface"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may utilize command-line interfaces (CLIs) to interact with systems and execute commands. CLIs provide a means of interacting with computer systems and are a common feature across many types of platforms and devices within control systems environments. Adversaries may also use CLIs to install and run new software, including malicious tools that may be installed over the course of an operation."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0807" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0104 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0807> ;
	.

attack:T0808
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Control Device Identification"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may perform control device identification to determine the make and model of a target device. Management software and device APIs may be utilized by the adversary to gain this information. By identifying and obtaining device specifics, the adversary may be able to determine device vulnerabilities. This device information can also be used to understand device functionality and inform the decision to target the environment. Deprecated by MITRE ATT&CK."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0808" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0102 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Field Controller/RTU/PLC/IED" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0808> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T0809
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Data Destruction"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may perform data destruction over the course of an operation. The adversary may drop or create malware, tools, or other non-native files on a target system to accomplish this, potentially leaving behind traces of malicious activities. Such non-native files and other data may be removed over the course of an intrusion to maintain a small footprint or as a standard part of the post-intrusion cleanup process."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0809" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0107 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0809> ;
	.

attack:T0810
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Data Historian Compromise"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may compromise and gain control of a data historian to gain a foothold into the control system environment. Access to a data historian may be used to learn stored database archival and analysis information on the control system. A dual-homed data historian may provide adversaries an interface from the IT environment to the OT environment. Deprecated by MITRE ATT&CK."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0810" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0108 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0810> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T0811
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Data from Information Repositories"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may target and collect data from information repositories. This can include sensitive data such as specifications, schematics, or diagrams of control system layouts, devices, and processes. Examples of information repositories include reference databases in the process environment, as well as databases in the corporate network that might contain information about the ICS."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0811" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0100 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0811> ;
	.

attack:T0812
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Default Credentials"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may leverage manufacturer or supplier set default credentials on control system devices. These default credentials may have administrative permissions and may be necessary for initial configuration of the device. It is general best practice to change the passwords for these accounts as soon as possible, but some manufacturers may have devices that have passwords or usernames that cannot be changed. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1694.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0812" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0109 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1694.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-20"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0812> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T0813
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Denial of Control"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may cause a denial of control to temporarily prevent operators and engineers from interacting with process controls. An adversary may attempt to deny process control access to cause a temporary loss of communication with the control device or to prevent operator adjustment of process controls. An affected process may still be operating during the period of control loss, but not necessarily in a desired state."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0813" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0105 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0813> ;
	.

attack:T0814
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Denial of Service"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may perform Denial-of-Service (DoS) attacks to disrupt expected device functionality. Examples of DoS attacks include overwhelming the target device with a high volume of requests in a short time period and sending the target device a request it does not know how to handle. Disrupting device state may temporarily render it unresponsive, possibly lasting until a reboot can occur."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0814" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0107 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0814> ;
	.

attack:T0815
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Denial of View"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may cause a denial of view in attempt to disrupt and prevent operator oversight on the status of an ICS environment. This may manifest itself as a temporary communication failure between a device and its control source, where the interface recovers and becomes available once the interference ceases."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0815" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0105 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0815> ;
	.

attack:T0816
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Device Restart/Shutdown"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may forcibly restart or shutdown a device in an ICS environment to disrupt and potentially negatively impact physical processes. Methods of device restart and shutdown exist in some devices as built-in, standard functionalities. These functionalities can be executed using interactive device web interfaces, CLIs, and network protocol commands."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0816" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0107 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0816> ;
	.

attack:T0817
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Drive-by Compromise"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gain access to a system during a drive-by compromise, when a user visits a website as part of a regular browsing session. With this technique, the user's web browser is targeted and exploited simply by visiting the compromised website."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0817" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0108 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0817> ;
	.

attack:T0818
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Engineering Workstation Compromise"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries will compromise and gain control of an engineering workstation for Initial Access into the control system environment. Access to an engineering workstation may occur through or physical means, such as a Valid Accounts with privileged access or infection by removable media. A dual-homed engineering workstation may allow the adversary access into multiple networks. For example, unsegregated process control, safety system, or information system networks. Deprecated by MITRE ATT&CK."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0818" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0108 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Engineering Workstation" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0818> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T0819
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Exploit Public-Facing Application"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may leverage weaknesses to exploit internet-facing software for initial access into an industrial network. Internet-facing software may be user applications, underlying networking implementations, an assets operating system, weak defenses, etc. Targets of this technique may be intentionally exposed for the purpose of remote management and visibility."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0819" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0108 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0819> ;
	.

attack:T0820
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Exploitation for Evasion"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may exploit a software vulnerability to take advantage of a programming error in a program, service, or within the operating system software or kernel itself to evade detection. Vulnerabilities may exist in software that can be used to disable or circumvent security features."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0820" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0103 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0820> ;
	.

attack:T0821
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Controller Tasking"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify the tasking of a controller to allow for the execution of their own programs. This can allow an adversary to manipulate the execution flow and behavior of a controller."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0821" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0104 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0821> ;
	.

attack:T0822
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "External Remote Services"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may leverage external remote services as a point of initial access into your network. These services allow users to connect to internal network resources from external locations. Examples are VPNs, Citrix, and other access mechanisms. Remote service gateways often manage connections and credential authentication for these services."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0822" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0108 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0822> ;
	.

attack:T0823
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Graphical User Interface"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to gain access to a machine via a Graphical User Interface (GUI) to enhance execution capabilities. Access to a GUI allows a user to interact with a computer in a more visual manner than a CLI. A GUI allows users to move a cursor and click on interface objects, with a mouse and keyboard as the main input devices, as opposed to just using the keyboard."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0823" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0104 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0823> ;
	.

attack:T0824
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "I/O Module Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use input/output (I/O) module discovery to gather key information about a control system device. An I/O module is a device that allows the control system device to either receive or send signals to other devices. These signals can be analog or digital, and may support a number of different protocols. Devices are often able to use attachable I/O modules to increase the number of inputs and outputs that it can utilize. Deprecated by MITRE ATT&CK."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0824" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0102 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Field Controller/RTU/PLC/IED" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0824> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T0825
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Location Identification"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may perform location identification using device data to inform operations and targeted impact for attacks. Location identification data can come in a number of forms, including geographic location, location relative to other control system devices, time zone, and current time. An adversary may use an embedded global positioning system (GPS) module in a device to figure out the physical coordinates of a device. Deprecated by MITRE ATT&CK."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0825" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0100 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Control Server" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0825> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T0826
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Loss of Availability"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to disrupt essential components or systems to prevent owner and operator from delivering products or services."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0826" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0105 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0826> ;
	.

attack:T0827
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Loss of Control"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may seek to achieve a sustained loss of control or a runaway condition in which operators cannot issue any commands even if the malicious interference has subsided."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0827" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0105 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0827> ;
	.

attack:T0828
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Loss of Productivity and Revenue"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may cause loss of productivity and revenue through disruption and even damage to the availability and integrity of control system operations, devices, and related processes. This technique may manifest as a direct effect of an ICS-targeting attack or tangentially, due to an IT-targeting attack against non-segregated environments."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0828" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0105 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0828> ;
	.

attack:T0829
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Loss of View"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may cause a sustained or permanent loss of view where the ICS equipment will require local, hands-on operator intervention; for instance, a restart or manual operation. By causing a sustained reporting or visibility loss, the adversary can effectively hide the present state of operations. This loss of view can occur without affecting the physical processes themselves."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0829" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0105 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0829> ;
	.

attack:T0830
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Adversary-in-the-Middle"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries with privileged network access may seek to modify network traffic in real time using adversary-in-the-middle (AiTM) attacks. This type of attack allows the adversary to intercept traffic to and/or from a particular device on the network. If a AiTM attack is established, then the adversary has the ability to block, log, modify, or inject traffic into the communication stream."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0830" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0100 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0830> ;
	.

attack:T0831
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Manipulation of Control"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may manipulate physical process control within the industrial environment. Methods of manipulating control can include changes to set point values, tags, or other parameters. Adversaries may manipulate control systems devices or possibly leverage their own, to communicate with and command physical control processes. The duration of manipulation may be temporary or longer sustained, depending on operator detection."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0831" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0105 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0831> ;
	.

attack:T0832
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Manipulation of View"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to manipulate the information reported back to operators or controllers. This manipulation may be short term or sustained. During this time the process itself could be in a much different state than what is reported."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0832" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0105 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0832> ;
	.

attack:T0833
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Control Logic"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may place malicious code in a system, which can cause the system to malfunction by modifying its control logic. Control system devices use programming languages (e.g. relay ladder logic) to control physical processes by affecting actuators, which cause machines to operate, based on environment sensor readings. These devices often include the ability to perform remote control logic updates. Deprecated by MITRE ATT&CK."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0833" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0106 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0107 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Field Controller/RTU/PLC/IED" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Safety Instrumented System/Protection Relay" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0833> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T0834
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Native API"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may directly interact with the native OS application programming interface (API) to access system functions. Native APIs provide a controlled means of calling low-level OS services within the kernel, such as those involving hardware/devices, memory, and processes. These native APIs are leveraged by the OS during system boot (when other system components are not yet initialized) as well as carrying out tasks and requests during routine operations."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0834" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0104 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0834> ;
	.

attack:T0835
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Manipulate I/O Image"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may manipulate the I/O image of PLCs through various means to prevent them from functioning as expected. Methods of I/O image manipulation may include overriding the I/O table via direct memory manipulation or using the override function used for testing PLC programs. During the scan cycle, a PLC reads the status of all inputs and stores them in an image table."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0835" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0107 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0835> ;
	.

attack:T0836
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Parameter"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify parameters used to instruct industrial control system devices. These devices operate via programs that dictate how and when to perform actions based on such parameters. Such parameters can determine the extent to which an action is performed and may specify additional options. For example, a program on a control system device dictating motor processes may take a parameter defining the total number of seconds to run that motor."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0836" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0106 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0836> ;
	.

attack:T0837
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Loss of Protection"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may compromise protective system functions designed to prevent the effects of faults and abnormal conditions. This can result in equipment damage, prolonged process disruptions and hazards to personnel."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0837" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0105 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0837> ;
	.

attack:T0838
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Alarm Settings"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify alarm settings to prevent alerts that may inform operators of their presence or to prevent responses to dangerous and unintended scenarios. Reporting messages are a standard part of data acquisition in control systems. Reporting messages are used as a way to transmit system state information and acknowledgements that specific actions have occurred."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0838" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0107 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0838> ;
	.

attack:T0839
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Module Firmware"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may install malicious or vulnerable firmware onto modular hardware devices. Control system devices often contain modular hardware devices. These devices may have their own set of firmware that is separate from the firmware of the main control system equipment. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1693.002."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0839" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0110 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0106 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1693.002 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-20"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0839> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T0840
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Network Connection Enumeration"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may perform network connection enumeration to discover information about device communication patterns. If an adversary can inspect the state of a network connection with tools, such as Netstat, in conjunction with System Firmware, then they can determine the role of certain devices on the network. The adversary can also use Network Sniffing to watch network traffic for details about the source, destination, protocol, and content."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0840" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0102 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0840> ;
	.

attack:T0841
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Network Service Scanning"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Network Service Scanning is the process of discovering services on networked systems. This can be achieved through a technique called port scanning or probing. Port scanning interacts with the TCP/IP ports on a target system to determine whether ports are open, closed, or filtered by a firewall. Deprecated by MITRE ATT&CK."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0841" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0102 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Field Controller/RTU/PLC/IED" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0841> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T0842
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Network Sniffing"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Network sniffing is the practice of using a network interface on a computer system to monitor or capture information regardless of whether it is the specified destination for the information."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0842" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0102 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0842> ;
	.

attack:T0843
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Program Download"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may perform a program download to transfer a user program to a controller."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0843" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0109 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0843> ;
	.

attack:T0843.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T0843 ;
	rdfs:label "Program Download: Download All"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may execute a full program download to a PLC to overwrite the entire PLC program and configuration to deploy a new project or make major changes. This typically requires stopping the PLC and adversely impacting control processes."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0843.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0109 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0843/001> ;
	.

attack:T0843.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T0843 ;
	rdfs:label "Program Download: Online Edit"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may execute an online edit of a PLC to update parts of an existing program. It does not require stopping the PLC which allows it to continue running during transfer and reconfiguration without interruption to process control. Adversaries may leverage this approach to minimize downtime and evade detection."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0843.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0109 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0843/002> ;
	.

attack:T0843.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T0843 ;
	rdfs:label "Program Download: Program Append"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may execute a program append to a PLC to update parts of an existing program. It may or may not require stopping the PLC which may allow it to continue running during transfer and reconfiguration without interruption to process control. Adversaries may leverage this approach to minimize downtime and evade detection."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0843.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0109 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0843/003> ;
	.

attack:T0844
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Program Organization Units"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Program Organizational Units (POUs) are block structures used within PLC programming to create programs and projects. POUs can be used to hold user programs written in IEC 61131-3 languages: Structured text, Instruction list, Function block, and Ladder logic. Application - 201203 They can also provide additional functionality, such as establishing connections between the PLC and other devices using TCON. Deprecated by MITRE ATT&CK."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0844" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0109 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0104 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Field Controller/RTU/PLC/IED" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Safety Instrumented System/Protection Relay" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0844> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T0845
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Program Upload"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to upload a program from a PLC to gather information about an industrial process. Uploading a program may allow them to acquire and study the underlying logic. Methods of program upload include vendor software, which enables the user to upload and read a program running on a PLC. This software can be used to upload the target program to a workstation, jump box, or an interfacing device."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0845" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0100 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-25"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0845> ;
	.

attack:T0846
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Remote System Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of other systems by IP address, hostname, or other logical identifier on a network that may be used for subsequent Lateral Movement or Discovery techniques. Functionality could exist within adversary tools to enable this, but utilities available on the operating system or vendor software could also be used."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0846" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0102 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0846> ;
	.

attack:T0846.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T0846 ;
	rdfs:label "Remote System Discovery: Port Scan"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may perform a port scan on a system, device, or network to identify live hosts, enumerate open ports and running services, identify operating systems, and map out the network. The results of a port scan may inform adversary Discovery, Lateral Movement, and vulnerability exploitation decisions (Exploitation for Evasion, Exploitation for Privilege Escalation, Exploitation of Remote Services)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0846.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0102 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0846/001> ;
	.

attack:T0846.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T0846 ;
	rdfs:label "Remote System Discovery: Broadcast Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may perform broadcast discovery requests to enumerate systems and devices on a network. Broadcast discovery works by one system or device sending messages to all systems and devices on a network (or subnet) and then waiting for a response. If a response is received that means the system or device that responded is live and can communicate over that protocol. Adversaries may leverage different protocols supported on the network for sending broadcast messages."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0846.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0102 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0846/002> ;
	.

attack:T0846.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T0846 ;
	rdfs:label "Remote System Discovery: Multicast Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may perform multicast discovery requests which is when one system or device sends messages to all systems and devices in a pre-defined group on a network (or subnet) and then waits for a response. If a response is received that means the system or device that responded is live and can communicate over that protocol. Multicast discovery tends to be stealthier than broadcast discovery because every system or device on the network (or subnet) is not being messaged."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0846.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0102 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0846/003> ;
	.

attack:T0847
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Replication Through Removable Media"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may move onto systems, such as those separated from the enterprise network, by copying malware to removable media which is inserted into the control systems environment. The adversary may rely on unknowing trusted third parties, such as suppliers or contractors with access privileges, to introduce the removable media. This technique enables initial access to target devices that never connect to untrusted networks, but are physically accessible."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0847" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0108 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0847> ;
	.

attack:T0848
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Rogue Master"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may setup a rogue master to leverage control server functions to communicate with outstations. A rogue master can be used to send legitimate control messages to other control system devices, affecting processes in unintended ways. It may also be used to disrupt network communications by capturing and receiving the network traffic meant for the actual master. Impersonating a master may also allow an adversary to avoid detection."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0848" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0108 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0848> ;
	.

attack:T0849
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Masquerading"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use masquerading to disguise a malicious application or executable as another file, to avoid operator and engineer suspicion. Possible disguises of these masquerading files can include commonly found programs, expected vendor executables and configuration files, and other commonplace application and naming conventions."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0849" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0103 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0849> ;
	.

attack:T0850
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Role Identification"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may perform role identification of devices involved with physical processes of interest in a target control system. Control systems devices often work in concert to control a physical process. Each device can have one or more roles that it performs within that control process. By collecting this role-based data, an adversary can construct a more targeted attack. Deprecated by MITRE ATT&CK."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0850" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0100 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Control Server" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Data Historian" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Field Controller/RTU/PLC/IED" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Human-Machine Interface" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0850> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T0851
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Rootkit"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may deploy rootkits to hide the presence of programs, files, network connections, services, drivers, and other system components. Rootkits are programs that hide the existence of malware by intercepting and modifying operating-system API calls that supply system information. Rootkits or rootkit-enabling functionality may reside at the user or kernel level in the operating system, or lower."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0851" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0103 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0107 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0851> ;
	.

attack:T0852
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Screen Capture"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to perform screen capture of devices in the control system environment. Screenshots may be taken of workstations, HMIs, or other devices that display environment-relevant process, device, reporting, alarm, or related data. These device displays may reveal information regarding the ICS process, layout, control, and related schematics. In particular, an HMI can provide a lot of important industrial process information."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0852" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0100 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0852> ;
	.

attack:T0853
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Scripting"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use scripting languages to execute arbitrary code in the form of a pre-written script or in the form of user-supplied code to an interpreter. Scripting languages are programming languages that differ from compiled languages, in that scripting languages use an interpreter, instead of a compiler. These interpreters read and compile part of the source code just before it is executed, as opposed to compilers, which compile each and every line of code to an executable file."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0853" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0104 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0853> ;
	.

attack:T0854
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Serial Connection Enumeration"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may perform serial connection enumeration to gather situational awareness after gaining access to devices in the OT network. Control systems devices often communicate to each other via various types of serial communication mediums. These serial communications are used to facilitate informational communication, as well as commands. Deprecated by MITRE ATT&CK."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0854" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0102 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Field Controller/RTU/PLC/IED" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Input/Output Server" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0854> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T0855
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Unauthorized Command Message"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may send unauthorized command messages to instruct control system assets to perform actions outside of their intended functionality, or without the logical preconditions to trigger their expected function. Command messages are used in ICS networks to give direct instructions to control systems devices. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1692.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0855" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0106 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1692.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-20"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0855> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T0856
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Spoof Reporting Message"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may spoof reporting messages in control system environments for evasion and to impair process control. In control systems, reporting messages contain telemetry data (e.g., I/O values) pertaining to the current state of equipment and the industrial process. Reporting messages are important for monitoring the normal operation of a system or identifying important events such as deviations from expected values. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1692.002."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0856" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0103 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0106 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1692.002 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-20"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0856> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T0857
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "System Firmware"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "System firmware on modern assets is often designed with an update feature. Older device firmware may be factory installed and require special reprograming equipment. When available, the firmware update feature enables vendors to remotely patch bugs and perform upgrades. Device firmware updates are often delegated to the user and may be done using a software update package. It may also be possible to perform this task over the network. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1693.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0857" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0110 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0107 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1693.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-20"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0857> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T0858
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Change Operating Mode"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may change the operating mode of a controller to gain additional access to engineering functions such as Program Download. Programmable controllers typically have several modes of operation that control the state of the user program and control access to the controllers API. Operating modes can be physically selected using a key switch on the face of the controller but may also be selected with calls to the controllers API."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0858" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0104 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0103 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0858> ;
	.

attack:T0859
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Valid Accounts"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may steal the credentials of a specific user or service account using credential access techniques. In some cases, default credentials for control system devices may be publicly available. Compromised credentials may be used to bypass access controls placed on various resources on hosts and within the network, and may even be used for persistent access to remote systems."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0859" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0110 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0109 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0859> ;
	.

attack:T0860
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Wireless Compromise"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may perform wireless compromise as a method of gaining communications and unauthorized access to a wireless network. Access to a wireless network may be gained through the compromise of a wireless device. Adversaries may also utilize radios and other wireless communication devices on the same frequency as the wireless network. Wireless compromise can be done as an initial access vector from a remote distance."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0860" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0108 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0860> ;
	.

attack:T0861
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Point & Tag Identification"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may collect point and tag values to gain a more comprehensive understanding of the process environment. Points may be values such as inputs, memory locations, outputs or other process specific variables. Tags are the identifiers given to points for operator convenience."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0861" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0100 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0861> ;
	.

attack:T0862
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Supply Chain Compromise"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may perform supply chain compromise to gain control systems environment access by means of infected products, software, and workflows. Supply chain compromise is the manipulation of products, such as devices or software, or their delivery mechanisms before receipt by the end consumer. Adversary compromise of these products and mechanisms is done for the goal of data or system compromise, once infected products are introduced to the target environment."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0862" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0108 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0862> ;
	.

attack:T0863
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "User Execution"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may rely on a targeted organizations user interaction for the execution of malicious code. User interaction may consist of installing applications, opening email attachments, or granting higher permissions to documents."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0863" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0104 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0863> ;
	.

attack:T0864
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Transient Cyber Asset"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may target devices that are transient across ICS networks and external networks. Normally, transient assets are brought into an environment by authorized personnel and do not remain in that environment on a permanent basis. Transient assets are commonly needed to support management functions and may be more common in systems where a remotely managed asset is not feasible, external connections for remote access do not exist, or 3rd party contractor/vendor access is required."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0864" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0108 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0864> ;
	.

attack:T0865
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Spearphishing Attachment"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use a spearphishing attachment, a variant of spearphishing, as a form of a social engineering attack against specific targets. Spearphishing attachments are different from other forms of spearphishing in that they employ malware attached to an email. All forms of spearphishing are electronically delivered and target a specific individual, company, or industry."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0865" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0108 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0865> ;
	.

attack:T0866
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Exploitation of Remote Services"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may exploit a software vulnerability to take advantage of a programming error in a program, service, or within the operating system software or kernel itself to enable remote service abuse. A common goal for post-compromise exploitation of remote services is for initial access into and lateral movement throughout the ICS environment to enable access to targeted systems."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0866" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0108 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0109 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0866> ;
	.

attack:T0867
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Lateral Tool Transfer"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may transfer tools or other files from one system to another to stage adversary tools or other files over the course of an operation. Copying of files may also be performed laterally between internal victim systems to support Lateral Movement with remote Execution using inherent file sharing protocols such as file sharing over SMB to connected network shares."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0867" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0109 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0867> ;
	.

attack:T0868
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Detect Operating Mode"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather information about a PLCs or controllers current operating mode. Operating modes dictate what change or maintenance functions can be manipulated and are often controlled by a key switch on the PLC (e.g., run, prog [program], and remote). Knowledge of these states may be valuable to an adversary to determine if they are able to reprogram the PLC. Operating modes and the mechanisms by which they are selected often vary by vendor and product line."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0868" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0100 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0868> ;
	.

attack:T0869
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Standard Application Layer Protocol"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may establish command and control capabilities over commonly used application layer protocols such as HTTP(S), OPC, RDP, telnet, DNP3, and modbus. These protocols may be used to disguise adversary actions as benign network traffic. Standard protocols may be seen on their associated port or in some cases over a non-standard port. Adversaries may use these protocols to reach out of the network for command and control, or in some cases to other infected devices within the network."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0869" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0101 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-25"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0869> ;
	.

attack:T0870
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Detect Program State"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may seek to gather information about the current state of a program on a PLC. State information reveals information about the program, including whether it's running, halted, stopped, or has generated an exception. This information may be leveraged as a verification of malicious program execution or to determine if a PLC is ready to download a new program. Deprecated by MITRE ATT&CK."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0870" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0100 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Field Controller/RTU/PLC/IED" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0870> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T0871
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Execution through API"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to leverage Application Program Interfaces (APIs) used for communication between control software and the hardware. Specific functionality is often coded into APIs which can be called by software to engage specific functions on a device or other software."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0871" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0104 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0871> ;
	.

attack:T0872
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Indicator Removal on Host"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to remove indicators of their presence on a system in an effort to cover their tracks. In cases where an adversary may feel detection is imminent, they may try to overwrite, delete, or cover up changes they have made to the device."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0872" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0103 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-25"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0872> ;
	.

attack:T0873
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Project File Infection"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to infect project files with malicious code. These project files may consist of objects, program organization units, variables such as tags, documentation, and other configurations needed for PLC programs to function. Using built in functions of the engineering software, adversaries may be able to download an infected program to a PLC in the operating environment enabling further Execution and Persistence techniques."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0873" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0110 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0873> ;
	.

attack:T0873.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T0873 ;
	rdfs:label "Project File Infection: Siemens Project File Format"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may infect Siemens PLC project files (i.e., Step 7, WinCC, etc.) to achieve Execution, Persistence, and Lateral Movement objectives. Adversaries may modify an existing project file or bring their own project files into the environment."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0873.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0110 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0873/001> ;
	.

attack:T0874
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Hooking"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may hook into application programming interface (API) functions used by processes to redirect calls for execution and privilege escalation means. Windows processes often leverage these API functions to perform tasks that require reusable system resources. Windows API functions are typically stored in dynamic-link libraries (DLLs) as exported functions."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0874" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0104 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0111 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0874> ;
	.

attack:T0875
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Change Program State"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to change the state of the current program on a control device. Program state changes may be used to allow for another program to take over control or be loaded onto the device. Deprecated by MITRE ATT&CK."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0875" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0104 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0106 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Field Controller/RTU/PLC/IED" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0875> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T0877
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "I/O Image"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may seek to capture process values related to the inputs and outputs of a PLC. During the scan cycle, a PLC reads the status of all inputs and stores them in an image table. The image table is the PLCs internal storage location where values of inputs/outputs for one scan are stored while it executes the user program. After the PLC has solved the entire logic program, it updates the output image table."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0877" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0100 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0877> ;
	.

attack:T0878
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Alarm Suppression"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may target protection function alarms to prevent them from notifying operators of critical conditions. Alarm messages may be a part of an overall reporting system and of particular interest for adversaries. Disruption of the alarm system does not imply the disruption of the reporting system as a whole."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0878" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0107 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0878> ;
	.

attack:T0879
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Damage to Property"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may cause damage and destruction of property to infrastructure, equipment, and the surrounding environment when attacking control systems. This technique may result in device and operational equipment breakdown, or represent tangential damage from other techniques used in an attack. Depending on the severity of physical damage and disruption caused to control processes and systems, this technique may result in Loss of Safety."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0879" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0105 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0879> ;
	.

attack:T0880
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Loss of Safety"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may compromise safety system functions designed to maintain safe operation of a process when unacceptable or dangerous conditions occur. Safety systems are often composed of the same elements as control systems but have the sole purpose of ensuring the process fails in a predetermined safe manner."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0880" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0105 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0880> ;
	.

attack:T0881
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Service Stop"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may stop or disable services on a system to render those services unavailable to legitimate users. Stopping critical services can inhibit or stop response to an incident or aid in the adversary's overall objectives to cause damage to the environment. Services may not allow for modification of their data stores while running. Adversaries may stop services in order to conduct Data Destruction."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0881" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0107 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0881> ;
	.

attack:T0882
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Theft of Operational Information"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may steal operational information on a production environment as a direct mission outcome for personal gain or to inform future operations. This information may include design documents, schedules, rotational data, or similar artifacts that provide insight on operations. In the Bowman Dam incident, adversaries probed systems for operational data."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0882" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0105 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0882> ;
	.

attack:T0883
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Internet Accessible Device"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gain access into industrial environments through systems exposed directly to the internet for remote access rather than through External Remote Services. Internet Accessible Devices are exposed to the internet unintentionally or intentionally without adequate protections. This may allow for adversaries to move directly into the control system network."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0883" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0108 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0883> ;
	.

attack:T0884
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Connection Proxy"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use a connection proxy to direct network traffic between systems or act as an intermediary for network communications."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0884" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0101 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0884> ;
	.

attack:T0885
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Commonly Used Port"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may communicate over a commonly used port to bypass firewalls or network detection systems and to blend in with normal network activity, to avoid more detailed inspection. They may use the protocol associated with the port, or a completely different protocol. They may use commonly open ports, such as the examples provided below."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0885" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0101 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0885> ;
	.

attack:T0886
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Remote Services"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may leverage remote services to move between assets and network segments. These services are often used to allow operators to interact with systems remotely within the network, some examples are RDP, SMB, SSH, and other similar mechanisms."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0886" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0108 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0109 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0886> ;
	.

attack:T0887
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Wireless Sniffing"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may seek to capture radio frequency (RF) communication used for remote control and reporting in distributed environments. RF communication frequencies vary between 3 kHz to 300 GHz, although are commonly between 300 MHz to 6 GHz. The wavelength and frequency of the signal affect how the signal propagates through open air, obstacles (e.g. walls and trees) and the type of radio required to capture them."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0887" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0102 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0100 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0887> ;
	.

attack:T0888
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Remote System Information Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may attempt to get detailed information about remote systems and their peripherals, such as make/model, role, and configuration. Adversaries may use information from Remote System Information Discovery to aid in targeting and shaping follow-on behaviors. For example, the system's operational role and model information can dictate whether it is a relevant target for the adversary's operational objectives."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0888" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0102 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0888> ;
	.

attack:T0889
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Program"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify or add a program on a controller to affect how it interacts with the physical process, peripheral devices and other hosts on the network. Modification to controller programs can be accomplished using a Program Download in addition to other types of program modification such as online edit and program append."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0889" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0110 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0889> ;
	.

attack:T0890
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Exploitation for Privilege Escalation"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may exploit software vulnerabilities in an attempt to elevate privileges. Exploitation of a software vulnerability occurs when an adversary takes advantage of a programming error in a program, service, or within the operating system software or kernel itself to execute adversary-controlled code."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0890" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0111 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0890> ;
	.

attack:T0891
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Hardcoded Credentials"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may leverage credentials that are hardcoded in software or firmware to gain an unauthorized interactive user session to an asset. Examples credentials that may be hardcoded in an asset include: Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1694.002."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0891" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0109 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0110 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1694.002 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-20"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0891> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T0892
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Change Credential"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify software and device credentials to prevent operator and responder access. Depending on the device, the modification or addition of this password could prevent any device configuration actions from being accomplished and may require a factory reset or replacement of hardware. These credentials are often built-in features provided by the device vendors as a means to restrict access to management interfaces."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0892" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0107 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0892> ;
	.

attack:T0893
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Data from Local System"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may target and collect data from local system sources, such as file systems, configuration files, or local databases. This can include sensitive data such as specifications, schematics, or diagrams of control system layouts, devices, and processes."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0893" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0100 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0893> ;
	.

attack:T0894
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "System Binary Proxy Execution"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may bypass process and/or signature-based defenses by proxying execution of malicious content with signed, or otherwise trusted, binaries. Binaries used in this technique are often Microsoft-signed files, indicating that they have been either downloaded from Microsoft or are already native in the operating system. Binaries signed with trusted digital certificates can typically execute on Windows systems protected by digital signature validation."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0894" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0103 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0894> ;
	.

attack:T0895
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Autorun Image"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may leverage AutoRun functionality or scripts to execute malicious code. Devices configured to enable AutoRun functionality or legacy operating systems may be susceptible to abuse of these features to run malicious code stored on various forms of removeable media (i.e., USB, Disk Images [.ISO]). Commonly, AutoRun or AutoPlay are disabled in many operating systems configurations to mitigate against this technique."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T0895" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0104 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0895> ;
	.

attack:T1001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Data Obfuscation"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may obfuscate command and control traffic to make it more difficult to detect. Command and control (C2) communications are hidden (but not necessarily encrypted) in an attempt to make the content more difficult to discover or decipher and to make the communication less conspicuous and hide commands from being seen. This encompasses many methods, such as adding junk data to protocol traffic, using steganography, or impersonating legitimate protocols."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1001> ;
	.

attack:T1001.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1001 ;
	rdfs:label "Data Obfuscation: Junk Data"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may add junk data to protocols used for command and control to make detection more difficult. By adding random or meaningless data to the protocols used for command and control, adversaries can prevent trivial methods for decoding, deciphering, or otherwise analyzing the traffic. Examples may include appending/prepending data with junk characters or writing junk characters between significant characters."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1001.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1001/001> ;
	.

attack:T1001.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1001 ;
	rdfs:label "Data Obfuscation: Steganography"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use steganographic techniques to hide command and control traffic to make detection efforts more difficult. Steganographic techniques can be used to hide data in digital messages that are transferred between systems. This hidden information can be used for command and control of compromised systems. In some cases, the passing of files embedded using steganography, such as image or document files, can be used for command and control."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1001.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1001/002> ;
	.

attack:T1001.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1001 ;
	rdfs:label "Data Obfuscation: Protocol or Service Impersonation"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may impersonate legitimate protocols or web service traffic to disguise command and control activity and thwart analysis efforts. By impersonating legitimate protocols or web services, adversaries can make their command and control traffic blend in with legitimate network traffic."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1001.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1001/003> ;
	.

attack:T1002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Data Compressed"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may compress data (e.g., sensitive documents) that is collected prior to exfiltration in order to make it portable and minimize the amount of data sent over the network. The compression is done separately from the exfiltration channel and is performed using a custom program or algorithm, or a more common compression library or utility such as 7zip, RAR, ZIP, or zlib. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1560."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0010 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1560 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1002> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "OS Credential Dumping"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to dump credentials to obtain account login and credential material, normally in the form of a hash or a clear text password. Credentials can be obtained from OS caches, memory, or structures. Credentials can then be used to perform Lateral Movement and access restricted information."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003> ;
	.

attack:T1003.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1003 ;
	rdfs:label "OS Credential Dumping: LSASS Memory"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to access credential material stored in the process memory of the Local Security Authority Subsystem Service (LSASS). After a user logs on, the system generates and stores a variety of credential materials in LSASS process memory. These credential materials can be harvested by an administrative user or SYSTEM and used to conduct Lateral Movement using Use Alternate Authentication Material."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1003.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003/001> ;
	.

attack:T1003.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1003 ;
	rdfs:label "OS Credential Dumping: Security Account Manager"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to extract credential material from the Security Account Manager (SAM) database either through in-memory techniques or through the Windows Registry where the SAM database is stored. The SAM is a database file that contains local accounts for the host, typically those found with the net user command. Enumerating the SAM database requires SYSTEM level access."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1003.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003/002> ;
	.

attack:T1003.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1003 ;
	rdfs:label "OS Credential Dumping: NTDS"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to access or create a copy of the Active Directory domain database in order to steal credential information, as well as obtain other information about domain members such as devices, users, and access rights. By default, the NTDS file (NTDS.dit) is located in %SystemRoot%\\NTDS\\Ntds.dit of a domain controller."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1003.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003/003> ;
	.

attack:T1003.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1003 ;
	rdfs:label "OS Credential Dumping: LSA Secrets"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries with SYSTEM access to a host may attempt to access Local Security Authority (LSA) secrets, which can contain a variety of different credential materials, such as credentials for service accounts. LSA secrets are stored in the registry at HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SECURITY\\Policy\\Secrets. LSA secrets can also be dumped from memory."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1003.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003/004> ;
	.

attack:T1003.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1003 ;
	rdfs:label "OS Credential Dumping: Cached Domain Credentials"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to access cached domain credentials used to allow authentication to occur in the event a domain controller is unavailable."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1003.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003/005> ;
	.

attack:T1003.006
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1003 ;
	rdfs:label "OS Credential Dumping: DCSync"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to access credentials and other sensitive information by abusing a Windows Domain Controller's application programming interface (API) to simulate the replication process from a remote domain controller using a technique called DCSync."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1003.006" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003/006> ;
	.

attack:T1003.007
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1003 ;
	rdfs:label "OS Credential Dumping: Proc Filesystem"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather credentials from the proc filesystem or `/proc`. The proc filesystem is a pseudo-filesystem used as an interface to kernel data structures for Linux based systems managing virtual memory. For each process, the `/proc//maps` file shows how memory is mapped within the process’s virtual address space. And `/proc//mem`, exposed for debugging purposes, provides access to the process’s virtual address space."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1003.007" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003/007> ;
	.

attack:T1003.008
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1003 ;
	rdfs:label "OS Credential Dumping: /etc/passwd and /etc/shadow"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to dump the contents of /etc/passwd and /etc/shadow to enable offline password cracking. Most modern Linux operating systems use a combination of /etc/passwd and /etc/shadow to store user account information, including password hashes in /etc/shadow. By default, /etc/shadow is only readable by the root user."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1003.008" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003/008> ;
	.

attack:T1004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Winlogon Helper DLL"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Winlogon.exe is a Windows component responsible for actions at logon/logoff as well as the secure attention sequence (SAS) triggered by Ctrl-Alt-Delete. Registry entries in HKLM\\Software\\[Wow6432Node\\]Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Winlogon\\ and HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Winlogon\\ are used to manage additional helper programs and functionalities that support Winlogon. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1547.004."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1547.004 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1004> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Data from Local System"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may search local system sources, such as file systems, configuration files, local databases, virtual machine files, or process memory, to find files of interest and sensitive data prior to Exfiltration."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0009 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1005> ;
	.

attack:T1006
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Direct Volume Access"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may directly access a volume to bypass file access controls and file system monitoring. Windows allows programs to have direct access to logical volumes. Programs with direct access may read and write files directly from the drive by analyzing file system data structures. This technique may bypass Windows file access controls as well as file system monitoring tools."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1006" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1006> ;
	.

attack:T1007
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "System Service Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may try to gather information about registered local system services. Adversaries may obtain information about services using tools as well as OS utility commands such as sc query, tasklist /svc, systemctl --type=service, and net start. Adversaries may also gather information about schedule tasks via commands such as `schtasks` on Windows or `crontab -l` on Linux and macOS."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1007" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1007> ;
	.

attack:T1008
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Fallback Channels"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use fallback or alternate communication channels if the primary channel is compromised or inaccessible in order to maintain reliable command and control and to avoid data transfer thresholds."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1008" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1008> ;
	.

attack:T1009
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Binary Padding"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries can use binary padding to add junk data and change the on-disk representation of malware without affecting the functionality or behavior of the binary. This will often increase the size of the binary beyond what some security tools are capable of handling due to file size limitations. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1027.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1009" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1027.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1009> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1010
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Application Window Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of open application windows. Window listings could convey information about how the system is used. For example, information about application windows could be used identify potential data to collect as well as identifying security tooling (Security Software Discovery) to evade."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1010" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1010> ;
	.

attack:T1011
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Exfiltration Over Other Network Medium"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to exfiltrate data over a different network medium than the command and control channel. If the command and control network is a wired Internet connection, the exfiltration may occur, for example, over a WiFi connection, modem, cellular data connection, Bluetooth, or another radio frequency (RF) channel."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1011" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0010 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1011> ;
	.

attack:T1011.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1011 ;
	rdfs:label "Exfiltration Over Other Network Medium: Exfiltration Over Bluetooth"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to exfiltrate data over Bluetooth rather than the command and control channel. If the command and control network is a wired Internet connection, an adversary may opt to exfiltrate data using a Bluetooth communication channel."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1011.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0010 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1011/001> ;
	.

attack:T1012
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Query Registry"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may interact with the Windows Registry to gather information about the system, configuration, and installed software."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1012" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1012> ;
	.

attack:T1013
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Port Monitors"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "A port monitor can be set through the API call to set a DLL to be loaded at startup. This DLL can be located in C:\\Windows\\System32 and will be loaded by the print spooler service, spoolsv.exe, on boot. The spoolsv.exe process also runs under SYSTEM level permissions. Alternatively, an arbitrary DLL can be loaded if permissions allow writing a fully-qualified pathname for that DLL to HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Print\\Monitors. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1547.010."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1013" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1547.010 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1013> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1014
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Rootkit"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use rootkits to hide the presence of programs, files, network connections, services, drivers, and other system components. Rootkits are programs that hide the existence of malware by intercepting/hooking and modifying operating system API calls that supply system information."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1014" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1014> ;
	.

attack:T1015
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Accessibility Features"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Windows contains accessibility features that may be launched with a key combination before a user has logged in (for example, when the user is on the Windows logon screen). An adversary can modify the way these programs are launched to get a command prompt or backdoor without logging in to the system. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1546.008."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1015" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1546.008 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1015> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1016
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "System Network Configuration Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may look for details about the network configuration and settings, such as IP and/or MAC addresses, of systems they access or through information discovery of remote systems. Several operating system administration utilities exist that can be used to gather this information. Examples include Arp, ipconfig/ifconfig, nbtstat, and route."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1016" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1016> ;
	.

attack:T1016.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1016 ;
	rdfs:label "System Network Configuration Discovery: Internet Connection Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may check for Internet connectivity on compromised systems. This may be performed during automated discovery and can be accomplished in numerous ways such as using Ping, tracert, and GET requests to websites, or performing initial speed testing to confirm bandwidth."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1016.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1016/001> ;
	.

attack:T1016.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1016 ;
	rdfs:label "System Network Configuration Discovery: Wi-Fi Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may search for information about Wi-Fi networks, such as network names and passwords, on compromised systems. Adversaries may use Wi-Fi information as part of Account Discovery, Remote System Discovery, and other discovery or Credential Access activity to support both ongoing and future campaigns."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1016.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1016/002> ;
	.

attack:T1017
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Application Deployment Software"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may deploy malicious software to systems within a network using application deployment systems employed by enterprise administrators. The permissions required for this action vary by system configuration; local credentials may be sufficient with direct access to the deployment server, or specific domain credentials may be required. However, the system may require an administrative account to log in or to perform software deployment. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1072."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1017" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0008 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1072 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1017> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1018
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Remote System Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of other systems by IP address, hostname, or other logical identifier on a network that may be used for Lateral Movement from the current system. Functionality could exist within remote access tools to enable this, but utilities available on the operating system could also be used such as Ping, net view using Net, or, on ESXi servers, `esxcli network diag ping`."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1018" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1018> ;
	.

attack:T1019
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "System Firmware"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "The BIOS (Basic Input/Output System) and The Unified Extensible Firmware Interface (UEFI) or Extensible Firmware Interface (EFI) are examples of system firmware that operate as the software interface between the operating system and hardware of a computer. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1542.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1019" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1542.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1019> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1020
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Automated Exfiltration"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may exfiltrate data, such as sensitive documents, through the use of automated processing after being gathered during Collection."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1020" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0010 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1020> ;
	.

attack:T1020.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1020 ;
	rdfs:label "Automated Exfiltration: Traffic Duplication"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may leverage traffic mirroring in order to automate data exfiltration over compromised infrastructure. Traffic mirroring is a native feature for some devices, often used for network analysis. For example, devices may be configured to forward network traffic to one or more destinations for analysis by a network analyzer or other monitoring device."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1020.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0010 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1020/001> ;
	.

attack:T1021
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Remote Services"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use Valid Accounts to log into a service that accepts remote connections, such as telnet, SSH, and VNC. The adversary may then perform actions as the logged-on user."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1021" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0008 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021> ;
	.

attack:T1021.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1021 ;
	rdfs:label "Remote Services: Remote Desktop Protocol"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use Valid Accounts to log into a computer using the Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP). The adversary may then perform actions as the logged-on user."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1021.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0008 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/001> ;
	.

attack:T1021.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1021 ;
	rdfs:label "Remote Services: SMB/Windows Admin Shares"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use Valid Accounts to interact with a remote network share using Server Message Block (SMB). The adversary may then perform actions as the logged-on user."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1021.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0008 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/002> ;
	.

attack:T1021.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1021 ;
	rdfs:label "Remote Services: Distributed Component Object Model"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use Valid Accounts to interact with remote machines by taking advantage of Distributed Component Object Model (DCOM). The adversary may then perform actions as the logged-on user."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1021.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0008 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/003> ;
	.

attack:T1021.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1021 ;
	rdfs:label "Remote Services: SSH"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use Valid Accounts to log into remote machines using Secure Shell (SSH). The adversary may then perform actions as the logged-on user."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1021.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0008 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/004> ;
	.

attack:T1021.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1021 ;
	rdfs:label "Remote Services: VNC"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use Valid Accounts to remotely control machines using Virtual Network Computing (VNC). VNC is a platform-independent desktop sharing system that uses the RFB (“remote framebuffer”) protocol to enable users to remotely control another computer’s display by relaying the screen, mouse, and keyboard inputs over the network."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1021.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0008 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/005> ;
	.

attack:T1021.006
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1021 ;
	rdfs:label "Remote Services: Windows Remote Management"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use Valid Accounts to interact with remote systems using Windows Remote Management (WinRM). The adversary may then perform actions as the logged-on user."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1021.006" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0008 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/006> ;
	.

attack:T1021.007
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1021 ;
	rdfs:label "Remote Services: Cloud Services"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may log into accessible cloud services within a compromised environment using Valid Accounts that are synchronized with or federated to on-premises user identities. The adversary may then perform management actions or access cloud-hosted resources as the logged-on user."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1021.007" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0008 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/007> ;
	.

attack:T1021.008
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1021 ;
	rdfs:label "Remote Services: Direct Cloud VM Connections"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may leverage Valid Accounts to log directly into accessible cloud hosted compute infrastructure through cloud native methods. Many cloud providers offer interactive connections to virtual infrastructure that can be accessed through the Cloud API, such as Azure Serial Console, AWS EC2 Instance Connect, and AWS System Manager.."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1021.008" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0008 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/008> ;
	.

attack:T1022
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Data Encrypted"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Data is encrypted before being exfiltrated in order to hide the information that is being exfiltrated from detection or to make the exfiltration less conspicuous upon inspection by a defender. The encryption is performed by a utility, programming library, or custom algorithm on the data itself and is considered separate from any encryption performed by the command and control or file transfer protocol. Common file archive formats that can encrypt files are RAR and zip. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1560."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1022" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0010 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1560 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1022> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1023
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Shortcut Modification"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Shortcuts or symbolic links are ways of referencing other files or programs that will be opened or executed when the shortcut is clicked or executed by a system startup process. Adversaries could use shortcuts to execute their tools for persistence. They may create a new shortcut as a means of indirection that may use Masquerading to look like a legitimate program. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1547.009."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1023" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1547.009 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1023> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1024
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Custom Cryptographic Protocol"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use a custom cryptographic protocol or algorithm to hide command and control traffic. A simple scheme, such as XOR-ing the plaintext with a fixed key, will produce a very weak ciphertext. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1573."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1024" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1573 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1024> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1025
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Data from Removable Media"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may search connected removable media on computers they have compromised to find files of interest. Sensitive data can be collected from any removable media (optical disk drive, USB memory, etc.) connected to the compromised system prior to Exfiltration. Interactive command shells may be in use, and common functionality within cmd may be used to gather information."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1025" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0009 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1025> ;
	.

attack:T1026
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Multiband Communication"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "**This technique has been deprecated and should no longer be used.** Deprecated by MITRE ATT&CK."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1026" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1026> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1027
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Obfuscated Files or Information"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to make an executable or file difficult to discover or analyze by encrypting, encoding, or otherwise obfuscating its contents on the system or in transit. This is common behavior that can be used across different platforms and the network to evade defenses."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1027" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1027> ;
	.

attack:T1027.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1027 ;
	rdfs:label "Obfuscated Files or Information: Binary Padding"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use binary padding to add junk data and change the on-disk representation of malware. This can be done without affecting the functionality or behavior of a binary, but can increase the size of the binary beyond what some security tools are capable of handling due to file size limitations."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1027.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1027/001> ;
	.

attack:T1027.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1027 ;
	rdfs:label "Obfuscated Files or Information: Software Packing"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may perform software packing or virtual machine software protection to conceal their code. Software packing is a method of compressing or encrypting an executable. Packing an executable changes the file signature in an attempt to avoid signature-based detection. Most decompression techniques decompress the executable code in memory. Virtual machine software protection translates an executable's original code into a special format that only a special virtual machine can run."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1027.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1027/002> ;
	.

attack:T1027.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1027 ;
	rdfs:label "Obfuscated Files or Information: Steganography"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use steganography techniques in order to prevent the detection of hidden information. Steganographic techniques can be used to hide data in digital media such as images, audio tracks, video clips, or text files."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1027.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1027/003> ;
	.

attack:T1027.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1027 ;
	rdfs:label "Obfuscated Files or Information: Compile After Delivery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to make payloads difficult to discover and analyze by delivering files to victims as uncompiled code. Text-based source code files may subvert analysis and scrutiny from protections targeting executables/binaries. These payloads will need to be compiled before execution; typically via native utilities such as ilasm.exe, csc.exe, or GCC/MinGW."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1027.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1027/004> ;
	.

attack:T1027.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1027 ;
	rdfs:label "Obfuscated Files or Information: Indicator Removal from Tools"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may remove indicators from tools if they believe their malicious tool was detected, quarantined, or otherwise curtailed. They can modify the tool by removing the indicator and using the updated version that is no longer detected by the target's defensive systems or subsequent targets that may use similar systems."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1027.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1027/005> ;
	.

attack:T1027.006
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1027 ;
	rdfs:label "Obfuscated Files or Information: HTML Smuggling"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may smuggle data and files past content filters by hiding malicious payloads inside of seemingly benign HTML files. HTML documents can store large binary objects known as JavaScript Blobs (immutable data that represents raw bytes) that can later be constructed into file-like objects. Data may also be stored in Data URLs, which enable embedding media type or MIME files inline of HTML documents. HTML5 also introduced a download attribute that may be used to initiate file downloads."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1027.006" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1027/006> ;
	.

attack:T1027.007
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1027 ;
	rdfs:label "Obfuscated Files or Information: Dynamic API Resolution"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may obfuscate then dynamically resolve API functions called by their malware in order to conceal malicious functionalities and impair defensive analysis. Malware commonly uses various Native API functions provided by the OS to perform various tasks such as those involving processes, files, and other system artifacts."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1027.007" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1027/007> ;
	.

attack:T1027.008
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1027 ;
	rdfs:label "Obfuscated Files or Information: Stripped Payloads"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to make a payload difficult to analyze by removing symbols, strings, and other human readable information. Scripts and executables may contain variables names and other strings that help developers document code functionality. Symbols are often created by an operating system’s `linker` when executable payloads are compiled. Reverse engineers use these symbols and strings to analyze code and to identify functionality in payloads."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1027.008" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1027/008> ;
	.

attack:T1027.009
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1027 ;
	rdfs:label "Obfuscated Files or Information: Embedded Payloads"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may embed payloads within other files to conceal malicious content from defenses. Otherwise seemingly benign files (such as scripts and executables) may be abused to carry and obfuscate malicious payloads and content. In some cases, embedded payloads may also enable adversaries to Subvert Trust Controls by not impacting execution controls such as digital signatures and notarization tickets."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1027.009" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1027/009> ;
	.

attack:T1027.010
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1027 ;
	rdfs:label "Obfuscated Files or Information: Command Obfuscation"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may obfuscate content during command execution to impede detection. Command-line obfuscation is a method of making strings and patterns within commands and scripts more difficult to signature and analyze. This type of obfuscation can be included within commands executed by delivered payloads (e.g., Phishing and Drive-by Compromise) or interactively via Command and Scripting Interpreter."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1027.010" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1027/010> ;
	.

attack:T1027.011
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1027 ;
	rdfs:label "Obfuscated Files or Information: Fileless Storage"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may store data in \"fileless\" formats to conceal malicious activity from defenses. Fileless storage can be broadly defined as any format other than a file. Common examples of non-volatile fileless storage in Windows systems include the Windows Registry, event logs, or WMI repository."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1027.011" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1027/011> ;
	.

attack:T1027.012
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1027 ;
	rdfs:label "Obfuscated Files or Information: LNK Icon Smuggling"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may smuggle commands to download malicious payloads past content filters by hiding them within otherwise seemingly benign windows shortcut files. Windows shortcut files (.LNK) include many metadata fields, including an icon location field (also known as the `IconEnvironmentDataBlock`) designed to specify the path to an icon file that is to be displayed for the LNK file within a host directory."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1027.012" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1027/012> ;
	.

attack:T1027.013
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1027 ;
	rdfs:label "Obfuscated Files or Information: Encrypted/Encoded File"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may encrypt or encode files to obfuscate strings, bytes, and other specific patterns to impede detection. Encrypting and/or encoding file content aims to conceal malicious artifacts within a file used in an intrusion. Many other techniques, such as Software Packing, Steganography, and Embedded Payloads, share this same broad objective."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1027.013" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1027/013> ;
	.

attack:T1027.014
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1027 ;
	rdfs:label "Obfuscated Files or Information: Polymorphic Code"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may utilize polymorphic code (also known as metamorphic or mutating code) to evade detection. Polymorphic code is a type of software capable of changing its runtime footprint during code execution. With each execution of the software, the code is mutated into a different version of itself that achieves the same purpose or objective as the original. This functionality enables the malware to evade traditional signature-based defenses, such as antivirus and antimalware tools."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1027.014" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1027/014> ;
	.

attack:T1027.015
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1027 ;
	rdfs:label "Obfuscated Files or Information: Compression"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use compression to obfuscate their payloads or files. Compressed file formats such as ZIP, gzip, 7z, and RAR can compress and archive multiple files together to make it easier and faster to transfer files. In addition to compressing files, adversaries may also compress shellcode directly - for example, in order to store it in a Windows Registry key (i.e., Fileless Storage)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1027.015" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1027/015> ;
	.

attack:T1027.016
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1027 ;
	rdfs:label "Obfuscated Files or Information: Junk Code Insertion"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use junk code / dead code to obfuscate a malware’s functionality. Junk code is code that either does not execute, or if it does execute, does not change the functionality of the code. Junk code makes analysis more difficult and time-consuming, as the analyst steps through non-functional code instead of analyzing the main code."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1027.016" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1027/016> ;
	.

attack:T1027.017
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1027 ;
	rdfs:label "Obfuscated Files or Information: SVG Smuggling"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may smuggle data and files past content filters by hiding malicious payloads inside of seemingly benign SVG files. SVGs, or Scalable Vector Graphics, are vector-based image files constructed using XML. As such, they can legitimately include `` tags that enable adversaries to include malicious JavaScript payloads. However, SVGs may appear less suspicious to users than other types of executable files, as they are often treated as image files."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1027.017" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1027/017> ;
	.

attack:T1027.018
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1027 ;
	rdfs:label "Obfuscated Files or Information: Invisible Unicode"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse invisible or non-printing Unicode characters to conceal malicious content within files, scripts, or text. By inserting characters that do not visibly render, adversaries may hide data, alter how content is interpreted, or make malicious code appear as benign text or whitespace."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1027.018" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1027/018> ;
	.

attack:T1028
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Windows Remote Management"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Windows Remote Management (WinRM) is the name of both a Windows service and a protocol that allows a user to interact with a remote system (e.g., run an executable, modify the Registry, modify services). It may be called with the winrm command or by any number of programs such as PowerShell. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1021.006."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1028" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0008 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1021.006 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1028> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1029
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Scheduled Transfer"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may schedule data exfiltration to be performed only at certain times of day or at certain intervals. This could be done to blend traffic patterns with normal activity or availability."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1029" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0010 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1029> ;
	.

attack:T1030
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Data Transfer Size Limits"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may exfiltrate data in fixed size chunks instead of whole files or limit packet sizes below certain thresholds. This approach may be used to avoid triggering network data transfer threshold alerts."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1030" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0010 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1030> ;
	.

attack:T1031
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Existing Service"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Windows service configuration information, including the file path to the service's executable or recovery programs/commands, is stored in the Registry. Service configurations can be modified using utilities such as sc.exe and Reg. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1543.003."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1031" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1543.003 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1031> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1032
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Standard Cryptographic Protocol"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may explicitly employ a known encryption algorithm to conceal command and control traffic rather than relying on any inherent protections provided by a communication protocol. Despite the use of a secure algorithm, these implementations may be vulnerable to reverse engineering if necessary secret keys are encoded and/or generated within malware samples/configuration files. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1573."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1032" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1573 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1032> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1033
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "System Owner/User Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to identify the primary user, currently logged in user, set of users that commonly uses a system, or whether a user is actively using the system. They may do this, for example, by retrieving account usernames or by using OS Credential Dumping."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1033" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1033> ;
	.

attack:T1034
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Path Interception"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "**This technique has been deprecated. Please use Path Interception by PATH Environment Variable, Path Interception by Search Order Hijacking, and/or Path Interception by Unquoted Path.** Deprecated by MITRE ATT&CK."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1034" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1034> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1035
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Service Execution"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may execute a binary, command, or script via a method that interacts with Windows services, such as the Service Control Manager. This can be done by either creating a new service or modifying an existing service. This technique is the execution used in conjunction with New Service and Modify Existing Service during service persistence or privilege escalation. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1569.002."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1035" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1569.002 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1035> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1036
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Masquerading"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to manipulate features of their artifacts to make them appear legitimate or benign to users and/or security tools. Masquerading occurs when the name or location of an object, legitimate or malicious, is manipulated or abused for the sake of evading defenses and observation. This may include manipulating file metadata, tricking users into misidentifying the file type, and giving legitimate task or service names."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1036" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1036> ;
	.

attack:T1036.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1036 ;
	rdfs:label "Masquerading: Invalid Code Signature"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to mimic features of valid code signatures to increase the chance of deceiving a user, analyst, or tool. Code signing provides a level of authenticity on a binary from the developer and a guarantee that the binary has not been tampered with. Adversaries can copy the metadata and signature information from a signed program, then use it as a template for an unsigned program."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1036.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1036/001> ;
	.

attack:T1036.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1036 ;
	rdfs:label "Masquerading: Right-to-Left Override"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse the right-to-left override (RTLO or RLO) character (U+202E) to disguise a string and/or file name to make it appear benign. RTLO is a non-printing Unicode character that causes the text that follows it to be displayed in reverse. For example, a Windows screensaver executable named March 25 \\u202Excod.scr will display as March 25 rcs.docx. A JavaScript file named photo_high_re\\u202Egnp.js will be displayed as photo_high_resj.png."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1036.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1036/002> ;
	.

attack:T1036.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1036 ;
	rdfs:label "Masquerading: Rename Legitimate Utilities"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may rename legitimate / system utilities to try to evade security mechanisms concerning the usage of those utilities. Security monitoring and control mechanisms may be in place for legitimate utilities adversaries are capable of abusing, including both built-in binaries and tools such as PSExec, AutoHotKey, and IronPython. It may be possible to bypass those security mechanisms by renaming the utility prior to utilization (ex: rename rundll32.exe)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1036.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1036/003> ;
	.

attack:T1036.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1036 ;
	rdfs:label "Masquerading: Masquerade Task or Service"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to manipulate the name of a task or service to make it appear legitimate or benign. Tasks/services executed by the Task Scheduler or systemd will typically be given a name and/or description. Windows services will have a service name as well as a display name. Many benign tasks and services exist that have commonly associated names. Adversaries may give tasks or services names that are similar or identical to those of legitimate ones."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1036.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1036/004> ;
	.

attack:T1036.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1036 ;
	rdfs:label "Masquerading: Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may match or approximate the name or location of legitimate files, Registry keys, or other resources when naming/placing them. This is done for the sake of evading defenses and observation."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1036.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1036/005> ;
	.

attack:T1036.006
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1036 ;
	rdfs:label "Masquerading: Space after Filename"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries can hide a program's true filetype by changing the extension of a file. With certain file types (specifically this does not work with .app extensions), appending a space to the end of a filename will change how the file is processed by the operating system."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1036.006" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1036/006> ;
	.

attack:T1036.007
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1036 ;
	rdfs:label "Masquerading: Double File Extension"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse a double extension in the filename as a means of masquerading the true file type. A file name may include a secondary file type extension that may cause only the first extension to be displayed (ex: File.txt.exe may render in some views as just File.txt). However, the second extension is the true file type that determines how the file is opened and executed."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1036.007" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1036/007> ;
	.

attack:T1036.008
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1036 ;
	rdfs:label "Masquerading: Masquerade File Type"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may masquerade malicious payloads as legitimate files through changes to the payload's formatting, including the file’s signature, extension, icon, and contents. Various file types have a typical standard format, including how they are encoded and organized. For example, a file’s signature (also known as header or magic bytes) is the beginning bytes of a file and is often used to identify the file’s type."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1036.008" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1036/008> ;
	.

attack:T1036.009
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1036 ;
	rdfs:label "Masquerading: Break Process Trees"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may attempt to evade process tree-based analysis by modifying executed malware's parent process ID (PPID). If endpoint protection software leverages the “parent-child\" relationship for detection, breaking this relationship could result in the adversary’s behavior not being associated with previous process tree activity. On Unix-based systems breaking this process tree is common practice for administrators to execute software using scripts and programs."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1036.009" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1036/009> ;
	.

attack:T1036.010
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1036 ;
	rdfs:label "Masquerading: Masquerade Account Name"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may match or approximate the names of legitimate accounts to make newly created ones appear benign. This will typically occur during Create Account, although accounts may also be renamed at a later date. This may also coincide with Account Access Removal if the actor first deletes an account before re-creating one with the same name."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1036.010" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1036/010> ;
	.

attack:T1036.011
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1036 ;
	rdfs:label "Masquerading: Overwrite Process Arguments"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify a process's in-memory arguments to change its name in order to appear as a legitimate or benign process. On Linux, the operating system stores command-line arguments in the process’s stack and passes them to the `main()` function as the `argv` array. The first element, `argv[0]`, typically contains the process name or path - by default, the command used to actually start the process (e.g., `cat /etc/passwd`)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1036.011" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1036/011> ;
	.

attack:T1036.012
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1036 ;
	rdfs:label "Masquerading: Browser Fingerprint"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to blend in with legitimate traffic by spoofing browser and system attributes like operating system, system language, platform, user-agent string, resolution, time zone, etc. The HTTP User-Agent request header is a string that lets servers and network peers identify the application, operating system, vendor, and/or version of the requesting user agent."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1036.012" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1036/012> ;
	.

attack:T1037
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Boot or Logon Initialization Scripts"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use scripts automatically executed at boot or logon initialization to establish persistence. Initialization scripts can be used to perform administrative functions, which may often execute other programs or send information to an internal logging server. These scripts can vary based on operating system and whether applied locally or remotely."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1037" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1037> ;
	.

attack:T1037.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1037 ;
	rdfs:label "Boot or Logon Initialization Scripts: Logon Script (Windows)"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use Windows logon scripts automatically executed at logon initialization to establish persistence. Windows allows logon scripts to be run whenever a specific user or group of users log into a system. This is done via adding a path to a script to the HKCU\\Environment\\UserInitMprLogonScript Registry key."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1037.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1037/001> ;
	.

attack:T1037.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1037 ;
	rdfs:label "Boot or Logon Initialization Scripts: Login Hook"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use a Login Hook to establish persistence executed upon user logon. A login hook is a plist file that points to a specific script to execute with root privileges upon user logon. The plist file is located in the /Library/Preferences/com.apple.loginwindow.plist file and can be modified using the defaults command-line utility. This behavior is the same for logout hooks where a script can be executed upon user logout."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1037.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1037/002> ;
	.

attack:T1037.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1037 ;
	rdfs:label "Boot or Logon Initialization Scripts: Network Logon Script"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use network logon scripts automatically executed at logon initialization to establish persistence. Network logon scripts can be assigned using Active Directory or Group Policy Objects. These logon scripts run with the privileges of the user they are assigned to. Depending on the systems within the network, initializing one of these scripts could apply to more than one or potentially all systems."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1037.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1037/003> ;
	.

attack:T1037.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1037 ;
	rdfs:label "Boot or Logon Initialization Scripts: RC Scripts"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may establish persistence by modifying RC scripts, which are executed during a Unix-like system’s startup. These files allow system administrators to map and start custom services at startup for different run levels. RC scripts require root privileges to modify."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1037.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1037/004> ;
	.

attack:T1037.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1037 ;
	rdfs:label "Boot or Logon Initialization Scripts: Startup Items"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use startup items automatically executed at boot initialization to establish persistence. Startup items execute during the final phase of the boot process and contain shell scripts or other executable files along with configuration information used by the system to determine the execution order for all startup items."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1037.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1037/005> ;
	.

attack:T1038
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "DLL Search Order Hijacking"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Windows systems use a common method to look for required DLLs to load into a program. Adversaries may take advantage of the Windows DLL search order and programs that ambiguously specify DLLs to gain privilege escalation and persistence. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1574.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1038" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1574.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1038> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1039
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Data from Network Shared Drive"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may search network shares on computers they have compromised to find files of interest. Sensitive data can be collected from remote systems via shared network drives (host shared directory, network file server, etc.) that are accessible from the current system prior to Exfiltration. Interactive command shells may be in use, and common functionality within cmd may be used to gather information."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1039" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0009 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1039> ;
	.

attack:T1040
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Network Sniffing"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may passively sniff network traffic to capture information about an environment, including authentication material passed over the network. Network sniffing refers to using the network interface on a system to monitor or capture information sent over a wired or wireless connection. An adversary may place a network interface into promiscuous mode to passively access data in transit over the network, or use span ports to capture a larger amount of data."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1040" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1040> ;
	.

attack:T1041
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Exfiltration Over C2 Channel"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may steal data by exfiltrating it over an existing command and control channel. Stolen data is encoded into the normal communications channel using the same protocol as command and control communications."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1041" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0010 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1041> ;
	.

attack:T1042
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Change Default File Association"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "When a file is opened, the default program used to open the file (also called the file association or handler) is checked. File association selections are stored in the Windows Registry and can be edited by users, administrators, or programs that have Registry access or by administrators using the built-in assoc utility. Applications can modify the file association for a given file extension to call an arbitrary program when a file with the given extension is opened. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1546.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1042" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1546.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1042> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1043
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Commonly Used Port"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "**This technique has been deprecated. Please use Non-Standard Port where appropriate.** Deprecated by MITRE ATT&CK."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1043" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1043> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1044
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "File System Permissions Weakness"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Processes may automatically execute specific binaries as part of their functionality or to perform other actions. If the permissions on the file system directory containing a target binary, or permissions on the binary itself, are improperly set, then the target binary may be overwritten with another binary using user-level permissions and executed by the original process. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1574.010."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1044" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1574.010 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1044> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1045
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Software Packing"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Software packing is a method of compressing or encrypting an executable. Packing an executable changes the file signature in an attempt to avoid signature-based detection. Most decompression techniques decompress the executable code in memory. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1027.002."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1045" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1027.002 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1045> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1046
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Network Service Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of services running on remote hosts and local network infrastructure devices, including those that may be vulnerable to remote software exploitation. Common methods to acquire this information include port, vulnerability, and/or wordlist scans using tools that are brought onto a system."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1046" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1046> ;
	.

attack:T1047
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Windows Management Instrumentation"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse Windows Management Instrumentation (WMI) to execute malicious commands and payloads. WMI is designed for programmers and is the infrastructure for management data and operations on Windows systems. WMI is an administration feature that provides a uniform environment to access Windows system components."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1047" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1047> ;
	.

attack:T1048
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Exfiltration Over Alternative Protocol"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may steal data by exfiltrating it over a different protocol than that of the existing command and control channel. The data may also be sent to an alternate network location from the main command and control server."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1048" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0010 ;
