attack:T1052
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Exfiltration Over Physical Medium"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to exfiltrate data via a physical medium, such as a removable drive. In certain circumstances, such as an air-gapped network compromise, exfiltration could occur via a physical medium or device introduced by a user. Such media could be an external hard drive, USB drive, cellular phone, MP3 player, or other removable storage and processing device. The physical medium or device could be used as the final exfiltration point or to hop between otherwise disconnected systems."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1052" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0010 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1052> ;
	.

attack:T1052.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1052 ;
	rdfs:label "Exfiltration Over Physical Medium: Exfiltration over USB"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to exfiltrate data over a USB connected physical device. In certain circumstances, such as an air-gapped network compromise, exfiltration could occur via a USB device introduced by a user. The USB device could be used as the final exfiltration point or to hop between otherwise disconnected systems."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1052.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0010 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1052/001> ;
	.

attack:T1053
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Scheduled Task/Job"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse task scheduling functionality to facilitate initial or recurring execution of malicious code. Utilities exist within all major operating systems to schedule programs or scripts to be executed at a specified date and time. A task can also be scheduled on a remote system, provided the proper authentication is met (ex: RPC and file and printer sharing in Windows environments)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1053" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1053> ;
	.

attack:T1053.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1053 ;
	rdfs:label "Scheduled Task/Job: At (Linux)"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse the at utility to perform task scheduling for initial, recurring, or future execution of malicious code. The at command within Linux operating systems enables administrators to schedule tasks. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1053.002."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1053.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1053.002 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1053/001> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1053.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1053 ;
	rdfs:label "Scheduled Task/Job: At"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse the at utility to perform task scheduling for initial or recurring execution of malicious code. The at utility exists as an executable within Windows, Linux, and macOS for scheduling tasks at a specified time and date. Although deprecated in favor of Scheduled Task's schtasks in Windows environments, using at requires that the Task Scheduler service be running, and the user to be logged on as a member of the local Administrators group."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1053.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1053/002> ;
	.

attack:T1053.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1053 ;
	rdfs:label "Scheduled Task/Job: Cron"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse the cron utility to perform task scheduling for initial or recurring execution of malicious code. The cron utility is a time-based job scheduler for Unix-like operating systems. The crontab file contains the schedule of cron entries to be run and the specified times for execution. Any crontab files are stored in operating system-specific file paths."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1053.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1053/003> ;
	.

attack:T1053.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1053 ;
	rdfs:label "Scheduled Task/Job: Launchd"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "This technique is deprecated due to the inaccurate usage. The report cited did not provide technical detail as to how the malware interacted directly with launchd rather than going through known services. Other system services are used to interact with launchd rather than launchd being used by itself. Deprecated by MITRE ATT&CK."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1053.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1053/004> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1053.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1053 ;
	rdfs:label "Scheduled Task/Job: Scheduled Task"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse the Windows Task Scheduler to perform task scheduling for initial or recurring execution of malicious code. There are multiple ways to access the Task Scheduler in Windows. The schtasks utility can be run directly on the command line, or the Task Scheduler can be opened through the GUI within the Administrator Tools section of the Control Panel."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1053.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1053/005> ;
	.

attack:T1053.006
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1053 ;
	rdfs:label "Scheduled Task/Job: Systemd Timers"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse systemd timers to perform task scheduling for initial or recurring execution of malicious code. Systemd timers are unit files with file extension .timer that control services. Timers can be set to run on a calendar event or after a time span relative to a starting point. They can be used as an alternative to Cron in Linux environments. Systemd timers may be activated remotely via the systemctl command line utility, which operates over SSH."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1053.006" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1053/006> ;
	.

attack:T1053.007
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1053 ;
	rdfs:label "Scheduled Task/Job: Container Orchestration Job"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse task scheduling functionality provided by container orchestration tools such as Kubernetes to schedule deployment of containers configured to execute malicious code. Container orchestration jobs run these automated tasks at a specific date and time, similar to cron jobs on a Linux system. Deployments of this type can also be configured to maintain a quantity of containers over time, automating the process of maintaining persistence within a cluster."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1053.007" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1053/007> ;
	.

attack:T1054
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Indicator Blocking"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may attempt to block indicators or events typically captured by sensors from being gathered and analyzed. This could include maliciously redirecting or even disabling host-based sensors, such as Event Tracing for Windows (ETW), by tampering settings that control the collection and flow of event telemetry. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1685."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1054" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1685 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1054> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1055
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Process Injection"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may inject code into processes in order to evade process-based defenses as well as possibly elevate privileges. Process injection is a method of executing arbitrary code in the address space of a separate live process. Running code in the context of another process may allow access to the process's memory, system/network resources, and possibly elevated privileges."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1055" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1055> ;
	.

attack:T1055.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1055 ;
	rdfs:label "Process Injection: Dynamic-link Library Injection"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may inject dynamic-link libraries (DLLs) into processes in order to evade process-based defenses as well as possibly elevate privileges. DLL injection is a method of executing arbitrary code in the address space of a separate live process."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1055.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1055/001> ;
	.

attack:T1055.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1055 ;
	rdfs:label "Process Injection: Portable Executable Injection"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may inject portable executables (PE) into processes in order to evade process-based defenses as well as possibly elevate privileges. PE injection is a method of executing arbitrary code in the address space of a separate live process."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1055.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1055/002> ;
	.

attack:T1055.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1055 ;
	rdfs:label "Process Injection: Thread Execution Hijacking"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may inject malicious code into hijacked processes in order to evade process-based defenses as well as possibly elevate privileges. Thread Execution Hijacking is a method of executing arbitrary code in the address space of a separate live process."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1055.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1055/003> ;
	.

attack:T1055.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1055 ;
	rdfs:label "Process Injection: Asynchronous Procedure Call"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may inject malicious code into processes via the asynchronous procedure call (APC) queue in order to evade process-based defenses as well as possibly elevate privileges. APC injection is a method of executing arbitrary code in the address space of a separate live process."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1055.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1055/004> ;
	.

attack:T1055.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1055 ;
	rdfs:label "Process Injection: Thread Local Storage"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may inject malicious code into processes via thread local storage (TLS) callbacks in order to evade process-based defenses as well as possibly elevate privileges. TLS callback injection is a method of executing arbitrary code in the address space of a separate live process."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1055.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
