attack:T1063
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Security Software Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of security software, configurations, defensive tools, and sensors that are installed on the system. This may include things such as local firewall rules and anti-virus. Adversaries may use the information from Security Software Discovery during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not the adversary fully infects the target and/or attempts specific actions. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1518.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1063" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1518.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1063> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1064
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Scripting"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "**This technique has been deprecated. Please use Command and Scripting Interpreter where appropriate.** Deprecated by MITRE ATT&CK."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1064" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1064> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1065
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Uncommonly Used Port"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may conduct C2 communications over a non-standard port to bypass proxies and firewalls that have been improperly configured. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1571."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1065" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1571 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1065> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1066
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Indicator Removal from Tools"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "If a malicious tool is detected and quarantined or otherwise curtailed, an adversary may be able to determine why the malicious tool was detected (the indicator), modify the tool by removing the indicator, and use the updated version that is no longer detected by the target's defensive systems or subsequent targets that may use similar systems. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1027.005."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1066" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1027.005 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1066> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1067
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Bootkit"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "A bootkit is a malware variant that modifies the boot sectors of a hard drive, including the Master Boot Record (MBR) and Volume Boot Record (VBR). Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1542.003."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1067" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1542.003 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1067> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1068
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Exploitation for Privilege Escalation"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may exploit software vulnerabilities in an attempt to elevate privileges. Exploitation of a software vulnerability occurs when an adversary takes advantage of a programming error in a program, service, or within the operating system software or kernel itself to execute adversary-controlled code."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1068" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1068> ;
	.

attack:T1069
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Permission Groups Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to discover group and permission settings. This information can help adversaries determine which user accounts and groups are available, the membership of users in particular groups, and which users and groups have elevated permissions."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1069" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1069> ;
	.

attack:T1069.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1069 ;
	rdfs:label "Permission Groups Discovery: Local Groups"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to find local system groups and permission settings. The knowledge of local system permission groups can help adversaries determine which groups exist and which users belong to a particular group. Adversaries may use this information to determine which users have elevated permissions, such as the users found within the local administrators group."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1069.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1069/001> ;
	.

attack:T1069.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1069 ;
	rdfs:label "Permission Groups Discovery: Domain Groups"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to find domain-level groups and permission settings. The knowledge of domain-level permission groups can help adversaries determine which groups exist and which users belong to a particular group. Adversaries may use this information to determine which users have elevated permissions, such as domain administrators."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1069.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1069/002> ;
	.

attack:T1069.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1069 ;
	rdfs:label "Permission Groups Discovery: Cloud Groups"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to find cloud groups and permission settings. The knowledge of cloud permission groups can help adversaries determine the particular roles of users and groups within an environment, as well as which users are associated with a particular group."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1069.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-08-04"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1069/003> ;
	.

attack:T1070
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Indicator Removal"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may selectively delete or modify artifacts generated to reduce indications of their presence and blend in with legitimate activity. Rather than broadly removing evidence, adversaries may target specific artifacts that appear anomalous or are likely to draw scrutiny, while leaving sufficient data intact to maintain the appearance of normal system behavior."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1070" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1070> ;
	.

attack:T1070.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1070 ;
	rdfs:label "Indicator Removal: Clear Windows Event Logs"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may clear Windows Event Logs to hide the activity of an intrusion. Windows Event Logs are a record of a computer's alerts and notifications. There are three system-defined sources of events: System, Application, and Security, with five event types: Error, Warning, Information, Success Audit, and Failure Audit. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1685.005."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1070.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1685.005 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1070/001> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1070.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1070 ;
	rdfs:label "Indicator Removal: Clear Linux or Mac System Logs"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may clear system logs to hide evidence of an intrusion. macOS and Linux both keep track of system or user-initiated actions via system logs. The majority of native system logging is stored under the /var/log/ directory. Subfolders in this directory categorize logs by their related functions, such as: Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1685.006."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1070.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1685.006 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1070/002> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1070.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1070 ;
	rdfs:label "Indicator Removal: Clear Command History"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "In addition to clearing system logs, an adversary may clear the command history of a compromised account to conceal the actions undertaken during an intrusion. Various command interpreters keep track of the commands users type in their terminal so that users can retrace what they've done."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1070.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1070/003> ;
	.

attack:T1070.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1070 ;
	rdfs:label "Indicator Removal: File Deletion"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may delete files left behind by the actions of their intrusion activity. Malware, tools, or other non-native files dropped or created on a system by an adversary (ex: Ingress Tool Transfer) may leave traces to indicate to what was done within a network and how. Removal of these files can occur during an intrusion, or as part of a post-intrusion process to minimize the adversary's footprint."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1070.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1070/004> ;
	.

attack:T1070.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1070 ;
	rdfs:label "Indicator Removal: Network Share Connection Removal"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may remove share connections that are no longer useful in order to clean up traces of their operation. Windows shared drive and SMB/Windows Admin Shares connections can be removed when no longer needed. Net is an example utility that can be used to remove network share connections with the net use \\\\system\\share /delete command."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1070.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1070/005> ;
	.

attack:T1070.006
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1070 ;
	rdfs:label "Indicator Removal: Timestomp"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify file time attributes to hide new files or changes to existing files. Timestomping is a technique that modifies the timestamps of a file (the modify, access, create, and change times), often to mimic files that are in the same folder and blend malicious files with legitimate files."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1070.006" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1070/006> ;
	.

attack:T1070.007
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1070 ;
	rdfs:label "Indicator Removal: Clear Network Connection History and Configurations"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may clear or remove evidence of malicious network connections in order to clean up traces of their operations. Configuration settings as well as various artifacts that highlight connection history may be created on a system and/or in application logs from behaviors that require network connections, such as Remote Services or External Remote Services. Defenders may use these artifacts to monitor or otherwise analyze network connections created by adversaries."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1070.007" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1070/007> ;
	.

attack:T1070.008
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1070 ;
	rdfs:label "Indicator Removal: Clear Mailbox Data"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify mail and mail application data to remove evidence of their activity. Email applications allow users and other programs to export and delete mailbox data via command line tools or use of APIs. Mail application data can be emails, email metadata, or logs generated by the application or operating system, such as export requests."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1070.008" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1070/008> ;
	.

attack:T1070.009
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1070 ;
	rdfs:label "Indicator Removal: Clear Persistence"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may clear artifacts associated with previously established persistence on a host system to remove evidence of their activity. This may involve various actions, such as removing services, deleting executables, Modify Registry, Plist File Modification, or other methods of cleanup to prevent defenders from collecting evidence of their persistent presence. Adversaries may also delete accounts previously created to maintain persistence (i.e. Create Account)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1070.009" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1070/009> ;
	.

attack:T1070.010
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1070 ;
	rdfs:label "Indicator Removal: Relocate Malware"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Once a payload is delivered, adversaries may reproduce copies of the same malware on the victim system to remove evidence of their presence and/or avoid defenses. Copying malware payloads to new locations may also be combined with File Deletion to cleanup older artifacts."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1070.010" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1070/010> ;
	.

attack:T1071
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Application Layer Protocol"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may communicate using OSI application layer protocols to avoid detection/network filtering by blending in with existing traffic. Commands to the remote system, and often the results of those commands, will be embedded within the protocol traffic between the client and server."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1071" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1071> ;
	.

attack:T1071.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1071 ;
	rdfs:label "Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may communicate using application layer protocols associated with web traffic to avoid detection/network filtering by blending in with existing traffic. Commands to the remote system, and often the results of those commands, will be embedded within the protocol traffic between the client and server."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1071.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1071/001> ;
	.

attack:T1071.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1071 ;
	rdfs:label "Application Layer Protocol: File Transfer Protocols"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may communicate using application layer protocols associated with transferring files to avoid detection/network filtering by blending in with existing traffic. Commands to the remote system, and often the results of those commands, will be embedded within the protocol traffic between the client and server."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1071.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1071/002> ;
	.

attack:T1071.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1071 ;
	rdfs:label "Application Layer Protocol: Mail Protocols"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may communicate using application layer protocols associated with electronic mail delivery to avoid detection/network filtering by blending in with existing traffic. Commands to the remote system, and often the results of those commands, will be embedded within the protocol traffic between the client and server."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1071.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1071/003> ;
	.

attack:T1071.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1071 ;
	rdfs:label "Application Layer Protocol: DNS"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may communicate using the Domain Name System (DNS) application layer protocol to avoid detection/network filtering by blending in with existing traffic. Commands to the remote system, and often the results of those commands, will be embedded within the protocol traffic between the client and server."@en ;
