	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1087/004> ;
	.

attack:T1088
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Bypass User Account Control"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Windows User Account Control (UAC) allows a program to elevate its privileges to perform a task under administrator-level permissions by prompting the user for confirmation. The impact to the user ranges from denying the operation under high enforcement to allowing the user to perform the action if they are in the local administrators group and click through the prompt or allowing them to enter an administrator password to complete the action. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1548.002."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1088" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1548.002 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1088> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1089
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Disabling Security Tools"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may disable security tools to avoid possible detection of their tools and activities. This can take the form of killing security software or event logging processes, deleting Registry keys so that tools do not start at run time, or other methods to interfere with security scanning or event reporting. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1685."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1089" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1685 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1089> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1090
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Proxy"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use a connection proxy to direct network traffic between systems or act as an intermediary for network communications to a command and control server to avoid direct connections to their infrastructure. Many tools exist that enable traffic redirection through proxies or port redirection, including HTRAN, ZXProxy, and ZXPortMap."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1090" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1090> ;
	.

attack:T1090.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1090 ;
	rdfs:label "Proxy: Internal Proxy"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use an internal proxy to direct command and control traffic between two or more systems in a compromised environment. Many tools exist that enable traffic redirection through proxies or port redirection, including HTRAN, ZXProxy, and ZXPortMap."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1090.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1090/001> ;
	.

attack:T1090.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1090 ;
	rdfs:label "Proxy: External Proxy"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use an external proxy to act as an intermediary for network communications to a command and control server to avoid direct connections to their infrastructure. Many tools exist that enable traffic redirection through proxies or port redirection, including HTRAN, ZXProxy, and ZXPortMap."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1090.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1090/002> ;
	.

attack:T1090.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1090 ;
	rdfs:label "Proxy: Multi-hop Proxy"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may chain together multiple proxies to disguise the source of malicious traffic. Typically, a defender will be able to identify the last proxy traffic traversed before it enters their network; the defender may or may not be able to identify any previous proxies before the last-hop proxy. This technique makes identifying the original source of the malicious traffic even more difficult by requiring the defender to trace malicious traffic through several proxies to identify its source."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1090.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1090/003> ;
	.

attack:T1090.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1090 ;
	rdfs:label "Proxy: Domain Fronting"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may take advantage of routing schemes in Content Delivery Networks (CDNs) and other services which host multiple domains to obfuscate the intended destination of HTTPS traffic or traffic tunneled through HTTPS. Domain fronting involves using different domain names in the SNI field of the TLS header and the Host field of the HTTP header. If both domains are served from the same CDN, then the CDN may route to the address specified in the HTTP header after unwrapping the TLS header."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1090.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1090/004> ;
	.

attack:T1091
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Replication Through Removable Media"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may move onto systems, possibly those on disconnected or air-gapped networks, by copying malware to removable media and taking advantage of Autorun features when the media is inserted into a system and executes. In the case of Lateral Movement, this may occur through modification of executable files stored on removable media or by copying malware and renaming it to look like a legitimate file to trick users into executing it on a separate system."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1091" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0008 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0001 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1091> ;
	.

attack:T1092
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Communication Through Removable Media"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries can perform command and control between compromised hosts on potentially disconnected networks using removable media to transfer commands from system to system. Both systems would need to be compromised, with the likelihood that an Internet-connected system was compromised first and the second through lateral movement by Replication Through Removable Media."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1092" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1092> ;
	.

attack:T1093
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Process Hollowing"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Process hollowing occurs when a process is created in a suspended state then its memory is unmapped and replaced with malicious code. Similar to Process Injection, execution of the malicious code is masked under a legitimate process and may evade defenses and detection analysis. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1055.012."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1093" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1055.012 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1093> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1094
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Custom Command and Control Protocol"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may communicate using a custom command and control protocol instead of encapsulating commands/data in an existing Application Layer Protocol. Implementations include mimicking well-known protocols or developing custom protocols (including raw sockets) on top of fundamental protocols provided by TCP/IP/another standard network stack. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1095."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1094" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1095 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1094> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1095
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Non-Application Layer Protocol"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use an OSI non-application layer protocol for communication between host and C2 server or among infected hosts within a network. The list of possible protocols is extensive. Specific examples include use of network layer protocols, such as the Internet Control Message Protocol (ICMP), transport layer protocols, such as the User Datagram Protocol (UDP), session layer protocols, such as Socket Secure (SOCKS), as well as redirected/tunneled protocols, such as Serial over LAN (SOL)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1095" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1095> ;
	.

attack:T1096
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "NTFS File Attributes"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Every New Technology File System (NTFS) formatted partition contains a Master File Table (MFT) that maintains a record for every file/directory on the partition. Within MFT entries are file attributes, such as Extended Attributes (EA) and Data [known as Alternate Data Streams (ADSs) when more than one Data attribute is present], that can be used to store arbitrary data (and even complete files). Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1564.004."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1096" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1564.004 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1096> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1097
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Pass the Ticket"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Pass the ticket (PtT) is a method of authenticating to a system using Kerberos tickets without having access to an account's password. Kerberos authentication can be used as the first step to lateral movement to a remote system. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1550.003."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1097" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0008 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1550.003 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1097> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1098
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Account Manipulation"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may manipulate accounts to maintain and/or elevate access to victim systems. Account manipulation may consist of any action that preserves or modifies adversary access to a compromised account, such as modifying credentials or permission groups. These actions could also include account activity designed to subvert security policies, such as performing iterative password updates to bypass password duration policies and preserve the life of compromised credentials."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1098" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1098> ;
	.

attack:T1098.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1098 ;
	rdfs:label "Account Manipulation: Additional Cloud Credentials"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may add adversary-controlled credentials to a cloud account to maintain persistent access to victim accounts and instances within the environment."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1098.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1098/001> ;
	.

attack:T1098.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1098 ;
	rdfs:label "Account Manipulation: Additional Email Delegate Permissions"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may grant additional permission levels to maintain persistent access to an adversary-controlled email account."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1098.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1098/002> ;
	.

attack:T1098.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1098 ;
	rdfs:label "Account Manipulation: Additional Cloud Roles"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may add additional roles or permissions to an adversary-controlled cloud account to maintain persistent access to a tenant. For example, adversaries may update IAM policies in cloud-based environments or add a new global administrator in Office 365 environments. With sufficient permissions, a compromised account can gain almost unlimited access to data and settings (including the ability to reset the passwords of other admins)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1098.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1098/003> ;
	.

attack:T1098.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1098 ;
	rdfs:label "Account Manipulation: SSH Authorized Keys"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify the SSH authorized_keys file to maintain persistence on a victim host. Linux distributions, macOS, and ESXi hypervisors commonly use key-based authentication to secure the authentication process of SSH sessions for remote management. The authorized_keys file in SSH specifies the SSH keys that can be used for logging into the user account for which the file is configured."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1098.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1098/004> ;
	.

attack:T1098.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1098 ;
	rdfs:label "Account Manipulation: Device Registration"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may register a device to an adversary-controlled account. Devices may be registered in a multifactor authentication (MFA) system, which handles authentication to the network, or in a device management system, which handles device access and compliance."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1098.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-05-22"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1098/005> ;
	.

attack:T1098.006
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1098 ;
	rdfs:label "Account Manipulation: Additional Container Cluster Roles"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may add additional roles or permissions to an adversary-controlled user or service account to maintain persistent access to a container orchestration system. For example, an adversary with sufficient permissions may create a RoleBinding or a ClusterRoleBinding to bind a Role or ClusterRole to a Kubernetes account."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1098.006" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1098/006> ;
	.

attack:T1098.007
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1098 ;
	rdfs:label "Account Manipulation: Additional Local or Domain Groups"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may add additional local or domain groups to an adversary-controlled account to maintain persistent access to a system or domain."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1098.007" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-09-26"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1098/007> ;
	.

attack:T1099
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Timestomp"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may take actions to hide the deployment of new, or modification of existing files to obfuscate their activities. Timestomping is a technique that modifies the timestamps of a file (the modify, access, create, and change times), often to mimic files that are in the same folder. This is done, for example, on files that have been modified or created by the adversary so that they do not appear conspicuous to forensic investigators or file analysis tools. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1070.006."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1099" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1070.006 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1099> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1100
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Web Shell"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "A Web shell is a Web script that is placed on an openly accessible Web server to allow an adversary to use the Web server as a gateway into a network. A Web shell may provide a set of functions to execute or a command-line interface on the system that hosts the Web server. In addition to a server-side script, a Web shell may have a client interface program that is used to talk to the Web server (see, for example, China Chopper Web shell client). Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1505.003."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1100" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1505.003 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1100> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1101
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Security Support Provider"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Windows Security Support Provider (SSP) DLLs are loaded into the Local Security Authority (LSA) process at system start. Once loaded into the LSA, SSP DLLs have access to encrypted and plaintext passwords that are stored in Windows, such as any logged-on user's Domain password or smart card PINs. The SSP configuration is stored in two Registry keys: HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Lsa\\Security Packages and HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Lsa\\OSConfig\\Security Packages. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1547.005."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1101" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1547.005 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1101> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1102
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Web Service"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use an existing, legitimate external Web service as a means for relaying data to/from a compromised system. Popular websites, cloud services, and social media acting as a mechanism for C2 may give a significant amount of cover due to the likelihood that hosts within a network are already communicating with them prior to a compromise. Using common services, such as those offered by Google, Microsoft, or Twitter, makes it easier for adversaries to hide in expected noise."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1102" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1102> ;
	.

attack:T1102.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1102 ;
	rdfs:label "Web Service: Dead Drop Resolver"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use an existing, legitimate external Web service to host information that points to additional command and control (C2) infrastructure. Adversaries may post content, known as a dead drop resolver, on Web services with embedded (and often obfuscated/encoded) domains or IP addresses. Once infected, victims will reach out to and be redirected by these resolvers."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1102.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1102/001> ;
	.

attack:T1102.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1102 ;
	rdfs:label "Web Service: Bidirectional Communication"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use an existing, legitimate external Web service as a means for sending commands to and receiving output from a compromised system over the Web service channel. Compromised systems may leverage popular websites and social media to host command and control (C2) instructions. Those infected systems can then send the output from those commands back over that Web service channel. The return traffic may occur in a variety of ways, depending on the Web service being utilized."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1102.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1102/002> ;
	.

attack:T1102.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1102 ;
	rdfs:label "Web Service: One-Way Communication"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use an existing, legitimate external Web service as a means for sending commands to a compromised system without receiving return output over the Web service channel. Compromised systems may leverage popular websites and social media to host command and control (C2) instructions. Those infected systems may opt to send the output from those commands back over a different C2 channel, including to another distinct Web service."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1102.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1102/003> ;
	.

attack:T1103
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "AppInit DLLs"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Dynamic-link libraries (DLLs) that are specified in the AppInit_DLLs value in the Registry keys HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Windows or HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\Software\\Wow6432Node\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Windows are loaded by user32.dll into every process that loads user32.dll. In practice this is nearly every program, since user32.dll is a very common library. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1546.010."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1103" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1546.010 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1103> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1104
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Multi-Stage Channels"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may create multiple stages for command and control that are employed under different conditions or for certain functions. Use of multiple stages may obfuscate the command and control channel to make detection more difficult."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1104" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1104> ;
	.

attack:T1105
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Ingress Tool Transfer"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may transfer tools or other files from an external system into a compromised environment. Tools or files may be copied from an external adversary-controlled system to the victim network through the command and control channel or through alternate protocols such as ftp. Once present, adversaries may also transfer/spread tools between victim devices within a compromised environment (i.e. Lateral Tool Transfer)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1105" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1105> ;
	.

attack:T1106
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Native API"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may interact with the native OS application programming interface (API) to execute behaviors. Native APIs provide a controlled means of calling low-level OS services within the kernel, such as those involving hardware/devices, memory, and processes. These native APIs are leveraged by the OS during system boot (when other system components are not yet initialized) as well as carrying out tasks and requests during routine operations."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1106" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1106> ;
	.

attack:T1107
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "File Deletion"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may delete files left behind by the actions of their intrusion activity. Malware, tools, or other non-native files dropped or created on a system by an adversary may leave traces to indicate to what was done within a network and how. Removal of these files can occur during an intrusion, or as part of a post-intrusion process to minimize the adversary's footprint. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1070.004."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1107" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1070.004 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1107> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1108
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Redundant Access"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "**This technique has been deprecated. Please use Create Account, Web Shell, and External Remote Services where appropriate.** Deprecated by MITRE ATT&CK."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1108" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1108> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1109
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Component Firmware"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Some adversaries may employ sophisticated means to compromise computer components and install malicious firmware that will execute adversary code outside of the operating system and main system firmware or BIOS. This technique may be similar to System Firmware but conducted upon other system components that may not have the same capability or level of integrity checking. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1542.002."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1109" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1542.002 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1109> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1110
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Brute Force"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use brute force techniques to gain access to accounts when passwords are unknown or when password hashes are obtained. Without knowledge of the password for an account or set of accounts, an adversary may systematically guess the password using a repetitive or iterative mechanism. Brute forcing passwords can take place via interaction with a service that will check the validity of those credentials or offline against previously acquired credential data, such as password hashes."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1110" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1110> ;
	.

attack:T1110.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1110 ;
	rdfs:label "Brute Force: Password Guessing"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries with no prior knowledge of legitimate credentials within the system or environment may guess passwords to attempt access to accounts. Without knowledge of the password for an account, an adversary may opt to systematically guess the password using a repetitive or iterative mechanism. An adversary may guess login credentials without prior knowledge of system or environment passwords during an operation by using a list of common passwords."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1110.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1110/001> ;
	.

attack:T1110.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1110 ;
	rdfs:label "Brute Force: Password Cracking"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use password cracking to attempt to recover usable credentials, such as plaintext passwords, when credential material such as password hashes are obtained. OS Credential Dumping can be used to obtain password hashes, this may only get an adversary so far when Pass the Hash is not an option. Further, adversaries may leverage Data from Configuration Repository in order to obtain hashed credentials for network devices."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1110.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1110/002> ;
	.

attack:T1110.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1110 ;
	rdfs:label "Brute Force: Password Spraying"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use a single or small list of commonly used passwords against many different accounts to attempt to acquire valid account credentials. Password spraying uses one password (e.g. 'Password01'), or a small list of commonly used passwords, that may match the complexity policy of the domain. Logins are attempted with that password against many different accounts on a network to avoid account lockouts that would normally occur when brute forcing a single account with many passwords."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1110.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1110/003> ;
	.

attack:T1110.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1110 ;
	rdfs:label "Brute Force: Credential Stuffing"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use credentials obtained from breach dumps of unrelated accounts to gain access to target accounts through credential overlap. Occasionally, large numbers of username and password pairs are dumped online when a website or service is compromised and the user account credentials accessed. The information may be useful to an adversary attempting to compromise accounts by taking advantage of the tendency for users to use the same passwords across personal and business accounts."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1110.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1110/004> ;
	.

attack:T1111
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Multi-Factor Authentication Interception"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may target multi-factor authentication (MFA) mechanisms, (i.e., smart cards, token generators, etc.) to gain access to credentials that can be used to access systems, services, and network resources. Use of MFA is recommended and provides a higher level of security than usernames and passwords alone, but organizations should be aware of techniques that could be used to intercept and bypass these security mechanisms."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1111" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1111> ;
	.

attack:T1112
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Registry"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may interact with the Windows Registry as part of a variety of other techniques to aid in defense evasion, persistence, and execution."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1112" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1112> ;
	.

attack:T1113
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Screen Capture"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to take screen captures of the desktop to gather information over the course of an operation. Screen capturing functionality may be included as a feature of a remote access tool used in post-compromise operations. Taking a screenshot is also typically possible through native utilities or API calls, such as CopyFromScreen, xwd, or screencapture."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1113" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0009 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1113> ;
	.

attack:T1114
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Email Collection"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may target user email to collect sensitive information. Emails may contain sensitive data, including trade secrets or personal information, that can prove valuable to adversaries. Emails may also contain details of ongoing incident response operations, which may allow adversaries to adjust their techniques in order to maintain persistence or evade defenses. Adversaries can collect or forward email from mail servers or clients."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1114" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0009 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1114> ;
	.

attack:T1114.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1114 ;
	rdfs:label "Email Collection: Local Email Collection"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may target user email on local systems to collect sensitive information. Files containing email data can be acquired from a user’s local system, such as Outlook storage or cache files."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1114.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0009 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1114/001> ;
	.

attack:T1114.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1114 ;
	rdfs:label "Email Collection: Remote Email Collection"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may target an Exchange server, Office 365, or Google Workspace to collect sensitive information. Adversaries may leverage a user's credentials and interact directly with the Exchange server to acquire information from within a network. Adversaries may also access externally facing Exchange services, Office 365, or Google Workspace to access email using credentials or access tokens. Tools such as MailSniper can be used to automate searches for specific keywords."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1114.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0009 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1114/002> ;
	.

attack:T1114.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1114 ;
	rdfs:label "Email Collection: Email Forwarding Rule"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may setup email forwarding rules to collect sensitive information. Adversaries may abuse email forwarding rules to monitor the activities of a victim, steal information, and further gain intelligence on the victim or the victim’s organization to use as part of further exploits or operations. Furthermore, email forwarding rules can allow adversaries to maintain persistent access to victim's emails even after compromised credentials are reset by administrators."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1114.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0009 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1114/003> ;
	.

attack:T1115
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Clipboard Data"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may collect data stored in the clipboard from users copying information within or between applications."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1115" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0009 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1115> ;
	.

attack:T1116
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Code Signing"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Code signing provides a level of authenticity on a binary from the developer and a guarantee that the binary has not been tampered with. However, adversaries are known to use code signing certificates to masquerade malware and tools as legitimate binaries. The certificates used during an operation may be created, forged, or stolen by the adversary. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1553.002."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1116" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1553.002 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1116> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1117
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Regsvr32"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Regsvr32.exe is a command-line program used to register and unregister object linking and embedding controls, including dynamic link libraries (DLLs), on Windows systems. Regsvr32.exe can be used to execute arbitrary binaries. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1218.010."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1117" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1218.010 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1117> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1118
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "InstallUtil"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "InstallUtil is a command-line utility that allows for installation and uninstallation of resources by executing specific installer components specified in .NET binaries. InstallUtil is located in the .NET directories on a Windows system: C:\\Windows\\Microsoft.NET\\Framework\\v\\InstallUtil.exe and C:\\Windows\\Microsoft.NET\\Framework64\\v\\InstallUtil.exe. InstallUtil.exe is digitally signed by Microsoft. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1218.004."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1118" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1218.004 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1118> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1119
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Automated Collection"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Once established within a system or network, an adversary may use automated techniques for collecting internal data. Methods for performing this technique could include use of a Command and Scripting Interpreter to search for and copy information fitting set criteria such as file type, location, or name at specific time intervals."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1119" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0009 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1119> ;
	.

attack:T1120
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Peripheral Device Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to gather information about attached peripheral devices and components connected to a computer system. Peripheral devices could include auxiliary resources that support a variety of functionalities such as keyboards, printers, cameras, smart card readers, or removable storage. The information may be used to enhance their awareness of the system and network environment or may be used for further actions."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1120" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1120> ;
	.

attack:T1121
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Regsvcs/Regasm"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Regsvcs and Regasm are Windows command-line utilities that are used to register .NET Component Object Model (COM) assemblies. Both are digitally signed by Microsoft. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1218.009."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1121" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1218.009 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1121> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1122
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Component Object Model Hijacking"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "The Component Object Model (COM) is a system within Windows to enable interaction between software components through the operating system. Adversaries can use this system to insert malicious code that can be executed in place of legitimate software through hijacking the COM references and relationships as a means for persistence. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1546.015."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1122" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1546.015 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1122> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1123
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Audio Capture"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary can leverage a computer's peripheral devices (e.g., microphones and webcams) or applications (e.g., voice and video call services) to capture audio recordings for the purpose of listening into sensitive conversations to gather information."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1123" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0009 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1123> ;
	.

attack:T1124
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "System Time Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may gather the system time and/or time zone settings from a local or remote system. The system time is set and stored by services, such as the Windows Time Service on Windows or systemsetup on macOS. These time settings may also be synchronized between systems and services in an enterprise network, typically accomplished with a network time server within a domain."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1124" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1124> ;
	.

attack:T1125
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Video Capture"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary can leverage a computer's peripheral devices (e.g., integrated cameras or webcams) or applications (e.g., video call services) to capture video recordings for the purpose of gathering information. Images may also be captured from devices or applications, potentially in specified intervals, in lieu of video files."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1125" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0009 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1125> ;
	.

attack:T1126
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Network Share Connection Removal"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may remove share connections that are no longer useful in order to clean up traces of their operation. Windows shared drive and Windows Admin Shares connections can be removed when no longer needed. Net is an example utility that can be used to remove network share connections with the net use \\\\system\\share /delete command. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1070.005."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1126" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1070.005 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1126> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1127
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Trusted Developer Utilities Proxy Execution"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may take advantage of trusted developer utilities to proxy execution of malicious payloads. There are many utilities used for software development related tasks that can be used to execute code in various forms to assist in development, debugging, and reverse engineering."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1127" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1127> ;
	.

attack:T1127.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1127 ;
	rdfs:label "Trusted Developer Utilities Proxy Execution: MSBuild"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use MSBuild to proxy execution of code through a trusted Windows utility. MSBuild.exe (Microsoft Build Engine) is a software build platform used by Visual Studio. It handles XML formatted project files that define requirements for loading and building various platforms and configurations."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1127.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1127/001> ;
	.

attack:T1127.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1127 ;
	rdfs:label "Trusted Developer Utilities Proxy Execution: ClickOnce"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use ClickOnce applications (.appref-ms and .application files) to proxy execution of code through a trusted Windows utility. ClickOnce is a deployment that enables a user to create self-updating Windows-based .NET applications (i.e, .XBAP, .EXE, or .DLL) that install and run from a file share or web page with minimal user interaction. The application launches as a child process of DFSVC.EXE, which is responsible for installing, launching, and updating the application."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1127.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1127/002> ;
	.

attack:T1127.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1127 ;
	rdfs:label "Trusted Developer Utilities Proxy Execution: JamPlus"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use `JamPlus` to proxy the execution of a malicious script. `JamPlus` is a build utility tool for code and data build systems. It works with several popular compilers and can be used for generating workspaces in code editors such as Visual Studio."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1127.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1127/003> ;
	.

attack:T1128
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Netsh Helper DLL"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Netsh.exe (also referred to as Netshell) is a command-line scripting utility used to interact with the network configuration of a system. It contains functionality to add helper DLLs for extending functionality of the utility. The paths to registered netsh.exe helper DLLs are entered into the Windows Registry at HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Netsh. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1546.007."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1128" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1546.007 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1128> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1129
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Shared Modules"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may execute malicious payloads via loading shared modules. Shared modules are executable files that are loaded into processes to provide access to reusable code, such as specific custom functions or invoking OS API functions (i.e., Native API)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1129" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1129> ;
	.

attack:T1130
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Install Root Certificate"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Root certificates are used in public key cryptography to identify a root certificate authority (CA). When a root certificate is installed, the system or application will trust certificates in the root's chain of trust that have been signed by the root certificate. Certificates are commonly used for establishing secure TLS/SSL communications within a web browser. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1553.004."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1130" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1553.004 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1130> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1131
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Authentication Package"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Windows Authentication Package DLLs are loaded by the Local Security Authority (LSA) process at system start. They provide support for multiple logon processes and multiple security protocols to the operating system. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1547.002."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1131" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1547.002 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1131> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1132
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Data Encoding"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may encode data to make the content of command and control traffic more difficult to detect. Command and control (C2) information can be encoded using a standard data encoding system. Use of data encoding may adhere to existing protocol specifications and includes use of ASCII, Unicode, Base64, MIME, or other binary-to-text and character encoding systems. Some data encoding systems may also result in data compression, such as gzip."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1132" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1132> ;
	.

attack:T1132.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1132 ;
	rdfs:label "Data Encoding: Standard Encoding"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may encode data with a standard data encoding system to make the content of command and control traffic more difficult to detect. Command and control (C2) information can be encoded using a standard data encoding system that adheres to existing protocol specifications. Common data encoding schemes include ASCII, Unicode, hexadecimal, Base64, and MIME. Some data encoding systems may also result in data compression, such as gzip."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1132.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1132/001> ;
	.

attack:T1132.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1132 ;
	rdfs:label "Data Encoding: Non-Standard Encoding"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may encode data with a non-standard data encoding system to make the content of command and control traffic more difficult to detect. Command and control (C2) information can be encoded using a non-standard data encoding system that diverges from existing protocol specifications. Non-standard data encoding schemes may be based on or related to standard data encoding schemes, such as a modified Base64 encoding for the message body of an HTTP request."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1132.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1132/002> ;
	.

attack:T1133
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "External Remote Services"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may leverage external-facing remote services to initially access and/or persist within a network. Remote services such as VPNs, Citrix, and other access mechanisms allow users to connect to internal enterprise network resources from external locations. There are often remote service gateways that manage connections and credential authentication for these services. Services such as Windows Remote Management and VNC can also be used externally."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1133" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0001 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1133> ;
	.

attack:T1134
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Access Token Manipulation"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify access tokens to operate under a different user or system security context to perform actions and bypass access controls. Windows uses access tokens to determine the ownership of a running process. A user can manipulate access tokens to make a running process appear as though it is the child of a different process or belongs to someone other than the user that started the process."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1134" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1134> ;
	.

attack:T1134.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1134 ;
	rdfs:label "Access Token Manipulation: Token Impersonation/Theft"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may duplicate then impersonate another user's existing token to escalate privileges and bypass access controls. For example, an adversary can duplicate an existing token using `DuplicateToken` or `DuplicateTokenEx`. The token can then be used with `ImpersonateLoggedOnUser` to allow the calling thread to impersonate a logged on user's security context, or with `SetThreadToken` to assign the impersonated token to a thread."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1134.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1134/001> ;
	.

attack:T1134.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1134 ;
	rdfs:label "Access Token Manipulation: Create Process with Token"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may create a new process with an existing token to escalate privileges and bypass access controls. Processes can be created with the token and resulting security context of another user using features such as CreateProcessWithTokenW and runas."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1134.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1134/002> ;
	.

attack:T1134.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1134 ;
	rdfs:label "Access Token Manipulation: Make and Impersonate Token"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may make new tokens and impersonate users to escalate privileges and bypass access controls. For example, if an adversary has a username and password but the user is not logged onto the system the adversary can then create a logon session for the user using the `LogonUser` function. The function will return a copy of the new session's access token and the adversary can use `SetThreadToken` to assign the token to a thread."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1134.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1134/003> ;
	.

attack:T1134.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1134 ;
	rdfs:label "Access Token Manipulation: Parent PID Spoofing"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may spoof the parent process identifier (PPID) of a new process to evade process-monitoring defenses or to elevate privileges. New processes are typically spawned directly from their parent, or calling, process unless explicitly specified. One way of explicitly assigning the PPID of a new process is via the CreateProcess API call, which supports a parameter that defines the PPID to use."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1134.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1134/004> ;
	.

attack:T1134.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1134 ;
	rdfs:label "Access Token Manipulation: SID-History Injection"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use SID-History Injection to escalate privileges and bypass access controls. The Windows security identifier (SID) is a unique value that identifies a user or group account. SIDs are used by Windows security in both security descriptors and access tokens. An account can hold additional SIDs in the SID-History Active Directory attribute, allowing inter-operable account migration between domains (e.g., all values in SID-History are included in access tokens)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1134.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1134/005> ;
	.

attack:T1135
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Network Share Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may look for folders and drives shared on remote systems as a means of identifying sources of information to gather as a precursor for Collection and to identify potential systems of interest for Lateral Movement. Networks often contain shared network drives and folders that enable users to access file directories on various systems across a network."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1135" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1135> ;
	.

attack:T1136
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Create Account"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may create an account to maintain access to victim systems. With a sufficient level of access, creating such accounts may be used to establish secondary credentialed access that do not require persistent remote access tools to be deployed on the system."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1136" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1136> ;
	.

attack:T1136.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1136 ;
	rdfs:label "Create Account: Local Account"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may create a local account to maintain access to victim systems. Local accounts are those configured by an organization for use by users, remote support, services, or for administration on a single system or service."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1136.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1136/001> ;
	.

attack:T1136.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1136 ;
	rdfs:label "Create Account: Domain Account"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may create a domain account to maintain access to victim systems. Domain accounts are those managed by Active Directory Domain Services where access and permissions are configured across systems and services that are part of that domain. Domain accounts can cover user, administrator, and service accounts. With a sufficient level of access, the net user /add /domain command can be used to create a domain account."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1136.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1136/002> ;
	.

attack:T1136.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1136 ;
	rdfs:label "Create Account: Cloud Account"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may create a cloud account to maintain access to victim systems. With a sufficient level of access, such accounts may be used to establish secondary credentialed access that does not require persistent remote access tools to be deployed on the system."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1136.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1136/003> ;
	.

attack:T1137
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Office Application Startup"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may leverage Microsoft Office-based applications for persistence between startups. Microsoft Office is a fairly common application suite on Windows-based operating systems within an enterprise network. There are multiple mechanisms that can be used with Office for persistence when an Office-based application is started; this can include the use of Office Template Macros and add-ins."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1137" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1137> ;
	.

attack:T1137.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1137 ;
	rdfs:label "Office Application Startup: Office Template Macros"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse Microsoft Office templates to obtain persistence on a compromised system. Microsoft Office contains templates that are part of common Office applications and are used to customize styles. The base templates within the application are used each time an application starts."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1137.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1137/001> ;
	.

attack:T1137.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1137 ;
	rdfs:label "Office Application Startup: Office Test"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse the Microsoft Office \"Office Test\" Registry key to obtain persistence on a compromised system. An Office Test Registry location exists that allows a user to specify an arbitrary DLL that will be executed every time an Office application is started. This Registry key is thought to be used by Microsoft to load DLLs for testing and debugging purposes while developing Office applications. This Registry key is not created by default during an Office installation."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1137.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1137/002> ;
	.

attack:T1137.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1137 ;
	rdfs:label "Office Application Startup: Outlook Forms"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse Microsoft Outlook forms to obtain persistence on a compromised system. Outlook forms are used as templates for presentation and functionality in Outlook messages. Custom Outlook forms can be created that will execute code when a specifically crafted email is sent by an adversary utilizing the same custom Outlook form."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1137.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1137/003> ;
	.

attack:T1137.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1137 ;
	rdfs:label "Office Application Startup: Outlook Home Page"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse Microsoft Outlook's Home Page feature to obtain persistence on a compromised system. Outlook Home Page is a legacy feature used to customize the presentation of Outlook folders. This feature allows for an internal or external URL to be loaded and presented whenever a folder is opened. A malicious HTML page can be crafted that will execute code when loaded by Outlook Home Page."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1137.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1137/004> ;
	.

attack:T1137.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1137 ;
	rdfs:label "Office Application Startup: Outlook Rules"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse Microsoft Outlook rules to obtain persistence on a compromised system. Outlook rules allow a user to define automated behavior to manage email messages. A benign rule might, for example, automatically move an email to a particular folder in Outlook if it contains specific words from a specific sender. Malicious Outlook rules can be created that can trigger code execution when an adversary sends a specifically crafted email to that user."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1137.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1137/005> ;
	.

attack:T1137.006
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1137 ;
	rdfs:label "Office Application Startup: Add-ins"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse Microsoft Office add-ins to obtain persistence on a compromised system. Office add-ins can be used to add functionality to Office programs. There are different types of add-ins that can be used by the various Office products; including Word/Excel add-in Libraries (WLL/XLL), VBA add-ins, Office Component Object Model (COM) add-ins, automation add-ins, VBA Editor (VBE), Visual Studio Tools for Office (VSTO) add-ins, and Outlook add-ins."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1137.006" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1137/006> ;
	.

attack:T1138
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Application Shimming"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "The Microsoft Windows Application Compatibility Infrastructure/Framework (Application Shim) was created to allow for backward compatibility of software as the operating system codebase changes over time. For example, the application shimming feature allows developers to apply fixes to applications (without rewriting code) that were created for Windows XP so that it will work with Windows 10. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1546.011."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1138" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1546.011 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1138> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1139
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Bash History"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Bash keeps track of the commands users type on the command-line with the \"history\" utility. Once a user logs out, the history is flushed to the user’s .bash_history file. For each user, this file resides at the same location: ~/.bash_history. Typically, this file keeps track of the user’s last 500 commands. Users often type usernames and passwords on the command-line as parameters to programs, which then get saved to this file when they log out. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1552.003."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1139" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1552.003 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1139> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1140
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use Obfuscated Files or Information to hide artifacts of an intrusion from analysis. They may require separate mechanisms to decode or deobfuscate that information depending on how they intend to use it. Methods for doing that include built-in functionality of malware or by using utilities present on the system."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1140" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1140> ;
	.

attack:T1141
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Input Prompt"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "When programs are executed that need additional privileges than are present in the current user context, it is common for the operating system to prompt the user for proper credentials to authorize the elevated privileges for the task (ex: Bypass User Account Control). Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1056.002."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1141" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1056.002 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1141> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1142
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Keychain"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Keychains are the built-in way for macOS to keep track of users' passwords and credentials for many services and features such as WiFi passwords, websites, secure notes, certificates, and Kerberos. Keychain files are located in ~/Library/Keychains/,/Library/Keychains/, and /Network/Library/Keychains/. The security command-line utility, which is built into macOS by default, provides a useful way to manage these credentials. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1555.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1142" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1555.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1142> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1143
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Hidden Window"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may implement hidden windows to conceal malicious activity from the plain sight of users. In some cases, windows that would typically be displayed when an application carries out an operation can be hidden. This may be utilized by system administrators to avoid disrupting user work environments when carrying out administrative tasks. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1564.003."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1143" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1564.003 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1143> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1144
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Gatekeeper Bypass"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "In macOS and OS X, when applications or programs are downloaded from the internet, there is a special attribute set on the file called com.apple.quarantine. This attribute is read by Apple's Gatekeeper defense program at execution time and provides a prompt to the user to allow or deny execution. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1553.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1144" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1553.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1144> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1145
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Private Keys"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Private cryptographic keys and certificates are used for authentication, encryption/decryption, and digital signatures. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1552.004."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1145" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1552.004 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1145> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1146
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Clear Command History"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "In addition to clearing system logs, an adversary may clear the command history of a compromised account to conceal the actions undertaken during an intrusion. macOS and Linux both keep track of the commands users type in their terminal so that users can retrace what they've done. These logs can be accessed in a few different ways. While logged in, this command history is tracked in a file pointed to by the environment variable HISTFILE. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1070.003."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1146" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1070.003 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1146> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1147
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Hidden Users"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Every user account in macOS has a userID associated with it. When creating a user, you can specify the userID for that account. There is a property value in /Library/Preferences/com.apple.loginwindow called Hide500Users that prevents users with userIDs 500 and lower from appearing at the login screen. By using the Create Account technique with a userID under 500 and enabling this property (setting it to Yes), an adversary can hide their user accounts much more easily: sudo dscl . Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1564.002."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1147" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1564.002 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1147> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1148
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "HISTCONTROL"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "The HISTCONTROL environment variable keeps track of what should be saved by the history command and eventually into the ~/.bash_history file when a user logs out. This setting can be configured to ignore commands that start with a space by simply setting it to \"ignorespace\". HISTCONTROL can also be set to ignore duplicate commands by setting it to \"ignoredups\". In some Linux systems, this is set by default to \"ignoreboth\" which covers both of the previous examples. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1690."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1148" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1690 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1148> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1149
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "LC_MAIN Hijacking"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "**This technique has been deprecated and should no longer be used.** Deprecated by MITRE ATT&CK."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1149" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1149> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1150
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Plist Modification"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Property list (plist) files contain all of the information that macOS and OS X uses to configure applications and services. These files are UTF-8 encoded and formatted like XML documents via a series of keys surrounded by < >. They detail when programs should execute, file paths to the executables, program arguments, required OS permissions, and many others. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1547.011."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1150" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1547.011 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1150> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1151
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Space after Filename"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries can hide a program's true filetype by changing the extension of a file. With certain file types (specifically this does not work with .app extensions), appending a space to the end of a filename will change how the file is processed by the operating system. For example, if there is a Mach-O executable file called evil.bin, when it is double clicked by a user, it will launch Terminal.app and execute. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1036.006."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1151" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1036.006 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1151> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1152
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Launchctl"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Launchctl controls the macOS launchd process which handles things like launch agents and launch daemons, but can execute other commands or programs itself. Launchctl supports taking subcommands on the command-line, interactively, or even redirected from standard input. By loading or reloading launch agents or launch daemons, adversaries can install persistence or execute changes they made. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1569.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1152" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1569.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1152> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1153
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Source"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "**This technique has been deprecated and should no longer be used.** Deprecated by MITRE ATT&CK."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1153" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1153> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1154
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Trap"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "The trap command allows programs and shells to specify commands that will be executed upon receiving interrupt signals. A common situation is a script allowing for graceful termination and handling of common keyboard interrupts like ctrl+c and ctrl+d. Adversaries can use this to register code to be executed when the shell encounters specific interrupts either to gain execution or as a persistence mechanism. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1546.005."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1154" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1546.005 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1154> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1155
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "AppleScript"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "macOS and OS X applications send AppleEvent messages to each other for interprocess communications (IPC). These messages can be easily scripted with AppleScript for local or remote IPC. Osascript executes AppleScript and any other Open Scripting Architecture (OSA) language scripts. A list of OSA languages installed on a system can be found by using the osalang program. AppleEvent messages can be sent independently or as part of a script. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1059.002."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1155" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1059.002 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1155> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1156
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Malicious Shell Modification"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may establish persistence through executing malicious commands triggered by a user’s shell. User shells execute several configuration scripts at different points throughout the session based on events. For example, when a user opens a command line interface or remotely logs in (such as SSH) a login shell is initiated. The login shell executes scripts from the system (/etc) and the user’s home directory (~/) to configure the environment. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1546.004."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1156" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1546.004 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1156> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1157
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Dylib Hijacking"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "macOS and OS X use a common method to look for required dynamic libraries (dylib) to load into a program based on search paths. Adversaries can take advantage of ambiguous paths to plant dylibs to gain privilege escalation or persistence. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1574.004."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1157" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1574.004 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1157> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1158
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Hidden Files and Directories"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "To prevent normal users from accidentally changing special files on a system, most operating systems have the concept of a ‘hidden’ file. These files don’t show up when a user browses the file system with a GUI or when using normal commands on the command line. Users must explicitly ask to show the hidden files either via a series of Graphical User Interface (GUI) prompts or with command line switches (dir /a for Windows and ls –a for Linux and macOS). Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1564.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1158" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1564.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1158> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1159
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Launch Agent"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Per Apple’s developer documentation, when a user logs in, a per-user launchd process is started which loads the parameters for each launch-on-demand user agent from the property list (plist) files found in /System/Library/LaunchAgents, /Library/LaunchAgents, and $HOME/Library/LaunchAgents. These launch agents have property list files which point to the executables that will be launched. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1543.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1159" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1543.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1159> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1160
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Launch Daemon"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Per Apple’s developer documentation, when macOS and OS X boot up, launchd is run to finish system initialization. This process loads the parameters for each launch-on-demand system-level daemon from the property list (plist) files found in /System/Library/LaunchDaemons and /Library/LaunchDaemons. These LaunchDaemons have property list files which point to the executables that will be launched. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1543.004."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1160" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1543.004 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1160> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1161
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "LC_LOAD_DYLIB Addition"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Mach-O binaries have a series of headers that are used to perform certain operations when a binary is loaded. The LC_LOAD_DYLIB header in a Mach-O binary tells macOS and OS X which dynamic libraries (dylibs) to load during execution time. These can be added ad-hoc to the compiled binary as long adjustments are made to the rest of the fields and dependencies. There are tools available to perform these changes. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1546.006."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1161" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1546.006 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1161> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1162
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Login Item"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "MacOS provides the option to list specific applications to run when a user logs in. These applications run under the logged in user's context, and will be started every time the user logs in. Login items installed using the Service Management Framework are not visible in the System Preferences and can only be removed by the application that created them. Users have direct control over login items installed using a shared file list which are also visible in System Preferences. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1547.011."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1162" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1547.011 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1162> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1163
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Rc.common"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "During the boot process, macOS executes source /etc/rc.common, which is a shell script containing various utility functions. This file also defines routines for processing command-line arguments and for gathering system settings, and is thus recommended to include in the start of Startup Item Scripts. In macOS and OS X, this is now a deprecated technique in favor of launch agents and launch daemons, but is currently still used. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1037.004."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1163" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1037.004 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1163> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1164
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Re-opened Applications"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Starting in Mac OS X 10.7 (Lion), users can specify certain applications to be re-opened when a user reboots their machine. While this is usually done via a Graphical User Interface (GUI) on an app-by-app basis, there are property list files (plist) that contain this information as well located at ~/Library/Preferences/com.apple.loginwindow.plist and ~/Library/Preferences/ByHost/com.apple.loginwindow.* .plist. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1547.007."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1164" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1547.007 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1164> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1165
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Startup Items"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Per Apple’s documentation, startup items execute during the final phase of the boot process and contain shell scripts or other executable files along with configuration information used by the system to determine the execution order for all startup items. This is technically a deprecated version (superseded by Launch Daemons), and thus the appropriate folder, /Library/StartupItems isn’t guaranteed to exist on the system by default, but does appear to exist by default on macOS Sierra. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1037.005."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1165" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1037.005 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1165> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1166
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Setuid and Setgid"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "When the setuid or setgid bits are set on Linux or macOS for an application, this means that the application will run with the privileges of the owning user or group respectively. Normally an application is run in the current user’s context, regardless of which user or group owns the application. There are instances where programs need to be executed in an elevated context to function properly, but the user running them doesn’t need the elevated privileges. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1548.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1166" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1548.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1166> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1167
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Securityd Memory"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "In OS X prior to El Capitan, users with root access can read plaintext keychain passwords of logged-in users because Apple’s keychain implementation allows these credentials to be cached so that users are not repeatedly prompted for passwords. Apple’s securityd utility takes the user’s logon password, encrypts it with PBKDF2, and stores this master key in memory. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1555.002."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1167" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1555.002 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1167> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1168
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Local Job Scheduling"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "On Linux and macOS systems, multiple methods are supported for creating pre-scheduled and periodic background jobs: cron, at, and launchd. Unlike Scheduled Task/Job on Windows systems, job scheduling on Linux-based systems cannot be done remotely unless used in conjunction within an established remote session, like secure shell (SSH). Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1053."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1168" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
