attack:T1474
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Supply Chain Compromise"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may manipulate products or product delivery mechanisms prior to receipt by a final consumer for the purpose of data or system compromise."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1474" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0027 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1474> ;
	.

attack:T1474.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1474 ;
	rdfs:label "Supply Chain Compromise: Compromise Software Dependencies and Development Tools"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may manipulate products or product delivery mechanisms prior to receipt by a final consumer for the purpose of data or system compromise. Applications often depend on external software to function properly. Popular open source projects that are used as dependencies in many applications may be targeted as a means to add malicious code to users of the dependency."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1474.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0027 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1474/001> ;
	.

attack:T1474.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1474 ;
	rdfs:label "Supply Chain Compromise: Compromise Hardware Supply Chain"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may manipulate hardware components in products prior to receipt by a final consumer for the purpose of data or system compromise. By modifying hardware or firmware in the supply chain, adversaries can insert a backdoor into consumer networks that may be difficult to detect and give the adversary a high degree of control over the system."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1474.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0027 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1474/002> ;
	.

attack:T1474.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1474 ;
	rdfs:label "Supply Chain Compromise: Compromise Software Supply Chain"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may manipulate application software prior to receipt by a final consumer for the purpose of data or system compromise. Supply chain compromise of software can take place in a number of ways, including manipulation of the application source code, manipulation of the update/distribution mechanism for that software, or replacing compiled releases with a modified version."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1474.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0027 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1474/003> ;
	.

attack:T1475
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Deliver Malicious App via Authorized App Store"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Malicious applications are a common attack vector used by adversaries to gain a presence on mobile devices. Mobile devices often are configured to allow application installation only from an authorized app store (e.g., Google Play Store or Apple App Store). An adversary may seek to place a malicious application in an authorized app store, enabling the application to be installed onto targeted devices. Deprecated by MITRE ATT&CK."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1475" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0027 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1475> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1476
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Deliver Malicious App via Other Means"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Malicious applications are a common attack vector used by adversaries to gain a presence on mobile devices. This technique describes installing a malicious application on targeted mobile devices without involving an authorized app store (e.g., Google Play Store or Apple App Store). Adversaries may wish to avoid placing malicious applications in an authorized app store due to increased potential risk of detection or other reasons. Deprecated by MITRE ATT&CK."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1476" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0027 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1476> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1477
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Exploit via Radio Interfaces"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "The mobile device may be targeted for exploitation through its interface to cellular networks or other radio interfaces. Deprecated by MITRE ATT&CK."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1477" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0027 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-18"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1477> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1478
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Install Insecure or Malicious Configuration"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary could attempt to install insecure or malicious configuration settings on the mobile device, through means such as phishing emails or text messages either directly containing the configuration settings as an attachment, or containing a web link to the configuration settings. The device user may be tricked into installing the configuration settings through social engineering techniques. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1632.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1478" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0027 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1632.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1478> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1480
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Execution Guardrails"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use execution guardrails to constrain execution or actions based on adversary supplied and environment specific conditions that are expected to be present on the target. Guardrails ensure that a payload only executes against an intended target and reduces collateral damage from an adversary’s campaign."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1480" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1480> ;
	.

attack:T1480.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1480 ;
	rdfs:label "Execution Guardrails: Environmental Keying"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may environmentally key payloads or other features of malware to evade defenses and constraint execution to a specific target environment. Environmental keying uses cryptography to constrain execution or actions based on adversary supplied environment specific conditions that are expected to be present on the target."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1480.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1480/001> ;
	.

attack:T1480.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1480 ;
	rdfs:label "Execution Guardrails: Mutual Exclusion"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may constrain execution or actions based on the presence of a mutex associated with malware. A mutex is a locking mechanism used to synchronize access to a resource. Only one thread or process can acquire a mutex at a given time."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1480.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1480/002> ;
	.

attack:T1481
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Web Service"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use an existing, legitimate external Web service as a means for relaying data to/from a compromised system. Popular websites and social media, acting as a mechanism for C2, may give a significant amount of cover. This is due to the likelihood that hosts within a network are already communicating with them prior to a compromise. Using common services, such as those offered by Google or Twitter, makes it easier for adversaries to hide in expected noise."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1481" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0037 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1481> ;
	.

attack:T1481.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1481 ;
	rdfs:label "Web Service: Dead Drop Resolver"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use an existing, legitimate external Web service to host information that points to additional command and control (C2) infrastructure. Adversaries may post content, known as a dead drop resolver, on Web services with embedded (and often obfuscated/encoded) domains or IP addresses. Once infected, victims will reach out to and be redirected by these resolvers."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1481.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0037 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1481/001> ;
	.

attack:T1481.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1481 ;
	rdfs:label "Web Service: Bidirectional Communication"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use an existing, legitimate external Web service channel as a means for sending commands to and receiving output from a compromised system. Compromised systems may leverage popular websites and social media to host command and control (C2) instructions. Those infected systems can then send the output from those commands back over that Web service channel. The return traffic may occur in a variety of ways, depending on the Web service being utilized."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1481.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0037 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1481/002> ;
	.

attack:T1481.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1481 ;
	rdfs:label "Web Service: One-Way Communication"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use an existing, legitimate external Web service channel as a means for sending commands to a compromised system without receiving return output. Compromised systems may leverage popular websites and social media to host command and control (C2) instructions. Those infected systems may opt to send the output from those commands back over a different C2 channel, including to another distinct Web service."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1481.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0037 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1481/003> ;
	.

attack:T1482
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Domain Trust Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to gather information on domain trust relationships that may be used to identify lateral movement opportunities in Windows multi-domain/forest environments. Domain trusts provide a mechanism for a domain to allow access to resources based on the authentication procedures of another domain. Domain trusts allow the users of the trusted domain to access resources in the trusting domain."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1482" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1482> ;
	.

attack:T1483
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Domain Generation Algorithms"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may make use of Domain Generation Algorithms (DGAs) to dynamically identify a destination for command and control traffic rather than relying on a list of static IP addresses or domains. This has the advantage of making it much harder for defenders block, track, or take over the command and control channel, as there potentially could be thousands of domains that malware can check for instructions. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1568.002."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1483" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1568.002 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1483> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1484
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Domain or Tenant Policy Modification"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify the configuration settings of a domain or identity tenant to evade defenses and/or escalate privileges in centrally managed environments. Such services provide a centralized means of managing identity resources such as devices and accounts, and often include configuration settings that may apply between domains or tenants such as trust relationships, identity syncing, or identity federation."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1484" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1484> ;
	.

attack:T1484.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1484 ;
	rdfs:label "Domain or Tenant Policy Modification: Group Policy Modification"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify Group Policy Objects (GPOs) to subvert the intended discretionary access controls for a domain, usually with the intention of escalating privileges on the domain. Group policy allows for centralized management of user and computer settings in Active Directory (AD). GPOs are containers for group policy settings made up of files stored within a predictable network path `\\\\SYSVOL\\\\Policies\\`."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1484.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1484/001> ;
	.

attack:T1484.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1484 ;
	rdfs:label "Domain or Tenant Policy Modification: Trust Modification"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may add new domain trusts, modify the properties of existing domain trusts, or otherwise change the configuration of trust relationships between domains and tenants to evade defenses and/or elevate privileges.Trust details, such as whether or not user identities are federated, allow authentication and authorization properties to apply between domains or tenants for the purpose of accessing shared resources."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1484.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1484/002> ;
	.

attack:T1485
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Data Destruction"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may destroy data and files on specific systems or in large numbers on a network to interrupt availability to systems, services, and network resources. Data destruction is likely to render stored data irrecoverable by forensic techniques through overwriting files or data on local and remote drives."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1485" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0040 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1485> ;
	.

attack:T1485.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1485 ;
	rdfs:label "Data Destruction: Lifecycle-Triggered Deletion"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify the lifecycle policies of a cloud storage bucket to destroy all objects stored within."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1485.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0040 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1485/001> ;
	.

attack:T1486
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Data Encrypted for Impact"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may encrypt data on target systems or on large numbers of systems in a network to interrupt availability to system and network resources. They can attempt to render stored data inaccessible by encrypting files or data on local and remote drives and withholding access to a decryption key."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1486" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0040 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
