	.

attack:T1497
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may employ various means to detect and avoid virtualization and analysis environments. This may include changing behaviors based on the results of checks for the presence of artifacts indicative of a virtual machine environment (VME) or sandbox. If the adversary detects a VME, they may alter their malware to disengage from the victim or conceal the core functions of the implant. They may also search for VME artifacts before dropping secondary or additional payloads."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1497" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1497> ;
	.

attack:T1497.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1497 ;
	rdfs:label "Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion: System Checks"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may employ various system checks to detect and avoid virtualization and analysis environments. This may include changing behaviors based on the results of checks for the presence of artifacts indicative of a virtual machine environment (VME) or sandbox. If the adversary detects a VME, they may alter their malware to disengage from the victim or conceal the core functions of the implant. They may also search for VME artifacts before dropping secondary or additional payloads."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1497.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1497/001> ;
	.

attack:T1497.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1497 ;
	rdfs:label "Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion: User Activity Based Checks"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may employ various user activity checks to detect and avoid virtualization and analysis environments. This may include changing behaviors based on the results of checks for the presence of artifacts indicative of a virtual machine environment (VME) or sandbox. If the adversary detects a VME, they may alter their malware to disengage from the victim or conceal the core functions of the implant. They may also search for VME artifacts before dropping secondary or additional payloads."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1497.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1497/002> ;
	.

attack:T1497.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1497 ;
	rdfs:label "Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion: Time Based Checks"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may employ various time-based methods to detect virtualization and analysis environments, particularly those that attempt to manipulate time mechanisms to simulate longer elapses of time. This may include enumerating time-based properties, such as uptime or the system clock."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1497.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1497/003> ;
	.

attack:T1498
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Network Denial of Service"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may perform Network Denial of Service (DoS) attacks to degrade or block the availability of targeted resources to users. Network DoS can be performed by exhausting the network bandwidth services rely on. Example resources include specific websites, email services, DNS, and web-based applications. Adversaries have been observed conducting network DoS attacks for political purposes and to support other malicious activities, including distraction, hacktivism, and extortion."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1498" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0040 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1498> ;
	.

attack:T1498.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1498 ;
	rdfs:label "Network Denial of Service: Direct Network Flood"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to cause a denial of service (DoS) by directly sending a high-volume of network traffic to a target. This DoS attack may also reduce the availability and functionality of the targeted system(s) and network. Direct Network Floods are when one or more systems are used to send a high-volume of network packets towards the targeted service's network. Almost any network protocol may be used for flooding."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1498.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0040 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1498/001> ;
	.

attack:T1498.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1498 ;
	rdfs:label "Network Denial of Service: Reflection Amplification"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to cause a denial of service (DoS) by reflecting a high-volume of network traffic to a target. This type of Network DoS takes advantage of a third-party server intermediary that hosts and will respond to a given spoofed source IP address. This third-party server is commonly termed a reflector. An adversary accomplishes a reflection attack by sending packets to reflectors with the spoofed address of the victim."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1498.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0040 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1498/002> ;
	.

attack:T1499
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Endpoint Denial of Service"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may perform Endpoint Denial of Service (DoS) attacks to degrade or block the availability of services to users. Endpoint DoS can be performed by exhausting the system resources those services are hosted on or exploiting the system to cause a persistent crash condition. Example services include websites, email services, DNS, and web-based applications."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1499" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0040 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1499> ;
	.

attack:T1499.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1499 ;
	rdfs:label "Endpoint Denial of Service: OS Exhaustion Flood"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may launch a denial of service (DoS) attack targeting an endpoint's operating system (OS). A system's OS is responsible for managing the finite resources as well as preventing the entire system from being overwhelmed by excessive demands on its capacity. These attacks do not need to exhaust the actual resources on a system; the attacks may simply exhaust the limits and available resources that an OS self-imposes."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1499.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0040 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1499/001> ;
	.

attack:T1499.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1499 ;
	rdfs:label "Endpoint Denial of Service: Service Exhaustion Flood"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may target the different network services provided by systems to conduct a denial of service (DoS). Adversaries often target the availability of DNS and web services, however others have been targeted as well. Web server software can be attacked through a variety of means, some of which apply generally while others are specific to the software being used to provide the service."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1499.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0040 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1499/002> ;
	.

attack:T1499.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1499 ;
	rdfs:label "Endpoint Denial of Service: Application Exhaustion Flood"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may target resource intensive features of applications to cause a denial of service (DoS), denying availability to those applications. For example, specific features in web applications may be highly resource intensive. Repeated requests to those features may be able to exhaust system resources and deny access to the application or the server itself."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1499.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0040 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1499/003> ;
	.

attack:T1499.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1499 ;
	rdfs:label "Endpoint Denial of Service: Application or System Exploitation"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may exploit software vulnerabilities that can cause an application or system to crash and deny availability to users. Some systems may automatically restart critical applications and services when crashes occur, but they can likely be re-exploited to cause a persistent denial of service (DoS) condition."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1499.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0040 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1499/004> ;
	.

attack:T1500
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Compile After Delivery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to make payloads difficult to discover and analyze by delivering files to victims as uncompiled code. Similar to Obfuscated Files or Information, text-based source code files may subvert analysis and scrutiny from protections targeting executables/binaries. These payloads will need to be compiled before execution; typically via native utilities such as csc.exe or GCC/MinGW. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1027.004."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1500" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1027.004 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1500> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1501
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Systemd Service"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Systemd services can be used to establish persistence on a Linux system. The systemd service manager is commonly used for managing background daemon processes (also known as services) and other system resources. Systemd is the default initialization (init) system on many Linux distributions starting with Debian 8, Ubuntu 15.04, CentOS 7, RHEL 7, Fedora 15, and replaces legacy init systems including SysVinit and Upstart while remaining backwards compatible with the aforementioned init systems. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1543.002."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1501" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1543.002 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1501> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1502
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Parent PID Spoofing"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may spoof the parent process identifier (PPID) of a new process to evade process-monitoring defenses or to elevate privileges. New processes are typically spawned directly from their parent, or calling, process unless explicitly specified. One way of explicitly assigning the PPID of a new process is via the CreateProcess API call, which supports a parameter that defines the PPID to use. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1134.004."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1502" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1134.004 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1502> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1503
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Credentials from Web Browsers"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may acquire credentials from web browsers by reading files specific to the target browser. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1555.003."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1503" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1555.003 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1503> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1504
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "PowerShell Profile"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gain persistence and elevate privileges in certain situations by abusing PowerShell profiles. A PowerShell profile (profile.ps1) is a script that runs when PowerShell starts and can be used as a logon script to customize user environments. PowerShell supports several profiles depending on the user or host program. For example, there can be different profiles for PowerShell host programs such as the PowerShell console, PowerShell ISE or Visual Studio Code. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1546.013."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1504" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1546.013 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1504> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1505
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Server Software Component"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse legitimate extensible development features of servers to establish persistent access to systems. Enterprise server applications may include features that allow developers to write and install software or scripts to extend the functionality of the main application. Adversaries may install malicious components to extend and abuse server applications."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1505" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1505> ;
	.

attack:T1505.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1505 ;
	rdfs:label "Server Software Component: SQL Stored Procedures"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse SQL stored procedures to establish persistent access to systems. SQL Stored Procedures are code that can be saved and reused so that database users do not waste time rewriting frequently used SQL queries. Stored procedures can be invoked via SQL statements to the database using the procedure name or via defined events (e.g. when a SQL server application is started/restarted)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1505.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1505/001> ;
	.

attack:T1505.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1505 ;
	rdfs:label "Server Software Component: Transport Agent"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse Microsoft transport agents to establish persistent access to systems. Microsoft Exchange transport agents can operate on email messages passing through the transport pipeline to perform various tasks such as filtering spam, filtering malicious attachments, journaling, or adding a corporate signature to the end of all outgoing emails."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1505.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1505/002> ;
	.

attack:T1505.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1505 ;
	rdfs:label "Server Software Component: Web Shell"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may backdoor web servers with web shells to establish persistent access to systems. A Web shell is a Web script that is placed on an openly accessible Web server to allow an adversary to access the Web server as a gateway into a network. A Web shell may provide a set of functions to execute or a command-line interface on the system that hosts the Web server."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1505.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1505/003> ;
	.

attack:T1505.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1505 ;
	rdfs:label "Server Software Component: IIS Components"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may install malicious components that run on Internet Information Services (IIS) web servers to establish persistence. IIS provides several mechanisms to extend the functionality of the web servers. For example, Internet Server Application Programming Interface (ISAPI) extensions and filters can be installed to examine and/or modify incoming and outgoing IIS web requests."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1505.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1505/004> ;
	.

attack:T1505.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1505 ;
	rdfs:label "Server Software Component: Terminal Services DLL"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse components of Terminal Services to enable persistent access to systems. Microsoft Terminal Services, renamed to Remote Desktop Services in some Windows Server OSs as of 2022, enable remote terminal connections to hosts. Terminal Services allows servers to transmit a full, interactive, graphical user interface to clients via RDP."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1505.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1505/005> ;
	.

attack:T1505.006
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1505 ;
	rdfs:label "Server Software Component: vSphere Installation Bundles"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse vSphere Installation Bundles (VIBs) to establish persistent access to ESXi hypervisors. VIBs are collections of files used for software distribution and virtual system management in VMware environments. Since ESXi uses an in-memory filesystem where changes made to most files are stored in RAM rather than in persistent storage, these modifications are lost after a reboot."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1505.006" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1505/006> ;
	.

attack:T1506
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Web Session Cookie"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries can use stolen session cookies to authenticate to web applications and services. This technique bypasses some multi-factor authentication protocols since the session is already authenticated. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1550.004."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1506" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0008 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office 365" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1550.004 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1506> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1507
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Network Information Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use device sensors to collect information about nearby networks, such as Wi-Fi and Bluetooth. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1421."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1507" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0035 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1421 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-18"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1507> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1508
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Suppress Application Icon"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "A malicious application could suppress its icon from being displayed to the user in the application launcher to hide the fact that it is installed, and to make it more difficult for the user to uninstall the application. Hiding the application's icon programmatically does not require any special permissions. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1628.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1508" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1628.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1508> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1509
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Non-Standard Port"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may generate network traffic using a protocol and port pairing that are typically not associated. For example, HTTPS over port 8088 or port 587 as opposed to the traditional port 443. Adversaries may make changes to the standard port used by a protocol to bypass filtering or muddle analysis/parsing of network data."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1509" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0037 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1509> ;
	.

attack:T1510
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Clipboard Modification"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse clipboard functionality to intercept and replace information in the Android device clipboard. Malicious applications may monitor the clipboard activity through the ClipboardManager.OnPrimaryClipChangedListener interface on Android to determine when the clipboard contents have changed. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1641.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1510" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0034 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1641.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1510> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1512
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Video Capture"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary can leverage a device’s cameras to gather information by capturing video recordings. Images may also be captured, potentially in specified intervals, in lieu of video files."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1512" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0035 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1512> ;
	.

attack:T1513
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Screen Capture"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use screen capture to collect additional information about a target device, such as applications running in the foreground, user data, credentials, or other sensitive information. Applications running in the background can capture screenshots or videos of another application running in the foreground by using the Android `MediaProjectionManager` (generally requires the device user to grant consent)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1513" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0035 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1513> ;
	.

attack:T1514
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Elevated Execution with Prompt"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may leverage the AuthorizationExecuteWithPrivileges API to escalate privileges by prompting the user for credentials. The purpose of this API is to give application developers an easy way to perform operations with root privileges, such as for application installation or updating. This API does not validate that the program requesting root privileges comes from a reputable source or has been maliciously modified. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1548.004."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1514" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1548.004 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1514> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1516
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Input Injection"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "A malicious application can inject input to the user interface to mimic user interaction through the abuse of Android's accessibility APIs."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1516" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0034 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1516> ;
	.

attack:T1517
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Access Notifications"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may collect data within notifications sent by the operating system or other applications. Notifications may contain sensitive data such as one-time authentication codes sent over SMS, email, or other mediums. In the case of Credential Access, adversaries may attempt to intercept one-time code sent to the device. Adversaries can also dismiss notifications to prevent the user from noticing that the notification has arrived and can trigger action buttons contained within notifications."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1517" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0035 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0031 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1517> ;
	.

attack:T1518
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Software Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of software and software versions that are installed on a system or in a cloud environment. Adversaries may use the information from Software Discovery during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not the adversary fully infects the target and/or attempts specific actions."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1518" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1518> ;
	.

attack:T1518.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1518 ;
	rdfs:label "Software Discovery: Security Software Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of security software, configurations, defensive tools, and sensors that are installed on a system or in a cloud environment. This may include things such as cloud monitoring agents and anti-virus. Adversaries may use the information from Security Software Discovery during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not the adversary fully infects the target and/or attempts specific actions."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1518.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1518/001> ;
	.

attack:T1518.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1518 ;
	rdfs:label "Software Discovery: Backup Software Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of backup software or configurations that are installed on a system. Adversaries may use this information to shape follow-on behaviors, such as Data Destruction, Inhibit System Recovery, or Data Encrypted for Impact."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1518.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-22"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1518/002> ;
	.

attack:T1519
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Emond"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use Event Monitor Daemon (emond) to establish persistence by scheduling malicious commands to run on predictable event triggers. Emond is a Launch Daemon that accepts events from various services, runs them through a simple rules engine, and takes action. The emond binary at /sbin/emond will load any rules from the /etc/emond.d/rules/ directory and take action once an explicitly defined event takes place. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1546.014."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1519" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1546.014 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1519> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1520
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Domain Generation Algorithms"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use Domain Generation Algorithms (DGAs) to procedurally generate domain names for command and control communication, and other uses such as malicious application distribution. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1637.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1520" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0037 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1637.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1520> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1521
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Encrypted Channel"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may explicitly employ a known encryption algorithm to conceal command and control traffic rather than relying on any inherent protections provided by a communication protocol. Despite the use of a secure algorithm, these implementations may be vulnerable to reverse engineering if necessary secret keys are encoded and/or generated within malware samples/configuration files."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1521" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0037 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1521> ;
	.

attack:T1521.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1521 ;
	rdfs:label "Encrypted Channel: Symmetric Cryptography"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may employ a known symmetric encryption algorithm to conceal command and control traffic, rather than relying on any inherent protections provided by a communication protocol. Symmetric encryption algorithms use the same key for plaintext encryption and ciphertext decryption. Common symmetric encryption algorithms include AES, Blowfish, and RC4."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1521.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0037 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1521/001> ;
	.

attack:T1521.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1521 ;
	rdfs:label "Encrypted Channel: Asymmetric Cryptography"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may employ a known asymmetric encryption algorithm to conceal command and control traffic, rather than relying on any inherent protections provided by a communication protocol. Asymmetric cryptography, also known as public key cryptography, uses a keypair per party: one public that can be freely distributed, and one private that should not be distributed."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1521.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0037 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1521/002> ;
	.

attack:T1521.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1521 ;
	rdfs:label "Encrypted Channel: SSL Pinning"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use SSL Pinning to protect the C2 traffic from being intercepted and analyzed."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1521.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0037 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2024-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1521/003> ;
	.

attack:T1522
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Cloud Instance Metadata API"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to access the Cloud Instance Metadata API to collect credentials and other sensitive data. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1552.005."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1522" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1552.005 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1522> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1523
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Evade Analysis Environment"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Malicious applications may attempt to detect their operating environment prior to fully executing their payloads. These checks are often used to ensure the application is not running within an analysis environment such as a sandbox used for application vetting, security research, or reverse engineering. Adversaries may use many different checks such as physical sensors, location, and system properties to fingerprint emulators and sandbox environments. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1633.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1523" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0032 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1633.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1523> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1525
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Implant Internal Image"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may implant cloud or container images with malicious code to establish persistence after gaining access to an environment. Amazon Web Services (AWS) Amazon Machine Images (AMIs), Google Cloud Platform (GCP) Images, and Azure Images as well as popular container runtimes such as Docker can be implanted or backdoored. Unlike Upload Malware, this technique focuses on adversaries implanting an image in a registry within a victim’s environment."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1525" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1525> ;
	.

attack:T1526
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Cloud Service Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may attempt to enumerate the cloud services running on a system after gaining access. These methods can differ from platform-as-a-service (PaaS), to infrastructure-as-a-service (IaaS), or software-as-a-service (SaaS). Many services exist throughout the various cloud providers and can include Continuous Integration and Continuous Delivery (CI/CD), Lambda Functions, Entra ID, etc."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1526" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1526> ;
	.

attack:T1527
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Application Access Token"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use application access tokens to bypass the typical authentication process and access restricted accounts, information, or services on remote systems. These tokens are typically stolen from users and used in lieu of login credentials. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1550.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1527" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0008 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office 365" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1550.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1527> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1528
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Steal Application Access Token"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries can steal application access tokens as a means of acquiring credentials to access remote systems and resources."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1528" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1528> ;
	.

attack:T1529
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "System Shutdown/Reboot"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may shutdown/reboot systems to interrupt access to, or aid in the destruction of, those systems. Operating systems may contain commands to initiate a shutdown/reboot of a machine or network device. In some cases, these commands may also be used to initiate a shutdown/reboot of a remote computer or network device via Network Device CLI (e.g. reload). They may also include shutdown/reboot of a virtual machine via hypervisor / cloud consoles or command line tools."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1529" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0040 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1529> ;
	.

attack:T1530
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Data from Cloud Storage"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may access data from cloud storage."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1530" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0009 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1530> ;
	.

attack:T1531
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Account Access Removal"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may interrupt availability of system and network resources by inhibiting access to accounts utilized by legitimate users. Accounts may be deleted, locked, or manipulated (ex: changed credentials, revoked permissions for SaaS platforms such as Sharepoint) to remove access to accounts. Adversaries may also subsequently log off and/or perform a System Shutdown/Reboot to set malicious changes into place."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1531" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0040 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1531> ;
	.

attack:T1532
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Archive Collected Data"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may compress and/or encrypt data that is collected prior to exfiltration. Compressing data can help to obfuscate its contents and minimize use of network resources. Encryption can be used to hide information that is being exfiltrated from detection or make exfiltration less conspicuous upon inspection by a defender."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1532" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0035 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1532> ;
	.

attack:T1533
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Data from Local System"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may search local system sources, such as file systems or local databases, to find files of interest and sensitive data prior to exfiltration."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1533" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0035 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1533> ;
	.

attack:T1534
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Internal Spearphishing"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "After they already have access to accounts or systems within the environment, adversaries may use internal spearphishing to gain access to additional information or compromise other users within the same organization. Internal spearphishing is multi-staged campaign where a legitimate account is initially compromised either by controlling the user's device or by compromising the account credentials of the user."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1534" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0008 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1534> ;
	.

attack:T1535
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Unused/Unsupported Cloud Regions"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may create cloud instances in unused geographic service regions in order to evade detection. Access is usually obtained through compromising accounts used to manage cloud infrastructure."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1535" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1535> ;
	.

attack:T1536
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Revert Cloud Instance"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may revert changes made to a cloud instance after they have performed malicious activities in attempt to evade detection and remove evidence of their presence. In highly virtualized environments, such as cloud-based infrastructure, this may be accomplished by restoring virtual machine (VM) or data storage snapshots through the cloud management dashboard or cloud APIs. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1578.004."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1536" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1578.004 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1536> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1537
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Transfer Data to Cloud Account"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may exfiltrate data by transferring the data, including through sharing/syncing and creating backups of cloud environments, to another cloud account they control on the same service."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1537" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0010 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1537> ;
	.

attack:T1538
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Cloud Service Dashboard"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may use a cloud service dashboard GUI with stolen credentials to gain useful information from an operational cloud environment, such as specific services, resources, and features. For example, the GCP Command Center can be used to view all assets, review findings of potential security risks, and run additional queries, such as finding public IP addresses and open ports."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1538" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1538> ;
	.

attack:T1539
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Steal Web Session Cookie"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may steal web application or service session cookies and use them to gain access to web applications or Internet services as an authenticated user without needing credentials. Web applications and services often use session cookies as an authentication token after a user has authenticated to a website."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1539" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1539> ;
	.

attack:T1540
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Code Injection"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use code injection attacks to implant arbitrary code into the address space of a running application. Code is then executed or interpreted by that application. Adversaries utilizing this technique may exploit capabilities to load code in at runtime through dynamic libraries. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1631.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1540" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0028 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0029 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1631.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1540> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1541
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Foreground Persistence"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse Android's `startForeground()` API method to maintain continuous sensor access. Beginning in Android 9, idle applications running in the background no longer have access to device sensors, such as the camera, microphone, and gyroscope. Applications can retain sensor access by running in the foreground, using Android’s `startForeground()` API method. This informs the system that the user is actively interacting with the application, and it should not be killed."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1541" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0028 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1541> ;
	.

attack:T1542
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Pre-OS Boot"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse Pre-OS Boot mechanisms as a way to establish persistence on a system. During the booting process of a computer, firmware and various startup services are loaded before the operating system. These programs control flow of execution before the operating system takes control."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1542" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1542> ;
	.

attack:T1542.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1542 ;
	rdfs:label "Pre-OS Boot: System Firmware"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify system firmware to persist on systems.The BIOS (Basic Input/Output System) and The Unified Extensible Firmware Interface (UEFI) or Extensible Firmware Interface (EFI) are examples of system firmware that operate as the software interface between the operating system and hardware of a computer."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1542.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1542/001> ;
	.

attack:T1542.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1542 ;
	rdfs:label "Pre-OS Boot: Component Firmware"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify component firmware to persist on systems. Some adversaries may employ sophisticated means to compromise computer components and install malicious firmware that will execute adversary code outside of the operating system and main system firmware or BIOS. This technique may be similar to System Firmware but conducted upon other system components/devices that may not have the same capability or level of integrity checking."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1542.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1542/002> ;
	.

attack:T1542.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1542 ;
	rdfs:label "Pre-OS Boot: Bootkit"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use bootkits to persist on systems. A bootkit is a malware variant that modifies the boot sectors of a hard drive, allowing malicious code to execute before a computer's operating system has loaded. Bootkits reside at a layer below the operating system and may make it difficult to perform full remediation unless an organization suspects one was used and can act accordingly."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1542.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1542/003> ;
	.

attack:T1542.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1542 ;
	rdfs:label "Pre-OS Boot: ROMMONkit"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse the ROM Monitor (ROMMON) by loading an unauthorized firmware with adversary code to provide persistent access and manipulate device behavior that is difficult to detect."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1542.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1542/004> ;
	.

attack:T1542.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1542 ;
	rdfs:label "Pre-OS Boot: TFTP Boot"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse netbooting to load an unauthorized network device operating system from a Trivial File Transfer Protocol (TFTP) server. TFTP boot (netbooting) is commonly used by network administrators to load configuration-controlled network device images from a centralized management server. Netbooting is one option in the boot sequence and can be used to centralize, manage, and control device images."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1542.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1542/005> ;
	.

attack:T1543
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Create or Modify System Process"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may create or modify system-level processes to repeatedly execute malicious payloads as part of persistence. When operating systems boot up, they can start processes that perform background system functions. On Windows and Linux, these system processes are referred to as services. On macOS, launchd processes known as Launch Daemon and Launch Agent are run to finish system initialization and load user specific parameters."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1543" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543> ;
	.

attack:T1543.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1543 ;
	rdfs:label "Create or Modify System Process: Launch Agent"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may create or modify launch agents to repeatedly execute malicious payloads as part of persistence. When a user logs in, a per-user launchd process is started which loads the parameters for each launch-on-demand user agent from the property list (.plist) file found in /System/Library/LaunchAgents, /Library/LaunchAgents, and ~/Library/LaunchAgents."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1543.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543/001> ;
	.

attack:T1543.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1543 ;
	rdfs:label "Create or Modify System Process: Systemd Service"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may create or modify systemd services to repeatedly execute malicious payloads as part of persistence. Systemd is a system and service manager commonly used for managing background daemon processes (also known as services) and other system resources. Systemd is the default initialization (init) system on many Linux distributions replacing legacy init systems, including SysVinit and Upstart, while remaining backwards compatible."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1543.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543/002> ;
	.

attack:T1543.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1543 ;
	rdfs:label "Create or Modify System Process: Windows Service"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may create or modify Windows services to repeatedly execute malicious payloads as part of persistence. When Windows boots up, it starts programs or applications called services that perform background system functions. Windows service configuration information, including the file path to the service's executable or recovery programs/commands, is stored in the Windows Registry."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1543.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543/003> ;
	.

attack:T1543.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1543 ;
	rdfs:label "Create or Modify System Process: Launch Daemon"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may create or modify Launch Daemons to execute malicious payloads as part of persistence. Launch Daemons are plist files used to interact with Launchd, the service management framework used by macOS. Launch Daemons require elevated privileges to install, are executed for every user on a system prior to login, and run in the background without the need for user interaction."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1543.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543/004> ;
	.

attack:T1543.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1543 ;
	rdfs:label "Create or Modify System Process: Container Service"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may create or modify container or container cluster management tools that run as daemons, agents, or services on individual hosts. These include software for creating and managing individual containers, such as Docker and Podman, as well as container cluster node-level agents such as kubelet. By modifying these services, an adversary may be able to achieve persistence or escalate their privileges on a host."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1543.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543/005> ;
	.

attack:T1544
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Ingress Tool Transfer"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may transfer tools or other files from an external system onto a compromised device to facilitate follow-on actions. Files may be copied from an external adversary-controlled system through the command and control channel or through alternate protocols with another tool such as FTP."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1544" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0037 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1544> ;
	.

attack:T1546
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Event Triggered Execution"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may establish persistence and/or elevate privileges using system mechanisms that trigger execution based on specific events. Various operating systems have means to monitor and subscribe to events such as logons or other user activity such as running specific applications/binaries. Cloud environments may also support various functions and services that monitor and can be invoked in response to specific cloud events."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1546" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-08-04"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1546> ;
	.

attack:T1546.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1546 ;
	rdfs:label "Event Triggered Execution: Change Default File Association"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may establish persistence by executing malicious content triggered by a file type association. When a file is opened, the default program used to open the file (also called the file association or handler) is checked. File association selections are stored in the Windows Registry and can be edited by users, administrators, or programs that have Registry access or by administrators using the built-in assoc utility."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1546.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1546/001> ;
	.

attack:T1546.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1546 ;
	rdfs:label "Event Triggered Execution: Screensaver"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may establish persistence by executing malicious content triggered by user inactivity. Screensavers are programs that execute after a configurable time of user inactivity and consist of Portable Executable (PE) files with a .scr file extension. The Windows screensaver application scrnsave.scr is located in C:\\Windows\\System32\\, and C:\\Windows\\sysWOW64\\ on 64-bit Windows systems, along with screensavers included with base Windows installations."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1546.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1546/002> ;
	.

attack:T1546.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1546 ;
	rdfs:label "Event Triggered Execution: Windows Management Instrumentation Event Subscription"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may establish persistence and elevate privileges by executing malicious content triggered by a Windows Management Instrumentation (WMI) event subscription. WMI can be used to install event filters, providers, consumers, and bindings that execute code when a defined event occurs. Examples of events that may be subscribed to are the wall clock time, user login, or the computer's uptime."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1546.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1546/003> ;
	.

attack:T1546.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1546 ;
	rdfs:label "Event Triggered Execution: Unix Shell Configuration Modification"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may establish persistence through executing malicious commands triggered by a user’s shell. User Unix Shells execute several configuration scripts at different points throughout the session based on events. For example, when a user opens a command-line interface or remotely logs in (such as via SSH) a login shell is initiated. The login shell executes scripts from the system (/etc) and the user’s home directory (~/) to configure the environment."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1546.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1546/004> ;
	.

attack:T1546.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1546 ;
	rdfs:label "Event Triggered Execution: Trap"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may establish persistence by executing malicious content triggered by an interrupt signal. The trap command allows programs and shells to specify commands that will be executed upon receiving interrupt signals. A common situation is a script allowing for graceful termination and handling of common keyboard interrupts like ctrl+c and ctrl+d."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1546.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1546/005> ;
	.

attack:T1546.006
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1546 ;
	rdfs:label "Event Triggered Execution: LC_LOAD_DYLIB Addition"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may establish persistence by executing malicious content triggered by the execution of tainted binaries. Mach-O binaries have a series of headers that are used to perform certain operations when a binary is loaded. The LC_LOAD_DYLIB header in a Mach-O binary tells macOS and OS X which dynamic libraries (dylibs) to load during execution time. These can be added ad-hoc to the compiled binary as long as adjustments are made to the rest of the fields and dependencies."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1546.006" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1546/006> ;
	.

attack:T1546.007
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1546 ;
	rdfs:label "Event Triggered Execution: Netsh Helper DLL"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may establish persistence by executing malicious content triggered by Netsh Helper DLLs. Netsh.exe (also referred to as Netshell) is a command-line scripting utility used to interact with the network configuration of a system. It contains functionality to add helper DLLs for extending functionality of the utility. The paths to registered netsh.exe helper DLLs are entered into the Windows Registry at HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Netsh."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1546.007" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1546/007> ;
	.

attack:T1546.008
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1546 ;
	rdfs:label "Event Triggered Execution: Accessibility Features"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may establish persistence and/or elevate privileges by executing malicious content triggered by accessibility features. Windows contains accessibility features that may be launched with a key combination before a user has logged in (ex: when the user is on the Windows logon screen). An adversary can modify the way these programs are launched to get a command prompt or backdoor without logging in to the system."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1546.008" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1546/008> ;
	.

attack:T1546.009
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1546 ;
	rdfs:label "Event Triggered Execution: AppCert DLLs"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may establish persistence and/or elevate privileges by executing malicious content triggered by AppCert DLLs loaded into processes. Dynamic-link libraries (DLLs) that are specified in the AppCertDLLs Registry key under HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\System\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Session Manager\\ are loaded into every process that calls the ubiquitously used application programming interface (API) functions CreateProcess, CreateProcessAsUser, CreateProcessWithLoginW, CreateProcessWithTokenW,…"@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1546.009" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1546/009> ;
	.

attack:T1546.010
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1546 ;
	rdfs:label "Event Triggered Execution: AppInit DLLs"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may establish persistence and/or elevate privileges by executing malicious content triggered by AppInit DLLs loaded into processes. Dynamic-link libraries (DLLs) that are specified in the AppInit_DLLs value in the Registry keys HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Windows or HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\Software\\Wow6432Node\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Windows are loaded by user32.dll into every process that loads user32.dll."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1546.010" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1546/010> ;
	.

attack:T1546.011
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1546 ;
	rdfs:label "Event Triggered Execution: Application Shimming"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may establish persistence and/or elevate privileges by executing malicious content triggered by application shims. The Microsoft Windows Application Compatibility Infrastructure/Framework (Application Shim) was created to allow for backward compatibility of software as the operating system codebase changes over time."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1546.011" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1546/011> ;
	.

attack:T1546.012
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1546 ;
	rdfs:label "Event Triggered Execution: Image File Execution Options Injection"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may establish persistence and/or elevate privileges by executing malicious content triggered by Image File Execution Options (IFEO) debuggers. IFEOs enable a developer to attach a debugger to an application. When a process is created, a debugger present in an application’s IFEO will be prepended to the application’s name, effectively launching the new process under the debugger (e.g., C:\\dbg\\ntsd.exe -g notepad.exe)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1546.012" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1546/012> ;
	.

attack:T1546.013
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1546 ;
	rdfs:label "Event Triggered Execution: PowerShell Profile"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gain persistence and elevate privileges by executing malicious content triggered by PowerShell profiles. A PowerShell profile (profile.ps1) is a script that runs when PowerShell starts and can be used as a logon script to customize user environments."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1546.013" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1546/013> ;
	.

attack:T1546.014
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1546 ;
	rdfs:label "Event Triggered Execution: Emond"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gain persistence and elevate privileges by executing malicious content triggered by the Event Monitor Daemon (emond). Emond is a Launch Daemon that accepts events from various services, runs them through a simple rules engine, and takes action. The emond binary at /sbin/emond will load any rules from the /etc/emond.d/rules/ directory and take action once an explicitly defined event takes place."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1546.014" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1546/014> ;
	.

attack:T1546.015
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1546 ;
	rdfs:label "Event Triggered Execution: Component Object Model Hijacking"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may establish persistence by executing malicious content triggered by hijacked references to Component Object Model (COM) objects. COM is a system within Windows to enable interaction between software components through the operating system. References to various COM objects are stored in the Registry."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1546.015" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1546/015> ;
	.

attack:T1546.016
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1546 ;
	rdfs:label "Event Triggered Execution: Installer Packages"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may establish persistence and elevate privileges by using an installer to trigger the execution of malicious content. Installer packages are OS specific and contain the resources an operating system needs to install applications on a system. Installer packages can include scripts that run prior to installation as well as after installation is complete. Installer scripts may inherit elevated permissions when executed."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1546.016" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1546/016> ;
	.

attack:T1546.017
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1546 ;
	rdfs:label "Event Triggered Execution: Udev Rules"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may maintain persistence through executing malicious content triggered using udev rules. Udev is the Linux kernel device manager that dynamically manages device nodes, handles access to pseudo-device files in the `/dev` directory, and responds to hardware events, such as when external devices like hard drives or keyboards are plugged in or removed."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1546.017" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1546/017> ;
	.

attack:T1546.018
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1546 ;
	rdfs:label "Event Triggered Execution: Python Startup Hooks"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may achieve persistence by leveraging Python’s startup mechanisms, including path configuration (`.pth`) files and the `sitecustomize.py` or `usercustomize.py` modules. These files are automatically processed during the initialization of the Python interpreter, allowing for the execution of arbitrary code whenever Python is invoked."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1546.018" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-21"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1546/018> ;
	.

attack:T1547
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Boot or Logon Autostart Execution"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may configure system settings to automatically execute a program during system boot or logon to maintain persistence or gain higher-level privileges on compromised systems. Operating systems may have mechanisms for automatically running a program on system boot or account logon."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1547" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1547> ;
	.

attack:T1547.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1547 ;
	rdfs:label "Boot or Logon Autostart Execution: Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may achieve persistence by adding a program to a startup folder or referencing it with a Registry run key. Adding an entry to the \"run keys\" in the Registry or startup folder will cause the program referenced to be executed when a user logs in. These programs will be executed under the context of the user and will have the account's associated permissions level."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1547.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1547/001> ;
	.

attack:T1547.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1547 ;
	rdfs:label "Boot or Logon Autostart Execution: Authentication Package"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse authentication packages to execute DLLs when the system boots. Windows authentication package DLLs are loaded by the Local Security Authority (LSA) process at system start. They provide support for multiple logon processes and multiple security protocols to the operating system."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1547.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1547/002> ;
	.

attack:T1547.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1547 ;
	rdfs:label "Boot or Logon Autostart Execution: Time Providers"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse time providers to execute DLLs when the system boots. The Windows Time service (W32Time) enables time synchronization across and within domains. W32Time time providers are responsible for retrieving time stamps from hardware/network resources and outputting these values to other network clients."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1547.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1547/003> ;
	.

attack:T1547.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1547 ;
	rdfs:label "Boot or Logon Autostart Execution: Winlogon Helper DLL"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse features of Winlogon to execute DLLs and/or executables when a user logs in. Winlogon.exe is a Windows component responsible for actions at logon/logoff as well as the secure attention sequence (SAS) triggered by Ctrl-Alt-Delete."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1547.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1547/004> ;
	.

attack:T1547.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1547 ;
	rdfs:label "Boot or Logon Autostart Execution: Security Support Provider"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse security support providers (SSPs) to execute DLLs when the system boots. Windows SSP DLLs are loaded into the Local Security Authority (LSA) process at system start. Once loaded into the LSA, SSP DLLs have access to encrypted and plaintext passwords that are stored in Windows, such as any logged-on user's Domain password or smart card PINs."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1547.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1547/005> ;
	.

attack:T1547.006
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1547 ;
	rdfs:label "Boot or Logon Autostart Execution: Kernel Modules and Extensions"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify the kernel to automatically execute programs on system boot. Loadable Kernel Modules (LKMs) are pieces of code that can be loaded and unloaded into the kernel upon demand. They extend the functionality of the kernel without the need to reboot the system. For example, one type of module is the device driver, which allows the kernel to access hardware connected to the system."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1547.006" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1547/006> ;
	.

attack:T1547.007
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1547 ;
	rdfs:label "Boot or Logon Autostart Execution: Re-opened Applications"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify plist files to automatically run an application when a user logs in. When a user logs out or restarts via the macOS Graphical User Interface (GUI), a prompt is provided to the user with a checkbox to \"Reopen windows when logging back in\". When selected, all applications currently open are added to a property list file named com.apple.loginwindow.[UUID].plist within the ~/Library/Preferences/ByHost directory."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1547.007" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1547/007> ;
	.

attack:T1547.008
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1547 ;
	rdfs:label "Boot or Logon Autostart Execution: LSASS Driver"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify or add LSASS drivers to obtain persistence on compromised systems. The Windows security subsystem is a set of components that manage and enforce the security policy for a computer or domain. The Local Security Authority (LSA) is the main component responsible for local security policy and user authentication."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1547.008" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1547/008> ;
	.

attack:T1547.009
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1547 ;
	rdfs:label "Boot or Logon Autostart Execution: Shortcut Modification"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may create or modify shortcuts that can execute a program during system boot or user login. Shortcuts or symbolic links are used to reference other files or programs that will be opened or executed when the shortcut is clicked or executed by a system startup process."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1547.009" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1547/009> ;
	.

attack:T1547.010
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1547 ;
	rdfs:label "Boot or Logon Autostart Execution: Port Monitors"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use port monitors to run an adversary supplied DLL during system boot for persistence or privilege escalation. A port monitor can be set through the AddMonitor API call to set a DLL to be loaded at startup. This DLL can be located in C:\\Windows\\System32 and will be loaded and run by the print spooler service, `spoolsv.exe`, under SYSTEM level permissions on boot."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1547.010" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1547/010> ;
	.

attack:T1547.011
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1547 ;
	rdfs:label "Boot or Logon Autostart Execution: Plist Modification"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries can modify property list files (plist files) to execute their code as part of establishing persistence. Plist files are used by macOS applications to store properties and configuration settings for applications and services. Applications use information plist files, Info.plist, to tell the operating system how to handle the application at runtime using structured metadata in the form of keys and values. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1647."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1547.011" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1647 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1547/011> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1547.012
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1547 ;
	rdfs:label "Boot or Logon Autostart Execution: Print Processors"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse print processors to run malicious DLLs during system boot for persistence and/or privilege escalation. Print processors are DLLs that are loaded by the print spooler service, `spoolsv.exe`, during boot."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1547.012" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1547/012> ;
	.

attack:T1547.013
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1547 ;
	rdfs:label "Boot or Logon Autostart Execution: XDG Autostart Entries"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may add or modify XDG Autostart Entries to execute malicious programs or commands when a user’s desktop environment is loaded at login. XDG Autostart entries are available for any XDG-compliant Linux system. XDG Autostart entries use Desktop Entry files (`.desktop`) to configure the user’s desktop environment upon user login."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1547.013" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1547/013> ;
	.

attack:T1547.014
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1547 ;
	rdfs:label "Boot or Logon Autostart Execution: Active Setup"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may achieve persistence by adding a Registry key to the Active Setup of the local machine. Active Setup is a Windows mechanism that is used to execute programs when a user logs in. The value stored in the Registry key will be executed after a user logs into the computer. These programs will be executed under the context of the user and will have the account's associated permissions level."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1547.014" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1547/014> ;
	.

attack:T1547.015
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1547 ;
	rdfs:label "Boot or Logon Autostart Execution: Login Items"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may add login items to execute upon user login to gain persistence or escalate privileges. Login items are applications, documents, folders, or server connections that are automatically launched when a user logs in. Login items can be added via a shared file list or Service Management Framework. Shared file list login items can be set using scripting languages such as AppleScript, whereas the Service Management Framework uses the API call SMLoginItemSetEnabled."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1547.015" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1547/015> ;
	.

attack:T1548
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Abuse Elevation Control Mechanism"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may circumvent mechanisms designed to control privilege elevation to gain higher-level permissions. Most modern systems contain native elevation control mechanisms that are intended to limit privileges that a user can perform on a machine. Authorization has to be granted to specific users in order to perform tasks that can be considered of higher risk."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1548" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1548> ;
	.

attack:T1548.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1548 ;
	rdfs:label "Abuse Elevation Control Mechanism: Setuid and Setgid"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may abuse configurations where an application has the setuid or setgid bits set in order to get code running in a different (and possibly more privileged) user’s context. On Linux or macOS, when the setuid or setgid bits are set for an application binary, the application will run with the privileges of the owning user or group respectively. Normally an application is run in the current user’s context, regardless of which user or group owns the application."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1548.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1548/001> ;
	.

attack:T1548.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1548 ;
	rdfs:label "Abuse Elevation Control Mechanism: Bypass User Account Control"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may bypass UAC mechanisms to elevate process privileges on system. Windows User Account Control (UAC) allows a program to elevate its privileges (tracked as integrity levels ranging from low to high) to perform a task under administrator-level permissions, possibly by prompting the user for confirmation."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1548.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1548/002> ;
	.

attack:T1548.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1548 ;
	rdfs:label "Abuse Elevation Control Mechanism: Sudo and Sudo Caching"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may perform sudo caching and/or use the sudoers file to elevate privileges. Adversaries may do this to execute commands as other users or spawn processes with higher privileges."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1548.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1548/003> ;
	.

attack:T1548.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1548 ;
	rdfs:label "Abuse Elevation Control Mechanism: Elevated Execution with Prompt"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may leverage the AuthorizationExecuteWithPrivileges API to escalate privileges by prompting the user for credentials. The purpose of this API is to give application developers an easy way to perform operations with root privileges, such as for application installation or updating. This API does not validate that the program requesting root privileges comes from a reputable source or has been maliciously modified."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1548.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1548/004> ;
	.

attack:T1548.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1548 ;
	rdfs:label "Abuse Elevation Control Mechanism: Temporary Elevated Cloud Access"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse permission configurations that allow them to gain temporarily elevated access to cloud resources. Many cloud environments allow administrators to grant user or service accounts permission to request just-in-time access to roles, impersonate other accounts, pass roles onto resources and services, or otherwise gain short-term access to a set of privileges that may be distinct from their own."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1548.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1548/005> ;
	.

attack:T1548.006
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1548 ;
	rdfs:label "Abuse Elevation Control Mechanism: TCC Manipulation"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries can manipulate or abuse the Transparency, Consent, & Control (TCC) service or database to grant malicious executables elevated permissions. TCC is a Privacy & Security macOS control mechanism used to determine if the running process has permission to access the data or services protected by TCC, such as screen sharing, camera, microphone, or Full Disk Access (FDA)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1548.006" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1548/006> ;
	.

attack:T1550
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Use Alternate Authentication Material"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use alternate authentication material, such as password hashes, Kerberos tickets, and application access tokens, in order to move laterally within an environment and bypass normal system access controls."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1550" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0008 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1550> ;
	.

attack:T1550.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1550 ;
	rdfs:label "Use Alternate Authentication Material: Application Access Token"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use stolen application access tokens to bypass the typical authentication process and access restricted accounts, information, or services on remote systems. These tokens are typically stolen from users or services and used in lieu of login credentials."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1550.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0008 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1550/001> ;
	.

attack:T1550.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1550 ;
	rdfs:label "Use Alternate Authentication Material: Pass the Hash"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may “pass the hash” using stolen password hashes to move laterally within an environment, bypassing normal system access controls. Pass the hash (PtH) is a method of authenticating as a user without having access to the user's cleartext password. This method bypasses standard authentication steps that require a cleartext password, moving directly into the portion of the authentication that uses the password hash."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1550.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0008 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1550/002> ;
	.

attack:T1550.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1550 ;
	rdfs:label "Use Alternate Authentication Material: Pass the Ticket"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may “pass the ticket” using stolen Kerberos tickets to move laterally within an environment, bypassing normal system access controls. Pass the ticket (PtT) is a method of authenticating to a system using Kerberos tickets without having access to an account's password. Kerberos authentication can be used as the first step to lateral movement to a remote system."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1550.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0008 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1550/003> ;
	.

attack:T1550.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1550 ;
	rdfs:label "Use Alternate Authentication Material: Web Session Cookie"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries can use stolen session cookies to authenticate to web applications and services. This technique bypasses some multi-factor authentication protocols since the session is already authenticated."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1550.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0008 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1550/004> ;
	.

attack:T1552
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Unsecured Credentials"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may search compromised systems to find and obtain insecurely stored credentials. These credentials can be stored and/or misplaced in many locations on a system, including plaintext files (e.g. Shell History), operating system or application-specific repositories (e.g. Credentials in Registry), or other specialized files/artifacts (e.g. Private Keys)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1552" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1552> ;
	.

attack:T1552.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1552 ;
	rdfs:label "Unsecured Credentials: Credentials In Files"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may search local file systems and remote file shares for files containing insecurely stored credentials. These can be files created by users to store their own credentials, shared credential stores for a group of individuals, configuration files containing passwords for a system or service, or source code/binary files containing embedded passwords."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1552.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1552/001> ;
	.

attack:T1552.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1552 ;
	rdfs:label "Unsecured Credentials: Credentials in Registry"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may search the Registry on compromised systems for insecurely stored credentials. The Windows Registry stores configuration information that can be used by the system or other programs. Adversaries may query the Registry looking for credentials and passwords that have been stored for use by other programs or services. Sometimes these credentials are used for automatic logons."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1552.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1552/002> ;
	.

attack:T1552.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1552 ;
	rdfs:label "Unsecured Credentials: Shell History"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may search the command history on compromised systems for insecurely stored credentials."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1552.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1552/003> ;
	.

attack:T1552.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1552 ;
	rdfs:label "Unsecured Credentials: Private Keys"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may search for private key certificate files on compromised systems for insecurely stored credentials. Private cryptographic keys and certificates are used for authentication, encryption/decryption, and digital signatures. Common key and certificate file extensions include: .key, .pgp, .gpg, .ppk., .p12, .pem, .pfx, .cer, .p7b, .asc."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1552.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1552/004> ;
	.

attack:T1552.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1552 ;
	rdfs:label "Unsecured Credentials: Cloud Instance Metadata API"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to access the Cloud Instance Metadata API to collect credentials and other sensitive data."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1552.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1552/005> ;
	.

attack:T1552.006
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1552 ;
	rdfs:label "Unsecured Credentials: Group Policy Preferences"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to find unsecured credentials in Group Policy Preferences (GPP). GPP are tools that allow administrators to create domain policies with embedded credentials. These policies allow administrators to set local accounts."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1552.006" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1552/006> ;
	.

attack:T1552.007
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1552 ;
	rdfs:label "Unsecured Credentials: Container API"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather credentials via APIs within a containers environment. APIs in these environments, such as the Docker API and Kubernetes APIs, allow a user to remotely manage their container resources and cluster components."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1552.007" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1552/007> ;
	.

attack:T1552.008
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1552 ;
	rdfs:label "Unsecured Credentials: Chat Messages"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may directly collect unsecured credentials stored or passed through user communication services. Credentials may be sent and stored in user chat communication applications such as email, chat services like Slack or Teams, collaboration tools like Jira or Trello, and any other services that support user communication."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1552.008" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1552/008> ;
	.

attack:T1553
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Subvert Trust Controls"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may undermine security controls that will either warn users of untrusted activity or prevent execution of untrusted programs. Operating systems and security products may contain mechanisms to identify programs or websites as possessing some level of trust."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1553" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1553> ;
	.

attack:T1553.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1553 ;
	rdfs:label "Subvert Trust Controls: Gatekeeper Bypass"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify file attributes and subvert Gatekeeper functionality to evade user prompts and execute untrusted programs. Gatekeeper is a set of technologies that act as layer of Apple’s security model to ensure only trusted applications are executed on a host. Gatekeeper was built on top of File Quarantine in Snow Leopard (10.6, 2009) and has grown to include Code Signing, security policy compliance, Notarization, and more."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1553.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1553/001> ;
	.

attack:T1553.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1553 ;
	rdfs:label "Subvert Trust Controls: Code Signing"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may create, acquire, or steal code signing materials to sign their malware or tools. Code signing provides a level of authenticity on a binary from the developer and a guarantee that the binary has not been tampered with. The certificates used during an operation may be created, acquired, or stolen by the adversary. Unlike Invalid Code Signature, this activity will result in a valid signature."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1553.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1553/002> ;
	.

attack:T1553.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1553 ;
	rdfs:label "Subvert Trust Controls: SIP and Trust Provider Hijacking"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may tamper with SIP and trust provider components to mislead the operating system and application control tools when conducting signature validation checks. In user mode, Windows Authenticode digital signatures are used to verify a file's origin and integrity, variables that may be used to establish trust in signed code (ex: a driver with a valid Microsoft signature may be handled as safe)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1553.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1553/003> ;
	.

attack:T1553.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1553 ;
	rdfs:label "Subvert Trust Controls: Install Root Certificate"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may install a root certificate on a compromised system to avoid warnings when connecting to adversary controlled web servers. Root certificates are used in public key cryptography to identify a root certificate authority (CA). When a root certificate is installed, the system or application will trust certificates in the root's chain of trust that have been signed by the root certificate."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1553.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1553/004> ;
	.

attack:T1553.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1553 ;
	rdfs:label "Subvert Trust Controls: Mark-of-the-Web Bypass"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse specific file formats to subvert Mark-of-the-Web (MOTW) controls. In Windows, when files are downloaded from the Internet, they are tagged with a hidden NTFS Alternate Data Stream (ADS) named Zone.Identifier with a specific value known as the MOTW. Files that are tagged with MOTW are protected and cannot perform certain actions. For example, starting in MS Office 10, if a MS Office file has the MOTW, it will open in Protected View."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1553.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1553/005> ;
	.

attack:T1553.006
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1553 ;
	rdfs:label "Subvert Trust Controls: Code Signing Policy Modification"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify code signing policies to enable execution of unsigned or self-signed code. Code signing provides a level of authenticity on a program from a developer and a guarantee that the program has not been tampered with. Security controls can include enforcement mechanisms to ensure that only valid, signed code can be run on an operating system."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1553.006" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1553/006> ;
	.

attack:T1554
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Compromise Host Software Binary"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify host software binaries to establish persistent access to systems. Software binaries/executables provide a wide range of system commands or services, programs, and libraries. Common software binaries are SSH clients, FTP clients, email clients, web browsers, and many other user or server applications."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1554" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1554> ;
	.

attack:T1555
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Credentials from Password Stores"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may search for common password storage locations to obtain user credentials. Passwords are stored in several places on a system, depending on the operating system or application holding the credentials. There are also specific applications and services that store passwords to make them easier for users to manage and maintain, such as password managers and cloud secrets vaults."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1555" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1555> ;
	.

attack:T1555.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1555 ;
	rdfs:label "Credentials from Password Stores: Keychain"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may acquire credentials from Keychain. Keychain (or Keychain Services) is the macOS credential management system that stores account names, passwords, private keys, certificates, sensitive application data, payment data, and secure notes. There are three types of Keychains: Login Keychain, System Keychain, and Local Items (iCloud) Keychain. The default Keychain is the Login Keychain, which stores user passwords and information."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1555.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1555/001> ;
	.

attack:T1555.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1555 ;
	rdfs:label "Credentials from Password Stores: Securityd Memory"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary with root access may gather credentials by reading `securityd`’s memory. `securityd` is a service/daemon responsible for implementing security protocols such as encryption and authorization. A privileged adversary may be able to scan through `securityd`'s memory to find the correct sequence of keys to decrypt the user’s logon keychain. This may provide the adversary with various plaintext passwords, such as those for users, WiFi, mail, browsers, certificates, secure notes, etc."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1555.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1555/002> ;
	.

attack:T1555.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1555 ;
	rdfs:label "Credentials from Password Stores: Credentials from Web Browsers"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may acquire credentials from web browsers by reading files specific to the target browser. Web browsers commonly save credentials such as website usernames and passwords so that they do not need to be entered manually in the future. Web browsers typically store the credentials in an encrypted format within a credential store; however, methods exist to extract plaintext credentials from web browsers."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1555.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1555/003> ;
	.

attack:T1555.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1555 ;
	rdfs:label "Credentials from Password Stores: Windows Credential Manager"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may acquire credentials from the Windows Credential Manager. The Credential Manager stores credentials for signing into websites, applications, and/or devices that request authentication through NTLM or Kerberos in Credential Lockers (previously known as Windows Vaults)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1555.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1555/004> ;
	.

attack:T1555.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1555 ;
	rdfs:label "Credentials from Password Stores: Password Managers"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may acquire user credentials from third-party password managers. Password managers are applications designed to store user credentials, normally in an encrypted database. Credentials are typically accessible after a user provides a master password that unlocks the database. After the database is unlocked, these credentials may be copied to memory. These databases can be stored as files on disk."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1555.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1555/005> ;
	.

attack:T1555.006
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1555 ;
	rdfs:label "Credentials from Password Stores: Cloud Secrets Management Stores"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may acquire credentials from cloud-native secret management solutions such as AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, and Terraform Vault."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1555.006" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1555/006> ;
	.

attack:T1556
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Authentication Process"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify authentication mechanisms and processes to access user credentials or enable otherwise unwarranted access to accounts. The authentication process is handled by mechanisms, such as the Local Security Authentication Server (LSASS) process and the Security Accounts Manager (SAM) on Windows, pluggable authentication modules (PAM) on Unix-based systems, and authorization plugins on MacOS systems, responsible for gathering, storing, and validating credentials."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1556" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1556> ;
	.

attack:T1556.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1556 ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Authentication Process: Domain Controller Authentication"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may patch the authentication process on a domain controller to bypass the typical authentication mechanisms and enable access to accounts."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1556.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1556/001> ;
	.

attack:T1556.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1556 ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Authentication Process: Password Filter DLL"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may register malicious password filter dynamic link libraries (DLLs) into the authentication process to acquire user credentials as they are validated."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1556.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1556/002> ;
	.

attack:T1556.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1556 ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Authentication Process: Pluggable Authentication Modules"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify pluggable authentication modules (PAM) to access user credentials or enable otherwise unwarranted access to accounts. PAM is a modular system of configuration files, libraries, and executable files which guide authentication for many services. The most common authentication module is pam_unix.so, which retrieves, sets, and verifies account authentication information in /etc/passwd and /etc/shadow."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1556.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1556/003> ;
	.

attack:T1556.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1556 ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Authentication Process: Network Device Authentication"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use Patch System Image to hard code a password in the operating system, thus bypassing of native authentication mechanisms for local accounts on network devices."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1556.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1556/004> ;
	.

attack:T1556.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1556 ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Authentication Process: Reversible Encryption"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may abuse Active Directory authentication encryption properties to gain access to credentials on Windows systems. The AllowReversiblePasswordEncryption property specifies whether reversible password encryption for an account is enabled or disabled. By default this property is disabled (instead storing user credentials as the output of one-way hashing functions) and should not be enabled unless legacy or other software require it."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1556.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1556/005> ;
	.

attack:T1556.006
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1556 ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Authentication Process: Multi-Factor Authentication"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may disable or modify multi-factor authentication (MFA) mechanisms to enable persistent access to compromised accounts."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1556.006" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1556/006> ;
	.

attack:T1556.007
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1556 ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Authentication Process: Hybrid Identity"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may patch, modify, or otherwise backdoor cloud authentication processes that are tied to on-premises user identities in order to bypass typical authentication mechanisms, access credentials, and enable persistent access to accounts."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1556.007" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1556/007> ;
	.

attack:T1556.008
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1556 ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Authentication Process: Network Provider DLL"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may register malicious network provider dynamic link libraries (DLLs) to capture cleartext user credentials during the authentication process. Network provider DLLs allow Windows to interface with specific network protocols and can also support add-on credential management functions. During the logon process, Winlogon (the interactive logon module) sends credentials to the local `mpnotify.exe` process via RPC."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1556.008" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1556/008> ;
	.

attack:T1556.009
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1556 ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Authentication Process: Conditional Access Policies"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may disable or modify conditional access policies to enable persistent access to compromised accounts. Conditional access policies are additional verifications used by identity providers and identity and access management systems to determine whether a user should be granted access to a resource."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1556.009" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1556/009> ;
	.

attack:T1557
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Adversary-in-the-Middle"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to position themselves between two or more networked devices using an adversary-in-the-middle (AiTM) technique to support follow-on behaviors such as Network Sniffing, Transmitted Data Manipulation, or replay attacks (Exploitation for Credential Access). By abusing features of common networking protocols that can determine the flow of network traffic (e.g."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1557" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0009 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1557> ;
	.

attack:T1557.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1557 ;
	rdfs:label "Adversary-in-the-Middle: Name Resolution Poisoning and SMB Relay"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "By responding to LLMNR/NBT-NS/mDNS network traffic, adversaries may spoof an authoritative source for name resolution to force communication with an adversary controlled system. This activity may be used to collect or relay authentication materials."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1557.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0009 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1557/001> ;
	.

attack:T1557.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1557 ;
	rdfs:label "Adversary-in-the-Middle: ARP Cache Poisoning"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may poison Address Resolution Protocol (ARP) caches to position themselves between the communication of two or more networked devices. This activity may be used to enable follow-on behaviors such as Network Sniffing or Transmitted Data Manipulation."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1557.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0009 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1557/002> ;
	.

attack:T1557.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1557 ;
	rdfs:label "Adversary-in-the-Middle: DHCP Spoofing"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may redirect network traffic to adversary-owned systems by spoofing Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) traffic and acting as a malicious DHCP server on the victim network. By achieving the adversary-in-the-middle (AiTM) position, adversaries may collect network communications, including passed credentials, especially those sent over insecure, unencrypted protocols. This may also enable follow-on behaviors such as Network Sniffing or Transmitted Data Manipulation."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1557.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0009 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1557/003> ;
	.

attack:T1557.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1557 ;
	rdfs:label "Adversary-in-the-Middle: Evil Twin"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may host seemingly genuine Wi-Fi access points to deceive users into connecting to malicious networks as a way of supporting follow-on behaviors such as Network Sniffing, Transmitted Data Manipulation, or Input Capture."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1557.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0009 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1557/004> ;
	.

attack:T1558
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Steal or Forge Kerberos Tickets"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to subvert Kerberos authentication by stealing or forging Kerberos tickets to enable Pass the Ticket. Kerberos is an authentication protocol widely used in modern Windows domain environments. In Kerberos environments, referred to as “realms”, there are three basic participants: client, service, and Key Distribution Center (KDC)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1558" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1558> ;
	.

attack:T1558.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1558 ;
	rdfs:label "Steal or Forge Kerberos Tickets: Golden Ticket"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries who have the KRBTGT account password hash may forge Kerberos ticket-granting tickets (TGT), also known as a golden ticket. Golden tickets enable adversaries to generate authentication material for any account in Active Directory."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1558.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1558/001> ;
	.

attack:T1558.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1558 ;
	rdfs:label "Steal or Forge Kerberos Tickets: Silver Ticket"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries who have the password hash of a target service account (e.g. SharePoint, MSSQL) may forge Kerberos ticket granting service (TGS) tickets, also known as silver tickets. Kerberos TGS tickets are also known as service tickets."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1558.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1558/002> ;
	.

attack:T1558.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1558 ;
	rdfs:label "Steal or Forge Kerberos Tickets: Kerberoasting"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse a valid Kerberos ticket-granting ticket (TGT) or sniff network traffic to obtain a ticket-granting service (TGS) ticket that may be vulnerable to Brute Force."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1558.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1558/003> ;
	.

attack:T1558.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1558 ;
	rdfs:label "Steal or Forge Kerberos Tickets: AS-REP Roasting"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may reveal credentials of accounts that have disabled Kerberos preauthentication by Password Cracking Kerberos messages."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1558.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1558/004> ;
	.

attack:T1558.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1558 ;
	rdfs:label "Steal or Forge Kerberos Tickets: Ccache Files"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to steal Kerberos tickets stored in credential cache files (or ccache). These files are used for short term storage of a user's active session credentials. The ccache file is created upon user authentication and allows for access to multiple services without the user having to re-enter credentials."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1558.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1558/005> ;
	.

attack:T1559
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Inter-Process Communication"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse inter-process communication (IPC) mechanisms for local code or command execution. IPC is typically used by processes to share data, communicate with each other, or synchronize execution. IPC is also commonly used to avoid situations such as deadlocks, which occurs when processes are stuck in a cyclic waiting pattern."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1559" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1559> ;
	.

attack:T1559.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1559 ;
	rdfs:label "Inter-Process Communication: Component Object Model"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use the Windows Component Object Model (COM) for local code execution. COM is an inter-process communication (IPC) component of the native Windows application programming interface (API) that enables interaction between software objects, or executable code that implements one or more interfaces. Through COM, a client object can call methods of server objects, which are typically binary Dynamic Link Libraries (DLL) or executables (EXE)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1559.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1559/001> ;
	.

attack:T1559.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1559 ;
	rdfs:label "Inter-Process Communication: Dynamic Data Exchange"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use Windows Dynamic Data Exchange (DDE) to execute arbitrary commands. DDE is a client-server protocol for one-time and/or continuous inter-process communication (IPC) between applications. Once a link is established, applications can autonomously exchange transactions consisting of strings, warm data links (notifications when a data item changes), hot data links (duplications of changes to a data item), and requests for command execution."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1559.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1559/002> ;
	.

attack:T1559.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1559 ;
	rdfs:label "Inter-Process Communication: XPC Services"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries can provide malicious content to an XPC service daemon for local code execution. macOS uses XPC services for basic inter-process communication between various processes, such as between the XPC Service daemon and third-party application privileged helper tools."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1559.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1559/003> ;
	.

attack:T1560
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Archive Collected Data"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may compress and/or encrypt data that is collected prior to exfiltration. Compressing the data can help to obfuscate the collected data and minimize the amount of data sent over the network. Encryption can be used to hide information that is being exfiltrated from detection or make exfiltration less conspicuous upon inspection by a defender."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1560" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0009 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1560> ;
	.

attack:T1560.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1560 ;
	rdfs:label "Archive Collected Data: Archive via Utility"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use utilities to compress and/or encrypt collected data prior to exfiltration. Many utilities include functionalities to compress, encrypt, or otherwise package data into a format that is easier/more secure to transport."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1560.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0009 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1560/001> ;
	.

attack:T1560.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1560 ;
	rdfs:label "Archive Collected Data: Archive via Library"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may compress or encrypt data that is collected prior to exfiltration using 3rd party libraries. Many libraries exist that can archive data, including Python rarfile, libzip, and zlib. Most libraries include functionality to encrypt and/or compress data."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1560.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0009 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1560/002> ;
	.

attack:T1560.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1560 ;
	rdfs:label "Archive Collected Data: Archive via Custom Method"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may compress or encrypt data that is collected prior to exfiltration using a custom method. Adversaries may choose to use custom archival methods, such as encryption with XOR or stream ciphers implemented with no external library or utility references. Custom implementations of well-known compression algorithms have also been used."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1560.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0009 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1560/003> ;
	.

attack:T1561
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Disk Wipe"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may wipe or corrupt raw disk data on specific systems or in large numbers in a network to interrupt availability to system and network resources. With direct write access to a disk, adversaries may attempt to overwrite portions of disk data. Adversaries may opt to wipe arbitrary portions of disk data and/or wipe disk structures like the master boot record (MBR). A complete wipe of all disk sectors may be attempted."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1561" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0040 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1561> ;
	.

attack:T1561.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1561 ;
	rdfs:label "Disk Wipe: Disk Content Wipe"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may erase the contents of storage devices on specific systems or in large numbers in a network to interrupt availability to system and network resources."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1561.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0040 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1561/001> ;
	.

attack:T1561.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1561 ;
	rdfs:label "Disk Wipe: Disk Structure Wipe"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may corrupt or wipe the disk data structures on a hard drive necessary to boot a system; targeting specific critical systems or in large numbers in a network to interrupt availability to system and network resources."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1561.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0040 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1561/002> ;
	.

attack:T1562
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Impair Defenses"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may maliciously modify components of a victim environment in order to hinder or disable defensive mechanisms. This not only involves impairing preventative defenses, such as firewalls and anti-virus, but also detection capabilities that defenders can use to audit activity and identify malicious behavior. This may also span both native defenses as well as supplemental capabilities installed by users and administrators. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1685."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1562" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1685 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1562> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1562.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1562 ;
	rdfs:label "Impair Defenses: Disable or Modify Tools"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify and/or disable security tools to avoid possible detection of their malware/tools and activities. This may take many forms, such as killing security software processes or services, modifying / deleting Registry keys or configuration files so that tools do not operate properly, or other methods to interfere with security tools scanning or reporting information. Adversaries may also disable updates to prevent the latest security patches from reaching tools on victim systems. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1685."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1562.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1685 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1562/001> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1562.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1562 ;
	rdfs:label "Impair Defenses: Disable Windows Event Logging"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may disable Windows event logging to limit data that can be leveraged for detections and audits. Windows event logs record user and system activity such as login attempts, process creation, and much more. This data is used by security tools and analysts to generate detections. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1685.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1562.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1685.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1562/002> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1562.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1562 ;
	rdfs:label "Impair Defenses: Impair Command History Logging"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may impair command history logging to hide commands they run on a compromised system. Various command interpreters keep track of the commands users type in their terminal so that users can retrace what they've done. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1690."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1562.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1690 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1562/003> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1562.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1562 ;
	rdfs:label "Impair Defenses: Disable or Modify System Firewall"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may disable or modify system firewalls in order to bypass controls limiting network usage. Changes could be disabling the entire mechanism as well as adding, deleting, or modifying particular rules. This can be done numerous ways depending on the operating system, including via command-line, editing Windows Registry keys, and Windows Control Panel. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1686."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1562.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1686 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1562/004> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1562.006
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1562 ;
	rdfs:label "Impair Defenses: Indicator Blocking"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may attempt to block indicators or events typically captured by sensors from being gathered and analyzed. This could include maliciously redirecting or even disabling host-based sensors, such as Event Tracing for Windows (ETW), by tampering settings that control the collection and flow of event telemetry. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1685."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1562.006" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1685 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1562/006> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1562.007
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1562 ;
	rdfs:label "Impair Defenses: Disable or Modify Cloud Firewall"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may disable or modify a firewall within a cloud environment to bypass controls that limit access to cloud resources. Cloud firewalls are separate from system firewalls that are described in Disable or Modify System Firewall. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1686.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1562.007" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1686.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1562/007> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1562.008
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1562 ;
	rdfs:label "Impair Defenses: Disable or Modify Cloud Logs"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may disable or modify cloud logging capabilities and integrations to limit what data is collected on their activities and avoid detection. Cloud environments allow for collection and analysis of audit and application logs that provide insight into what activities a user does within the environment. If an adversary has sufficient permissions, they can disable or modify logging to avoid detection of their activities. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1685.002."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1562.008" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1685.002 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1562/008> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1562.009
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1562 ;
	rdfs:label "Impair Defenses: Safe Mode Boot"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse Windows safe mode to disable endpoint defenses. Safe mode starts up the Windows operating system with a limited set of drivers and services. Third-party security software such as endpoint detection and response (EDR) tools may not start after booting Windows in safe mode. There are two versions of safe mode: Safe Mode and Safe Mode with Networking. It is possible to start additional services after a safe mode boot. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1688."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1562.009" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1688 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1562/009> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1562.010
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1562 ;
	rdfs:label "Impair Defenses: Downgrade Attack"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may downgrade or use a version of system features that may be outdated, vulnerable, and/or does not support updated security controls. Downgrade attacks typically take advantage of a system’s backward compatibility to force it into less secure modes of operation. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1689."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1562.010" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1689 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1562/010> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1562.011
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1562 ;
	rdfs:label "Impair Defenses: Spoof Security Alerting"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may spoof security alerting from tools, presenting false evidence to impair defenders’ awareness of malicious activity. Messages produced by defensive tools contain information about potential security events as well as the functioning status of security software and the system. Security reporting messages are important for monitoring the normal operation of a system and identifying important events that can signal a security incident. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1685.003."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1562.011" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1685.003 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1562/011> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1562.012
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1562 ;
	rdfs:label "Impair Defenses: Disable or Modify Linux Audit System"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may disable or modify the Linux audit system to hide malicious activity and avoid detection. Linux admins use the Linux Audit system to track security-relevant information on a system. The Linux Audit system operates at the kernel-level and maintains event logs on application and system activity such as process, network, file, and login events based on pre-configured rules. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1685.004."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1562.012" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1685.004 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1562/012> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1562.013
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1562 ;
	rdfs:label "Impair Defenses: Disable or Modify Network Device Firewall"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may disable network device-based firewall mechanisms entirely or add, delete, or modify particular rules in order to bypass controls limiting network usage. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1686.002."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1562.013" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1686.002 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1562/013> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1563
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Remote Service Session Hijacking"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may take control of preexisting sessions with remote services to move laterally in an environment. Users may use valid credentials to log into a service specifically designed to accept remote connections, such as telnet, SSH, and RDP. When a user logs into a service, a session will be established that will allow them to maintain a continuous interaction with that service."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1563" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0008 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1563> ;
	.

attack:T1563.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1563 ;
	rdfs:label "Remote Service Session Hijacking: SSH Hijacking"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may hijack a legitimate user's SSH session to move laterally within an environment. Secure Shell (SSH) is a standard means of remote access on Linux and macOS systems. It allows a user to connect to another system via an encrypted tunnel, commonly authenticating through a password, certificate or the use of an asymmetric encryption key pair."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1563.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0008 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1563/001> ;
	.

attack:T1563.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1563 ;
	rdfs:label "Remote Service Session Hijacking: RDP Hijacking"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may hijack a legitimate user’s remote desktop session to move laterally within an environment. Remote desktop is a common feature in operating systems. It allows a user to log into an interactive session with a system desktop graphical user interface on a remote system. Microsoft refers to its implementation of the Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP) as Remote Desktop Services (RDS)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1563.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0008 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1563/002> ;
	.

attack:T1564
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Hide Artifacts"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to hide artifacts associated with their behaviors to evade detection. Operating systems may have features to hide various artifacts, such as important system files and administrative task execution, to avoid disrupting user work environments and prevent users from changing files or features on the system. Adversaries may abuse these features to hide artifacts such as files, directories, user accounts, or other system activity to evade detection."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1564" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1564> ;
	.

attack:T1564.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1564 ;
	rdfs:label "Hide Artifacts: Hidden Files and Directories"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may set files and directories to be hidden to evade detection mechanisms. To prevent normal users from accidentally changing special files on a system, most operating systems have the concept of a ‘hidden’ file. These files don’t show up when a user browses the file system with a GUI or when using normal commands on the command line."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1564.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1564/001> ;
	.

attack:T1564.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1564 ;
	rdfs:label "Hide Artifacts: Hidden Users"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use hidden users to hide the presence of user accounts they create or modify. Administrators may want to hide users when there are many user accounts on a given system or if they want to hide their administrative or other management accounts from other users."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1564.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1564/002> ;
	.

attack:T1564.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1564 ;
	rdfs:label "Hide Artifacts: Hidden Window"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use hidden windows to conceal malicious activity from the plain sight of users. In some cases, windows that would typically be displayed when an application carries out an operation can be hidden. This may be utilized by system administrators to avoid disrupting user work environments when carrying out administrative tasks."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1564.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1564/003> ;
	.

attack:T1564.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1564 ;
	rdfs:label "Hide Artifacts: NTFS File Attributes"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use NTFS file attributes to hide their malicious data in order to evade detection. Every New Technology File System (NTFS) formatted partition contains a Master File Table (MFT) that maintains a record for every file/directory on the partition. Within MFT entries are file attributes, such as Extended Attributes (EA) and Data [known as Alternate Data Streams (ADSs) when more than one Data attribute is present], that can be used to store arbitrary data (and even complete files)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1564.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1564/004> ;
	.

attack:T1564.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1564 ;
	rdfs:label "Hide Artifacts: Hidden File System"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use a hidden file system to conceal malicious activity from users and security tools. File systems provide a structure to store and access data from physical storage. Typically, a user engages with a file system through applications that allow them to access files and directories, which are an abstraction from their physical location (ex: disk sector). Standard file systems include FAT, NTFS, ext4, and APFS."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1564.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1564/005> ;
	.

attack:T1564.006
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1564 ;
	rdfs:label "Hide Artifacts: Run Virtual Instance"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may carry out malicious operations using a virtual instance to avoid detection. A wide variety of virtualization technologies exist that allow for the emulation of a computer or computing environment. By running malicious code inside of a virtual instance, adversaries can hide artifacts associated with their behavior from security tools that are unable to monitor activity inside the virtual instance."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1564.006" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1564/006> ;
	.

attack:T1564.007
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1564 ;
	rdfs:label "Hide Artifacts: VBA Stomping"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may hide malicious Visual Basic for Applications (VBA) payloads embedded within MS Office documents by replacing the VBA source code with benign data."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1564.007" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1564/007> ;
	.

attack:T1564.008
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1564 ;
	rdfs:label "Hide Artifacts: Email Hiding Rules"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use email rules to hide inbound emails in a compromised user's mailbox. Many email clients allow users to create inbox rules for various email functions, including moving emails to other folders, marking emails as read, or deleting emails. Rules may be created or modified within email clients or through external features such as the New-InboxRule or Set-InboxRule PowerShell cmdlets on Windows systems."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1564.008" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-08-04"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1564/008> ;
	.

attack:T1564.009
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1564 ;
	rdfs:label "Hide Artifacts: Resource Forking"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse resource forks to hide malicious code or executables to evade detection and bypass security applications. A resource fork provides applications a structured way to store resources such as thumbnail images, menu definitions, icons, dialog boxes, and code. Usage of a resource fork is identifiable when displaying a file’s extended attributes, using ls -l@ or xattr -l commands. Resource forks have been deprecated and replaced with the application bundle structure."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1564.009" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1564/009> ;
	.

attack:T1564.010
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1564 ;
	rdfs:label "Hide Artifacts: Process Argument Spoofing"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to hide process command-line arguments by overwriting process memory. Process command-line arguments are stored in the process environment block (PEB), a data structure used by Windows to store various information about/used by a process. The PEB includes the process command-line arguments that are referenced when executing the process. When a process is created, defensive tools/sensors that monitor process creations may retrieve the process arguments from the PEB."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1564.010" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1564/010> ;
	.

attack:T1564.011
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1564 ;
	rdfs:label "Hide Artifacts: Ignore Process Interrupts"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may evade defensive mechanisms by executing commands that hide from process interrupt signals. Many operating systems use signals to deliver messages to control process behavior. Command interpreters often include specific commands/flags that ignore errors and other hangups, such as when the user of the active session logs off. These interrupt signals may also be used by defensive tools and/or analysts to pause or terminate specified running processes."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1564.011" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1564/011> ;
	.

attack:T1564.012
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1564 ;
	rdfs:label "Hide Artifacts: File/Path Exclusions"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to hide their file-based artifacts by writing them to specific folders or file names excluded from antivirus (AV) scanning and other defensive capabilities. AV and other file-based scanners often include exclusions to optimize performance as well as ease installation and legitimate use of applications."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1564.012" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1564/012> ;
	.

attack:T1564.013
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1564 ;
	rdfs:label "Hide Artifacts: Bind Mounts"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse bind mounts on file structures to hide their activity and artifacts from native utilities. A bind mount maps a directory or file from one location on the filesystem to another, similar to a shortcut on Windows. It’s commonly used to provide access to specific files or directories across different environments, such as inside containers or chroot environments, and requires sudo access."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1564.013" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1564/013> ;
	.

attack:T1564.014
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1564 ;
	rdfs:label "Hide Artifacts: Extended Attributes"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse extended attributes (xattrs) on macOS and Linux to hide their malicious data in order to evade detection. Extended attributes are key-value pairs of file and directory metadata used by both macOS and Linux. They are not visible through standard tools like `Finder`, `ls`, or `cat` and require utilities such as `xattr` (macOS) or `getfattr` (Linux) for inspection. Operating systems and applications use xattrs for tagging, integrity checks, and access control."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1564.014" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1564/014> ;
	.

attack:T1565
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Data Manipulation"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may insert, delete, or manipulate data in order to influence external outcomes or hide activity, thus threatening the integrity of the data. By manipulating data, adversaries may attempt to affect a business process, organizational understanding, or decision making."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1565" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0040 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1565> ;
	.

attack:T1565.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1565 ;
	rdfs:label "Data Manipulation: Stored Data Manipulation"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may insert, delete, or manipulate data at rest in order to influence external outcomes or hide activity, thus threatening the integrity of the data. By manipulating stored data, adversaries may attempt to affect a business process, organizational understanding, and decision making."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1565.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0040 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-11-13"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1565/001> ;
	.

attack:T1565.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1565 ;
	rdfs:label "Data Manipulation: Transmitted Data Manipulation"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may alter data en route to storage or other systems in order to manipulate external outcomes or hide activity, thus threatening the integrity of the data. By manipulating transmitted data, adversaries may attempt to affect a business process, organizational understanding, and decision making."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1565.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0040 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1565/002> ;
	.

attack:T1565.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1565 ;
	rdfs:label "Data Manipulation: Runtime Data Manipulation"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify systems in order to manipulate the data as it is accessed and displayed to an end user, thus threatening the integrity of the data. By manipulating runtime data, adversaries may attempt to affect a business process, organizational understanding, and decision making."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1565.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0040 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-11-13"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1565/003> ;
	.

attack:T1566
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Phishing"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may send phishing messages to gain access to victim systems. All forms of phishing are electronically delivered social engineering. Phishing can be targeted, known as spearphishing. In spearphishing, a specific individual, company, or industry will be targeted by the adversary. More generally, adversaries can conduct non-targeted phishing, such as in mass malware spam campaigns."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1566" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0001 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566> ;
	.

attack:T1566.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1566 ;
	rdfs:label "Phishing: Spearphishing Attachment"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may send spearphishing emails with a malicious attachment in an attempt to gain access to victim systems. Spearphishing attachment is a specific variant of spearphishing. Spearphishing attachment is different from other forms of spearphishing in that it employs the use of malware attached to an email. All forms of spearphishing are electronically delivered social engineering targeted at a specific individual, company, or industry."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1566.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0001 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566/001> ;
	.

attack:T1566.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1566 ;
	rdfs:label "Phishing: Spearphishing Link"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may send spearphishing emails with a malicious link in an attempt to gain access to victim systems. Spearphishing with a link is a specific variant of spearphishing. It is different from other forms of spearphishing in that it employs the use of links to download malware contained in email, instead of attaching malicious files to the email itself, to avoid defenses that may inspect email attachments."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1566.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0001 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566/002> ;
	.

attack:T1566.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1566 ;
	rdfs:label "Phishing: Spearphishing via Service"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may send spearphishing messages via third-party services in an attempt to gain access to victim systems. Spearphishing via service is a specific variant of spearphishing. It is different from other forms of spearphishing in that it employs the use of third party services rather than directly via enterprise email channels."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1566.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0001 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566/003> ;
	.

attack:T1566.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1566 ;
	rdfs:label "Phishing: Spearphishing Voice"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use voice communications to ultimately gain access to victim systems. Spearphishing voice is a specific variant of spearphishing. It is different from other forms of spearphishing in that it employs the use of manipulating a user into providing access to systems through a phone call or other forms of voice communications."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1566.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0001 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566/004> ;
	.

attack:T1567
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Exfiltration Over Web Service"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use an existing, legitimate external Web service to exfiltrate data rather than their primary command and control channel. Popular Web services acting as an exfiltration mechanism may give a significant amount of cover due to the likelihood that hosts within a network are already communicating with them prior to compromise. Firewall rules may also already exist to permit traffic to these services."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1567" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0010 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1567> ;
	.

attack:T1567.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1567 ;
	rdfs:label "Exfiltration Over Web Service: Exfiltration to Code Repository"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may exfiltrate data to a code repository rather than over their primary command and control channel. Code repositories are often accessible via an API (ex: https://api.github.com). Access to these APIs are often over HTTPS, which gives the adversary an additional level of protection."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1567.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0010 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1567/001> ;
	.

attack:T1567.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1567 ;
	rdfs:label "Exfiltration Over Web Service: Exfiltration to Cloud Storage"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may exfiltrate data to a cloud storage service rather than over their primary command and control channel. Cloud storage services allow for the storage, edit, and retrieval of data from a remote cloud storage server over the Internet."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1567.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0010 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1567/002> ;
	.

attack:T1567.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1567 ;
	rdfs:label "Exfiltration Over Web Service: Exfiltration to Text Storage Sites"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may exfiltrate data to text storage sites instead of their primary command and control channel. Text storage sites, such as pastebin[.]com, are commonly used by developers to share code and other information."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1567.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0010 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1567/003> ;
	.

attack:T1567.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1567 ;
	rdfs:label "Exfiltration Over Web Service: Exfiltration Over Webhook"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may exfiltrate data to a webhook endpoint rather than over their primary command and control channel. Webhooks are simple mechanisms for allowing a server to push data over HTTP/S to a client without the need for the client to continuously poll the server. Many public and commercial services, such as Discord, Slack, and `webhook.site`, support the creation of webhook endpoints that can be used by other services, such as Github, Jira, or Trello."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1567.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0010 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1567/004> ;
	.

attack:T1568
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Dynamic Resolution"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may dynamically establish connections to command and control infrastructure to evade common detections and remediations. This may be achieved by using malware that shares a common algorithm with the infrastructure the adversary uses to receive the malware's communications. These calculations can be used to dynamically adjust parameters such as the domain name, IP address, or port number the malware uses for command and control."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1568" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1568> ;
	.

attack:T1568.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1568 ;
	rdfs:label "Dynamic Resolution: Fast Flux DNS"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use Fast Flux DNS to hide a command and control channel behind an array of rapidly changing IP addresses linked to a single domain resolution. This technique uses a fully qualified domain name, with multiple IP addresses assigned to it which are swapped with high frequency, using a combination of round robin IP addressing and short Time-To-Live (TTL) for a DNS resource record."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1568.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1568/001> ;
	.

attack:T1568.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1568 ;
	rdfs:label "Dynamic Resolution: Domain Generation Algorithms"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may make use of Domain Generation Algorithms (DGAs) to dynamically identify a destination domain for command and control traffic rather than relying on a list of static IP addresses or domains. This has the advantage of making it much harder for defenders to block, track, or take over the command and control channel, as there potentially could be thousands of domains that malware can check for instructions."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1568.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1568/002> ;
	.

attack:T1568.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1568 ;
	rdfs:label "Dynamic Resolution: DNS Calculation"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may perform calculations on addresses returned in DNS results to determine which port and IP address to use for command and control, rather than relying on a predetermined port number or the actual returned IP address. A IP and/or port number calculation can be used to bypass egress filtering on a C2 channel."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1568.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1568/003> ;
	.

attack:T1569
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "System Services"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse system services or daemons to execute commands or programs. Adversaries can execute malicious content by interacting with or creating services either locally or remotely. Many services are set to run at boot, which can aid in achieving persistence (Create or Modify System Process), but adversaries can also abuse services for one-time or temporary execution."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1569" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1569> ;
	.

attack:T1569.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1569 ;
	rdfs:label "System Services: Launchctl"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse launchctl to execute commands or programs. Launchctl interfaces with launchd, the service management framework for macOS. Launchctl supports taking subcommands on the command-line, interactively, or even redirected from standard input."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1569.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1569/001> ;
	.

attack:T1569.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1569 ;
	rdfs:label "System Services: Service Execution"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse the Windows service control manager to execute malicious commands or payloads. The Windows service control manager (services.exe) is an interface to manage and manipulate services. The service control manager is accessible to users via GUI components as well as system utilities such as sc.exe and Net."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1569.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1569/002> ;
	.

attack:T1569.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1569 ;
	rdfs:label "System Services: Systemctl"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse systemctl to execute commands or programs. Systemctl is the primary interface for systemd, the Linux init system and service manager. Typically invoked from a shell, Systemctl can also be integrated into scripts or applications."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1569.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1569/003> ;
	.

attack:T1570
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Lateral Tool Transfer"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may transfer tools or other files between systems in a compromised environment. Once brought into the victim environment (i.e., Ingress Tool Transfer) files may then be copied from one system to another to stage adversary tools or other files over the course of an operation."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1570" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0008 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1570> ;
	.

attack:T1571
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Non-Standard Port"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may communicate using a protocol and port pairing that are typically not associated. For example, HTTPS over port 8088 or port 587 as opposed to the traditional port 443. Adversaries may make changes to the standard port used by a protocol to bypass filtering or muddle analysis/parsing of network data."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1571" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1571> ;
	.

attack:T1572
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Protocol Tunneling"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may tunnel network communications to and from a victim system within a separate protocol to avoid detection/network filtering and/or enable access to otherwise unreachable systems. Tunneling involves explicitly encapsulating a protocol within another. This behavior may conceal malicious traffic by blending in with existing traffic and/or provide an outer layer of encryption (similar to a VPN)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1572" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1572> ;
	.

attack:T1573
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Encrypted Channel"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may employ an encryption algorithm to conceal command and control traffic rather than relying on any inherent protections provided by a communication protocol. Despite the use of a secure algorithm, these implementations may be vulnerable to reverse engineering if secret keys are encoded and/or generated within malware samples/configuration files."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1573" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1573> ;
	.

attack:T1573.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1573 ;
	rdfs:label "Encrypted Channel: Symmetric Cryptography"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may employ a known symmetric encryption algorithm to conceal command and control traffic rather than relying on any inherent protections provided by a communication protocol. Symmetric encryption algorithms use the same key for plaintext encryption and ciphertext decryption. Common symmetric encryption algorithms include AES, DES, 3DES, Blowfish, and RC4."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1573.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1573/001> ;
	.

attack:T1573.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1573 ;
	rdfs:label "Encrypted Channel: Asymmetric Cryptography"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may employ a known asymmetric encryption algorithm to conceal command and control traffic rather than relying on any inherent protections provided by a communication protocol. Asymmetric cryptography, also known as public key cryptography, uses a keypair per party: one public that can be freely distributed, and one private. Due to how the keys are generated, the sender encrypts data with the receiver’s public key and the receiver decrypts the data with their private key."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1573.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1573/002> ;
	.

attack:T1574
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Hijack Execution Flow"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may execute their own malicious payloads by hijacking the way operating systems run programs. Hijacking execution flow can be for the purposes of persistence, since this hijacked execution may reoccur over time. Adversaries may also use these mechanisms to elevate privileges or evade defenses, such as application control or other restrictions on execution."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1574" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1574> ;
	.

attack:T1574.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1574 ;
	rdfs:label "Hijack Execution Flow: DLL"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse dynamic-link library files (DLLs) in order to achieve persistence, escalate privileges, and evade defenses. DLLs are libraries that contain code and data that can be simultaneously utilized by multiple programs. While DLLs are not malicious by nature, they can be abused through mechanisms such as side-loading, hijacking search order, and phantom DLL hijacking."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1574.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1574/001> ;
	.

attack:T1574.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1574 ;
	rdfs:label "Hijack Execution Flow: DLL Side-Loading"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may execute their own malicious payloads by side-loading DLLs. Similar to DLL, side-loading involves hijacking which DLL a program loads. But rather than just planting the DLL within the search order of a program then waiting for the victim application to be invoked, adversaries may directly side-load their payloads by planting then invoking a legitimate application that executes their payload(s). Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1574.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1574.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1574.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1574/002> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1574.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1574 ;
	rdfs:label "Hijack Execution Flow: Dylib Hijacking"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may execute their own payloads by placing a malicious dynamic library (dylib) with an expected name in a path a victim application searches at runtime. The dynamic loader will try to find the dylibs based on the sequential order of the search paths. Paths to dylibs may be prefixed with @rpath, which allows developers to use relative paths to specify an array of search paths used at runtime based on the location of the executable."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1574.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1574/004> ;
	.

attack:T1574.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1574 ;
	rdfs:label "Hijack Execution Flow: Executable Installer File Permissions Weakness"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may execute their own malicious payloads by hijacking the binaries used by an installer. These processes may automatically execute specific binaries as part of their functionality or to perform other actions. If the permissions on the file system directory containing a target binary, or permissions on the binary itself, are improperly set, then the target binary may be overwritten with another binary using user-level permissions and executed by the original process."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1574.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1574/005> ;
	.

attack:T1574.006
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1574 ;
	rdfs:label "Hijack Execution Flow: Dynamic Linker Hijacking"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may execute their own malicious payloads by hijacking environment variables the dynamic linker uses to load shared libraries. During the execution preparation phase of a program, the dynamic linker loads specified absolute paths of shared libraries from various environment variables and files, such as LD_PRELOAD on Linux or DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES on macOS."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1574.006" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1574/006> ;
	.

attack:T1574.007
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1574 ;
	rdfs:label "Hijack Execution Flow: Path Interception by PATH Environment Variable"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may execute their own malicious payloads by hijacking environment variables used to load libraries. The PATH environment variable contains a list of directories (User and System) that the OS searches sequentially through in search of the binary that was called from a script or the command line."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1574.007" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1574/007> ;
	.

attack:T1574.008
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1574 ;
	rdfs:label "Hijack Execution Flow: Path Interception by Search Order Hijacking"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may execute their own malicious payloads by hijacking the search order used to load other programs. Because some programs do not call other programs using the full path, adversaries may place their own file in the directory where the calling program is located, causing the operating system to launch their malicious software at the request of the calling program."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1574.008" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1574/008> ;
	.

attack:T1574.009
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1574 ;
	rdfs:label "Hijack Execution Flow: Path Interception by Unquoted Path"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may execute their own malicious payloads by hijacking vulnerable file path references. Adversaries can take advantage of paths that lack surrounding quotations by placing an executable in a higher level directory within the path, so that Windows will choose the adversary's executable to launch."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1574.009" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1574/009> ;
	.

attack:T1574.010
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1574 ;
	rdfs:label "Hijack Execution Flow: Services File Permissions Weakness"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may execute their own malicious payloads by hijacking the binaries used by services. Adversaries may use flaws in the permissions of Windows services to replace the binary that is executed upon service start. These service processes may automatically execute specific binaries as part of their functionality or to perform other actions."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1574.010" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1574/010> ;
	.

attack:T1574.011
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1574 ;
	rdfs:label "Hijack Execution Flow: Services Registry Permissions Weakness"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may execute their own malicious payloads by hijacking the Registry entries used by services. Flaws in the permissions for Registry keys related to services can allow adversaries to redirect the originally specified executable to one they control, launching their own code when a service starts. Windows stores local service configuration information in the Registry under HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1574.011" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1574/011> ;
	.

attack:T1574.012
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1574 ;
	rdfs:label "Hijack Execution Flow: COR_PROFILER"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may leverage the COR_PROFILER environment variable to hijack the execution flow of programs that load the .NET CLR. The COR_PROFILER is a .NET Framework feature which allows developers to specify an unmanaged (or external of .NET) profiling DLL to be loaded into each .NET process that loads the Common Language Runtime (CLR). These profilers are designed to monitor, troubleshoot, and debug managed code executed by the .NET CLR."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1574.012" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1574/012> ;
	.

attack:T1574.013
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1574 ;
	rdfs:label "Hijack Execution Flow: KernelCallbackTable"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse the KernelCallbackTable of a process to hijack its execution flow in order to run their own payloads. The KernelCallbackTable can be found in the Process Environment Block (PEB) and is initialized to an array of graphic functions available to a GUI process once user32.dll is loaded."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1574.013" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1574/013> ;
	.

attack:T1574.014
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1574 ;
	rdfs:label "Hijack Execution Flow: AppDomainManager"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may execute their own malicious payloads by hijacking how the .NET `AppDomainManager` loads assemblies. The .NET framework uses the `AppDomainManager` class to create and manage one or more isolated runtime environments (called application domains) inside a process to host the execution of .NET applications. Assemblies (`.exe` or `.dll` binaries compiled to run as .NET code) may be loaded into an application domain as executable code."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1574.014" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1574/014> ;
	.

attack:T1575
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Native API"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use Android’s Native Development Kit (NDK) to write native functions that can achieve execution of binaries or functions. Like system calls on a traditional desktop operating system, native code achieves execution on a lower level than normal Android SDK calls."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1575" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0041 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1575> ;
	.

attack:T1576
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Uninstall Malicious Application"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may include functionality in malware that uninstalls the malicious application from the device. This can be achieved by: Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1630.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1576" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1630.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-18"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1576> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1577
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Compromise Application Executable"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify applications installed on a device to establish persistent access to a victim. These malicious modifications can be used to make legitimate applications carry out adversary tasks when these applications are in use."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1577" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0028 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1577> ;
	.

attack:T1578
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Cloud Compute Infrastructure"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may attempt to modify a cloud account's compute service infrastructure to evade defenses. A modification to the compute service infrastructure can include the creation, deletion, or modification of one or more components such as compute instances, virtual machines, and snapshots."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1578" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1578> ;
	.

attack:T1578.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1578 ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Cloud Compute Infrastructure: Create Snapshot"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may create a snapshot or data backup within a cloud account to evade defenses. A snapshot is a point-in-time copy of an existing cloud compute component such as a virtual machine (VM), virtual hard drive, or volume."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1578.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1578/001> ;
	.

attack:T1578.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1578 ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Cloud Compute Infrastructure: Create Cloud Instance"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may create a new instance or virtual machine (VM) within the compute service of a cloud account to evade defenses. Creating a new instance may allow an adversary to bypass firewall rules and permissions that exist on instances currently residing within an account."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1578.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1578/002> ;
	.

attack:T1578.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1578 ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Cloud Compute Infrastructure: Delete Cloud Instance"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may delete a cloud instance after they have performed malicious activities in an attempt to evade detection and remove evidence of their presence. Deleting an instance or virtual machine can remove valuable forensic artifacts and other evidence of suspicious behavior if the instance is not recoverable."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1578.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1578/003> ;
	.

attack:T1578.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1578 ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Cloud Compute Infrastructure: Revert Cloud Instance"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may revert changes made to a cloud instance after they have performed malicious activities in attempt to evade detection and remove evidence of their presence. In highly virtualized environments, such as cloud-based infrastructure, this may be accomplished by restoring virtual machine (VM) or data storage snapshots through the cloud management dashboard or cloud APIs."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1578.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1578/004> ;
	.

attack:T1578.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1578 ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Cloud Compute Infrastructure: Modify Cloud Compute Configurations"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify settings that directly affect the size, locations, and resources available to cloud compute infrastructure in order to evade defenses. These settings may include service quotas, subscription associations, tenant-wide policies, or other configurations that impact available compute."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1578.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1578/005> ;
	.

attack:T1579
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Keychain"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may collect the keychain storage data from an iOS device to acquire credentials. Keychains are the built-in way for iOS to keep track of users' passwords and credentials for many services and features such as Wi-Fi passwords, websites, secure notes, certificates, private keys, and VPN credentials. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1634.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1579" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0031 ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1634.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1579> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1580
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Cloud Infrastructure Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may attempt to discover infrastructure and resources that are available within an infrastructure-as-a-service (IaaS) environment. This includes compute service resources such as instances, virtual machines, and snapshots as well as resources of other services including the storage and database services."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1580" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1580> ;
	.

attack:T1581
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Geofencing"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use a device’s geographical location to limit certain malicious behaviors. For example, malware operators may limit the distribution of a second stage payload to certain geographic regions. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1627.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1581" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1627.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1581> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1582
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "SMS Control"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may delete, alter, or send SMS messages without user authorization. This could be used to hide C2 SMS messages, spread malware, or various external effects."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1582" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0034 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1582> ;
	.

attack:T1583
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Acquire Infrastructure"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may buy, lease, rent, or obtain infrastructure that can be used during targeting. A wide variety of infrastructure exists for hosting and orchestrating adversary operations. Infrastructure solutions include physical or cloud servers, domains, and third-party web services. Some infrastructure providers offer free trial periods, enabling infrastructure acquisition at limited to no cost. Additionally, botnets are available for rent or purchase."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1583" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1583> ;
	.

attack:T1583.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1583 ;
	rdfs:label "Acquire Infrastructure: Domains"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may acquire domains that can be used during targeting. Domain names are the human readable names used to represent one or more IP addresses. They can be purchased or, in some cases, acquired for free."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1583.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1583/001> ;
	.

attack:T1583.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1583 ;
	rdfs:label "Acquire Infrastructure: DNS Server"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may set up their own Domain Name System (DNS) servers that can be used during targeting. During post-compromise activity, adversaries may utilize DNS traffic for various tasks, including for Command and Control (ex: Application Layer Protocol). Instead of hijacking existing DNS servers, adversaries may opt to configure and run their own DNS servers in support of operations."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1583.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1583/002> ;
	.

attack:T1583.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1583 ;
	rdfs:label "Acquire Infrastructure: Virtual Private Server"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may rent Virtual Private Servers (VPSs) that can be used during targeting. There exist a variety of cloud service providers that will sell virtual machines/containers as a service. By utilizing a VPS, adversaries can make it difficult to physically tie back operations to them. The use of cloud infrastructure can also make it easier for adversaries to rapidly provision, modify, and shut down their infrastructure."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1583.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1583/003> ;
	.

attack:T1583.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1583 ;
	rdfs:label "Acquire Infrastructure: Server"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may buy, lease, rent, or obtain physical servers that can be used during targeting. Use of servers allows an adversary to stage, launch, and execute an operation. During post-compromise activity, adversaries may utilize servers for various tasks, such as watering hole operations in Drive-by Compromise, enabling Phishing operations, or facilitating Command and Control."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1583.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1583/004> ;
	.

attack:T1583.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1583 ;
	rdfs:label "Acquire Infrastructure: Botnet"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may buy, lease, or rent a network of compromised systems that can be used during targeting. A botnet is a network of compromised systems that can be instructed to perform coordinated tasks. Adversaries may purchase a subscription to use an existing botnet from a booter/stresser service."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1583.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1583/005> ;
	.

attack:T1583.006
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1583 ;
	rdfs:label "Acquire Infrastructure: Web Services"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may register for web services that can be used during targeting. A variety of popular websites exist for adversaries to register for a web-based service that can be abused during later stages of the adversary lifecycle, such as during Command and Control (Web Service), Exfiltration Over Web Service, or Phishing. Using common services, such as those offered by Google, GitHub, or Twitter, makes it easier for adversaries to hide in expected noise."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1583.006" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1583/006> ;
	.

attack:T1583.007
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1583 ;
	rdfs:label "Acquire Infrastructure: Serverless"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may purchase and configure serverless cloud infrastructure, such as Cloudflare Workers, AWS Lambda functions, or Google Apps Scripts, that can be used during targeting. By utilizing serverless infrastructure, adversaries can make it more difficult to attribute infrastructure used during operations back to them."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1583.007" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1583/007> ;
	.

attack:T1583.008
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1583 ;
	rdfs:label "Acquire Infrastructure: Malvertising"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may purchase online advertisements that can be abused to distribute malware to victims. Ads can be purchased to plant as well as favorably position artifacts in specific locations online, such as prominently placed within search engine results. These ads may make it more difficult for users to distinguish between actual search results and advertisements."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1583.008" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1583/008> ;
	.

attack:T1584
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Compromise Infrastructure"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may compromise third-party infrastructure that can be used during targeting. Infrastructure solutions include physical or cloud servers, domains, network devices, and third-party web and DNS services. Instead of buying, leasing, or renting infrastructure an adversary may compromise infrastructure and use it during other phases of the adversary lifecycle. Additionally, adversaries may compromise numerous machines to form a botnet they can leverage."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1584" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1584> ;
	.

attack:T1584.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1584 ;
	rdfs:label "Compromise Infrastructure: Domains"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may hijack domains and/or subdomains that can be used during targeting. Domain registration hijacking is the act of changing the registration of a domain name without the permission of the original registrant. Adversaries may gain access to an email account for the person listed as the owner of the domain. The adversary can then claim that they forgot their password in order to make changes to the domain registration."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1584.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1584/001> ;
	.

attack:T1584.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1584 ;
	rdfs:label "Compromise Infrastructure: DNS Server"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may compromise third-party DNS servers that can be used during targeting. During post-compromise activity, adversaries may utilize DNS traffic for various tasks, including for Command and Control (ex: Application Layer Protocol). Instead of setting up their own DNS servers, adversaries may compromise third-party DNS servers in support of operations."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1584.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1584/002> ;
	.

attack:T1584.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1584 ;
	rdfs:label "Compromise Infrastructure: Virtual Private Server"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may compromise third-party Virtual Private Servers (VPSs) that can be used during targeting. There exist a variety of cloud service providers that will sell virtual machines/containers as a service. Adversaries may compromise VPSs purchased by third-party entities. By compromising a VPS to use as infrastructure, adversaries can make it difficult to physically tie back operations to themselves."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1584.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1584/003> ;
	.

attack:T1584.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1584 ;
	rdfs:label "Compromise Infrastructure: Server"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may compromise third-party servers that can be used during targeting. Use of servers allows an adversary to stage, launch, and execute an operation. During post-compromise activity, adversaries may utilize servers for various tasks, including for Command and Control. Instead of purchasing a Server or Virtual Private Server, adversaries may compromise third-party servers in support of operations."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1584.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1584/004> ;
	.

attack:T1584.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1584 ;
	rdfs:label "Compromise Infrastructure: Botnet"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may compromise numerous third-party systems to form a botnet that can be used during targeting. A botnet is a network of compromised systems that can be instructed to perform coordinated tasks. Instead of purchasing/renting a botnet from a booter/stresser service, adversaries may build their own botnet by compromising numerous third-party systems. Adversaries may also conduct a takeover of an existing botnet, such as redirecting bots to adversary-controlled C2 servers."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1584.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1584/005> ;
	.

attack:T1584.006
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1584 ;
	rdfs:label "Compromise Infrastructure: Web Services"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may compromise access to third-party web services that can be used during targeting. A variety of popular websites exist for legitimate users to register for web-based services, such as GitHub, Twitter, Dropbox, Google, SendGrid, etc. Adversaries may try to take ownership of a legitimate user's access to a web service and use that web service as infrastructure in support of cyber operations."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1584.006" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1584/006> ;
	.

attack:T1584.007
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1584 ;
	rdfs:label "Compromise Infrastructure: Serverless"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may compromise serverless cloud infrastructure, such as Cloudflare Workers, AWS Lambda functions, or Google Apps Scripts, that can be used during targeting. By utilizing serverless infrastructure, adversaries can make it more difficult to attribute infrastructure used during operations back to them."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1584.007" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1584/007> ;
	.

attack:T1584.008
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1584 ;
	rdfs:label "Compromise Infrastructure: Network Devices"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may compromise third-party network devices that can be used during targeting. Network devices, such as small office/home office (SOHO) routers, may be compromised where the adversary's ultimate goal is not Initial Access to that environment, but rather to leverage these devices to support additional targeting."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1584.008" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1584/008> ;
	.

attack:T1585
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Establish Accounts"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may create and cultivate accounts with services that can be used during targeting. Adversaries can create accounts that can be used to build a persona to further operations. Persona development consists of the development of public information, presence, history and appropriate affiliations."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1585" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1585> ;
	.

attack:T1585.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1585 ;
	rdfs:label "Establish Accounts: Social Media Accounts"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may create and cultivate social media accounts that can be used during targeting. Adversaries can create social media accounts that can be used to build a persona to further operations. Persona development consists of the development of public information, presence, history and appropriate affiliations."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1585.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1585/001> ;
	.

attack:T1585.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1585 ;
	rdfs:label "Establish Accounts: Email Accounts"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may create email accounts that can be used during targeting. Adversaries can use accounts created with email providers to further their operations, such as leveraging them to conduct Phishing for Information or Phishing. Establishing email accounts may also allow adversaries to abuse free services – such as trial periods – to Acquire Infrastructure for follow-on purposes."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1585.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1585/002> ;
	.

attack:T1585.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1585 ;
	rdfs:label "Establish Accounts: Cloud Accounts"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may create accounts with cloud providers that can be used during targeting. Adversaries can use cloud accounts to further their operations, including leveraging cloud storage services such as Dropbox, MEGA, Microsoft OneDrive, or AWS S3 buckets for Exfiltration to Cloud Storage or to Upload Tools. Cloud accounts can also be used in the acquisition of infrastructure, such as Virtual Private Servers or Serverless infrastructure."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1585.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1585/003> ;
	.

attack:T1586
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Compromise Accounts"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may compromise accounts with services that can be used during targeting. For operations incorporating social engineering, the utilization of an online persona may be important. Rather than creating and cultivating accounts (i.e. Establish Accounts), adversaries may compromise existing accounts. Utilizing an existing persona may engender a level of trust in a potential victim if they have a relationship, or knowledge of, the compromised persona."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1586" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1586> ;
	.

attack:T1586.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1586 ;
	rdfs:label "Compromise Accounts: Social Media Accounts"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may compromise social media accounts that can be used during targeting. For operations incorporating social engineering, the utilization of an online persona may be important. Rather than creating and cultivating social media profiles (i.e. Social Media Accounts), adversaries may compromise existing social media accounts. Utilizing an existing persona may engender a level of trust in a potential victim if they have a relationship, or knowledge of, the compromised persona."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1586.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1586/001> ;
	.

attack:T1586.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1586 ;
	rdfs:label "Compromise Accounts: Email Accounts"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may compromise email accounts that can be used during targeting. Adversaries can use compromised email accounts to further their operations, such as leveraging them to conduct Phishing for Information, Phishing, or large-scale spam email campaigns. Utilizing an existing persona with a compromised email account may engender a level of trust in a potential victim if they have a relationship with, or knowledge of, the compromised persona."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1586.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1586/002> ;
	.

attack:T1586.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1586 ;
	rdfs:label "Compromise Accounts: Cloud Accounts"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may compromise cloud accounts that can be used during targeting. Adversaries can use compromised cloud accounts to further their operations, including leveraging cloud storage services such as Dropbox, Microsoft OneDrive, or AWS S3 buckets for Exfiltration to Cloud Storage or to Upload Tools. Cloud accounts can also be used in the acquisition of infrastructure, such as Virtual Private Servers or Serverless infrastructure."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1586.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1586/003> ;
	.

attack:T1587
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Develop Capabilities"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may build capabilities that can be used during targeting. Rather than purchasing, freely downloading, or stealing capabilities, adversaries may develop their own capabilities in-house. This is the process of identifying development requirements and building solutions such as malware, exploits, and self-signed certificates. Adversaries may develop capabilities to support their operations throughout numerous phases of the adversary lifecycle."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1587" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1587> ;
	.

attack:T1587.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1587 ;
	rdfs:label "Develop Capabilities: Malware"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may develop malware and malware components that can be used during targeting. Building malicious software can include the development of payloads, droppers, post-compromise tools, backdoors (including backdoored images), packers, C2 protocols, and the creation of infected removable media. Adversaries may develop malware to support their operations, creating a means for maintaining control of remote machines, evading defenses, and executing post-compromise behaviors."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1587.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1587/001> ;
	.

attack:T1587.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1587 ;
	rdfs:label "Develop Capabilities: Code Signing Certificates"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may create self-signed code signing certificates that can be used during targeting. Code signing is the process of digitally signing executables and scripts to confirm the software author and guarantee that the code has not been altered or corrupted. Code signing provides a level of authenticity for a program from the developer and a guarantee that the program has not been tampered with."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1587.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1587/002> ;
	.

attack:T1587.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1587 ;
	rdfs:label "Develop Capabilities: Digital Certificates"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may create self-signed SSL/TLS certificates that can be used during targeting. SSL/TLS certificates are designed to instill trust. They include information about the key, information about its owner's identity, and the digital signature of an entity that has verified the certificate's contents are correct. If the signature is valid, and the person examining the certificate trusts the signer, then they know they can use that key to communicate with its owner."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1587.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1587/003> ;
	.

attack:T1587.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1587 ;
	rdfs:label "Develop Capabilities: Exploits"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may develop exploits that can be used during targeting. An exploit takes advantage of a bug or vulnerability in order to cause unintended or unanticipated behavior to occur on computer hardware or software. Rather than finding/modifying exploits from online or purchasing them from exploit vendors, an adversary may develop their own exploits. Adversaries may use information acquired via Vulnerabilities to focus exploit development efforts."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1587.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1587/004> ;
	.

attack:T1588
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Obtain Capabilities"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may buy and/or steal capabilities that can be used during targeting. Rather than developing their own capabilities in-house, adversaries may purchase, freely download, or steal them. Activities may include the acquisition of malware, software (including licenses), exploits, certificates, and information relating to vulnerabilities. Adversaries may obtain capabilities to support their operations throughout numerous phases of the adversary lifecycle."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1588" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1588> ;
	.

attack:T1588.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1588 ;
	rdfs:label "Obtain Capabilities: Malware"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may buy, steal, or download malware that can be used during targeting. Malicious software can include payloads, droppers, post-compromise tools, backdoors, packers, and C2 protocols. Adversaries may acquire malware to support their operations, obtaining a means for maintaining control of remote machines, evading defenses, and executing post-compromise behaviors."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1588.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1588/001> ;
	.

attack:T1588.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1588 ;
	rdfs:label "Obtain Capabilities: Tool"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may buy, steal, or download software tools that can be used during targeting. Tools can be open or closed source, free or commercial. A tool can be used for malicious purposes by an adversary, but (unlike malware) were not intended to be used for those purposes (ex: PsExec)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1588.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1588/002> ;
	.

attack:T1588.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1588 ;
	rdfs:label "Obtain Capabilities: Code Signing Certificates"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may buy and/or steal code signing certificates that can be used during targeting. Code signing is the process of digitally signing executables and scripts to confirm the software author and guarantee that the code has not been altered or corrupted. Code signing provides a level of authenticity for a program from the developer and a guarantee that the program has not been tampered with."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1588.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1588/003> ;
	.

attack:T1588.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1588 ;
	rdfs:label "Obtain Capabilities: Digital Certificates"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may buy and/or steal SSL/TLS certificates that can be used during targeting. SSL/TLS certificates are designed to instill trust. They include information about the key, information about its owner's identity, and the digital signature of an entity that has verified the certificate's contents are correct. If the signature is valid, and the person examining the certificate trusts the signer, then they know they can use that key to communicate with its owner."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1588.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1588/004> ;
	.

attack:T1588.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1588 ;
	rdfs:label "Obtain Capabilities: Exploits"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may buy, steal, or download exploits that can be used during targeting. An exploit takes advantage of a bug or vulnerability in order to cause unintended or unanticipated behavior to occur on computer hardware or software. Rather than developing their own exploits, an adversary may find/modify exploits from online or purchase them from exploit vendors."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1588.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1588/005> ;
	.

attack:T1588.006
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1588 ;
	rdfs:label "Obtain Capabilities: Vulnerabilities"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may acquire information about vulnerabilities that can be used during targeting. A vulnerability is a weakness in computer hardware or software that can, potentially, be exploited by an adversary to cause unintended or unanticipated behavior to occur. Adversaries may find vulnerability information by searching open databases or gaining access to closed vulnerability databases."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1588.006" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1588/006> ;
	.

attack:T1588.007
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1588 ;
	rdfs:label "Obtain Capabilities: Artificial Intelligence"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may obtain access to generative artificial intelligence tools, such as large language models (LLMs), to aid various techniques during targeting. These tools may be used to inform, bolster, and enable a variety of malicious tasks, including conducting Reconnaissance, creating basic scripts, assisting social engineering, and even developing payloads."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1588.007" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1588/007> ;
	.

attack:T1589
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Gather Victim Identity Information"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather information about the victim's identity that can be used during targeting. Information about identities may include a variety of details, including personal data (ex: employee names, email addresses, security question responses, etc.) as well as sensitive details such as credentials or multi-factor authentication (MFA) configurations."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1589" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1589> ;
	.

attack:T1589.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1589 ;
	rdfs:label "Gather Victim Identity Information: Credentials"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather credentials that can be used during targeting. Account credentials gathered by adversaries may be those directly associated with the target victim organization or attempt to take advantage of the tendency for users to use the same passwords across personal and business accounts."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1589.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1589/001> ;
	.

attack:T1589.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1589 ;
	rdfs:label "Gather Victim Identity Information: Email Addresses"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather email addresses that can be used during targeting. Even if internal instances exist, organizations may have public-facing email infrastructure and addresses for employees."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1589.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1589/002> ;
	.

attack:T1589.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1589 ;
	rdfs:label "Gather Victim Identity Information: Employee Names"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather employee names that can be used during targeting. Employee names be used to derive email addresses as well as to help guide other reconnaissance efforts and/or craft more-believable lures."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1589.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1589/003> ;
	.

attack:T1590
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Gather Victim Network Information"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather information about the victim's networks that can be used during targeting. Information about networks may include a variety of details, including administrative data (ex: IP ranges, domain names, etc.) as well as specifics regarding its topology and operations."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1590" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1590> ;
	.

attack:T1590.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1590 ;
	rdfs:label "Gather Victim Network Information: Domain Properties"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather information about the victim's network domain(s) that can be used during targeting. Information about domains and their properties may include a variety of details, including what domain(s) the victim owns as well as administrative data (ex: name, registrar, etc.) and more directly actionable information such as contacts (email addresses and phone numbers), business addresses, and name servers."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1590.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1590/001> ;
	.

attack:T1590.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1590 ;
	rdfs:label "Gather Victim Network Information: DNS"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather information about the victim's DNS that can be used during targeting. DNS information may include a variety of details, including registered name servers as well as records that outline addressing for a target’s subdomains, mail servers, and other hosts. DNS MX, TXT, and SPF records may also reveal the use of third party cloud and SaaS providers, such as Office 365, G Suite, Salesforce, or Zendesk."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1590.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1590/002> ;
	.

attack:T1590.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1590 ;
	rdfs:label "Gather Victim Network Information: Network Trust Dependencies"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather information about the victim's network trust dependencies that can be used during targeting. Information about network trusts may include a variety of details, including second or third-party organizations/domains (ex: managed service providers, contractors, etc.) that have connected (and potentially elevated) network access."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1590.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1590/003> ;
	.

attack:T1590.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1590 ;
	rdfs:label "Gather Victim Network Information: Network Topology"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather information about the victim's network topology that can be used during targeting. Information about network topologies may include a variety of details, including the physical and/or logical arrangement of both external-facing and internal network environments. This information may also include specifics regarding network devices (gateways, routers, etc.) and other infrastructure."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1590.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1590/004> ;
	.

attack:T1590.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1590 ;
	rdfs:label "Gather Victim Network Information: IP Addresses"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather the victim's IP addresses that can be used during targeting. Public IP addresses may be allocated to organizations by block, or a range of sequential addresses. Information about assigned IP addresses may include a variety of details, such as which IP addresses are in use."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1590.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1590/005> ;
	.

attack:T1590.006
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1590 ;
	rdfs:label "Gather Victim Network Information: Network Security Appliances"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather information about the victim's network security appliances that can be used during targeting. Information about network security appliances may include a variety of details, such as the existence and specifics of deployed firewalls, content filters, and proxies/bastion hosts. Adversaries may also target information about victim network-based intrusion detection systems (NIDS) or other appliances related to defensive cybersecurity operations."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1590.006" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1590/006> ;
	.

attack:T1591
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Gather Victim Org Information"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather information about the victim's organization that can be used during targeting. Information about an organization may include a variety of details, including the names of divisions/departments, specifics of business operations, as well as the roles and responsibilities of key employees."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1591" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1591> ;
	.

attack:T1591.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1591 ;
	rdfs:label "Gather Victim Org Information: Determine Physical Locations"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather the victim's physical location(s) that can be used during targeting. Information about physical locations of a target organization may include a variety of details, including where key resources and infrastructure are housed. Physical locations may also indicate what legal jurisdiction and/or authorities the victim operates within."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1591.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1591/001> ;
	.

attack:T1591.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1591 ;
	rdfs:label "Gather Victim Org Information: Business Relationships"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather information about the victim's business relationships that can be used during targeting. Information about an organization’s business relationships may include a variety of details, including second or third-party organizations/domains (ex: managed service providers, contractors, etc.) that have connected (and potentially elevated) network access. This information may also reveal supply chains and shipment paths for the victim’s hardware and software resources."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1591.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1591/002> ;
	.

attack:T1591.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1591 ;
	rdfs:label "Gather Victim Org Information: Identify Business Tempo"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather information about the victim's business tempo that can be used during targeting. Information about an organization’s business tempo may include a variety of details, including operational hours/days of the week. This information may also reveal times/dates of purchases and shipments of the victim’s hardware and software resources."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1591.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1591/003> ;
	.

attack:T1591.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1591 ;
	rdfs:label "Gather Victim Org Information: Identify Roles"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather information about identities and roles within the victim organization that can be used during targeting. Information about business roles may reveal a variety of targetable details, including identifiable information for key personnel as well as what data/resources they have access to."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1591.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1591/004> ;
	.

attack:T1592
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Gather Victim Host Information"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather information about the victim's hosts that can be used during targeting. Information about hosts may include a variety of details, including administrative data (ex: name, assigned IP, functionality, etc.) as well as specifics regarding its configuration (ex: operating system, language, etc.)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1592" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1592> ;
	.

attack:T1592.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1592 ;
	rdfs:label "Gather Victim Host Information: Hardware"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather information about the victim's host hardware that can be used during targeting. Information about hardware infrastructure may include a variety of details such as types and versions on specific hosts, as well as the presence of additional components that might be indicative of added defensive protections (ex: card/biometric readers, dedicated encryption hardware, etc.)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1592.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1592/001> ;
	.

attack:T1592.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1592 ;
	rdfs:label "Gather Victim Host Information: Software"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather information about the victim's host software that can be used during targeting. Information about installed software may include a variety of details such as types and versions on specific hosts, as well as the presence of additional components that might be indicative of added defensive protections (ex: antivirus, SIEMs, etc.)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1592.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1592/002> ;
	.

attack:T1592.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1592 ;
	rdfs:label "Gather Victim Host Information: Firmware"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather information about the victim's host firmware that can be used during targeting. Information about host firmware may include a variety of details such as type and versions on specific hosts, which may be used to infer more information about hosts in the environment (ex: configuration, purpose, age/patch level, etc.)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1592.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1592/003> ;
	.

attack:T1592.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1592 ;
	rdfs:label "Gather Victim Host Information: Client Configurations"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather information about the victim's client configurations that can be used during targeting. Information about client configurations may include a variety of details and settings, including operating system/version, virtualization, architecture (ex: 32 or 64 bit), language, and/or time zone."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1592.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1592/004> ;
	.

attack:T1593
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Search Open Websites/Domains"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may search freely available websites and/or domains for information about victims that can be used during targeting. Information about victims may be available in various online sites, such as social media, new sites, or those hosting information about business operations such as hiring or requested/rewarded contracts."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1593" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1593> ;
	.

attack:T1593.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1593 ;
	rdfs:label "Search Open Websites/Domains: Social Media"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may search social media for information about victims that can be used during targeting. Social media sites may contain various information about a victim organization, such as business announcements as well as information about the roles, locations, and interests of staff."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1593.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1593/001> ;
	.

attack:T1593.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1593 ;
	rdfs:label "Search Open Websites/Domains: Search Engines"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use search engines to collect information about victims that can be used during targeting. Search engine services typical crawl online sites to index context and may provide users with specialized syntax to search for specific keywords or specific types of content (i.e. filetypes)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1593.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1593/002> ;
	.

attack:T1593.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1593 ;
	rdfs:label "Search Open Websites/Domains: Code Repositories"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may search public code repositories for information about victims that can be used during targeting. Victims may store code in repositories on various third-party websites such as GitHub, GitLab, SourceForge, and BitBucket. Users typically interact with code repositories through a web application or command-line utilities such as git."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1593.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1593/003> ;
	.

attack:T1594
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Search Victim-Owned Websites"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may search websites owned by the victim for information that can be used during targeting. Victim-owned websites may contain a variety of details, including names of departments/divisions, physical locations, and data about key employees such as names, roles, and contact info (ex: Email Addresses). These sites may also have details highlighting business operations and relationships."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1594" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1594> ;
	.

attack:T1595
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Active Scanning"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may execute active reconnaissance scans to gather information that can be used during targeting. Active scans are those where the adversary probes victim infrastructure via network traffic, as opposed to other forms of reconnaissance that do not involve direct interaction."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1595" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1595> ;
	.

attack:T1595.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1595 ;
	rdfs:label "Active Scanning: Scanning IP Blocks"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may scan victim IP blocks to gather information that can be used during targeting. Public IP addresses may be allocated to organizations by block, or a range of sequential addresses."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1595.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1595/001> ;
	.

attack:T1595.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1595 ;
	rdfs:label "Active Scanning: Vulnerability Scanning"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may scan victims for vulnerabilities that can be used during targeting. Vulnerability scans typically check if the configuration of a target host/application (ex: software and version) potentially aligns with the target of a specific exploit the adversary may seek to use."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1595.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1595/002> ;
	.

attack:T1595.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1595 ;
	rdfs:label "Active Scanning: Wordlist Scanning"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may iteratively probe infrastructure using brute-forcing and crawling techniques. While this technique employs similar methods to Brute Force, its goal is the identification of content and infrastructure rather than the discovery of valid credentials. Wordlists used in these scans may contain generic, commonly used names and file extensions or terms specific to a particular software."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1595.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1595/003> ;
	.

attack:T1596
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Search Open Technical Databases"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may search freely available technical databases for information about victims that can be used during targeting. Information about victims may be available in online databases and repositories, such as registrations of domains/certificates as well as public collections of network data/artifacts gathered from traffic and/or scans."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1596" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1596> ;
	.

attack:T1596.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1596 ;
	rdfs:label "Search Open Technical Databases: DNS/Passive DNS"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may search DNS data for information about victims that can be used during targeting. DNS information may include a variety of details, including registered name servers as well as records that outline addressing for a target’s subdomains, mail servers, and other hosts."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1596.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1596/001> ;
	.

attack:T1596.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1596 ;
	rdfs:label "Search Open Technical Databases: WHOIS"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may search public WHOIS data for information about victims that can be used during targeting. WHOIS data is stored by regional Internet registries (RIR) responsible for allocating and assigning Internet resources such as domain names. Anyone can query WHOIS servers for information about a registered domain, such as assigned IP blocks, contact information, and DNS nameservers."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1596.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1596/002> ;
	.

attack:T1596.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1596 ;
	rdfs:label "Search Open Technical Databases: Digital Certificates"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may search public digital certificate data for information about victims that can be used during targeting. Digital certificates are issued by a certificate authority (CA) in order to cryptographically verify the origin of signed content. These certificates, such as those used for encrypted web traffic (HTTPS SSL/TLS communications), contain information about the registered organization such as name and location."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1596.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1596/003> ;
	.

attack:T1596.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1596 ;
	rdfs:label "Search Open Technical Databases: CDNs"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may search content delivery network (CDN) data about victims that can be used during targeting. CDNs allow an organization to host content from a distributed, load balanced array of servers. CDNs may also allow organizations to customize content delivery based on the requestor’s geographical region."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1596.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1596/004> ;
	.

attack:T1596.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1596 ;
	rdfs:label "Search Open Technical Databases: Scan Databases"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may search within public scan databases for information about victims that can be used during targeting. Various online services continuously publish the results of Internet scans/surveys, often harvesting information such as active IP addresses, hostnames, open ports, certificates, and even server banners."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1596.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1596/005> ;
	.

attack:T1597
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Search Closed Sources"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may search and gather information about victims from closed (e.g., paid, private, or otherwise not freely available) sources that can be used during targeting. Information about victims may be available for purchase from reputable private sources and databases, such as paid subscriptions to feeds of technical/threat intelligence data. Adversaries may also purchase information from less-reputable sources such as dark web or cybercrime blackmarkets."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1597" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1597> ;
	.

attack:T1597.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1597 ;
	rdfs:label "Search Closed Sources: Threat Intel Vendors"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may search private data from threat intelligence vendors for information that can be used during targeting. Threat intelligence vendors may offer paid feeds or portals that offer more data than what is publicly reported. Although sensitive details (such as customer names and other identifiers) may be redacted, this information may contain trends regarding breaches such as target industries, attribution claims, and successful TTPs/countermeasures."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1597.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1597/001> ;
	.

attack:T1597.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1597 ;
	rdfs:label "Search Closed Sources: Purchase Technical Data"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may purchase technical information about victims that can be used during targeting. Information about victims may be available for purchase within reputable private sources and databases, such as paid subscriptions to feeds of scan databases or other data aggregation services. Adversaries may also purchase information from less-reputable sources such as dark web or cybercrime blackmarkets."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1597.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1597/002> ;
	.

attack:T1598
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Phishing for Information"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may send phishing messages to elicit sensitive information that can be used during targeting. Phishing for information is an attempt to trick targets into divulging information, frequently credentials or other actionable information. Phishing for information is different from Phishing in that the objective is gathering data from the victim rather than executing malicious code."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1598" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1598> ;
	.

attack:T1598.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1598 ;
	rdfs:label "Phishing for Information: Spearphishing Service"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may send spearphishing messages via third-party services to elicit sensitive information that can be used during targeting. Spearphishing for information is an attempt to trick targets into divulging information, frequently credentials or other actionable information."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1598.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1598/001> ;
	.

attack:T1598.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1598 ;
	rdfs:label "Phishing for Information: Spearphishing Attachment"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may send spearphishing messages with a malicious attachment to elicit sensitive information that can be used during targeting. Spearphishing for information is an attempt to trick targets into divulging information, frequently credentials or other actionable information."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1598.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1598/002> ;
	.

attack:T1598.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1598 ;
	rdfs:label "Phishing for Information: Spearphishing Link"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may send spearphishing messages with a malicious link to elicit sensitive information that can be used during targeting. Spearphishing for information is an attempt to trick targets into divulging information, frequently credentials or other actionable information."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1598.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1598/003> ;
	.

attack:T1598.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1598 ;
	rdfs:label "Phishing for Information: Spearphishing Voice"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use voice communications to elicit sensitive information that can be used during targeting. Spearphishing for information is an attempt to trick targets into divulging information, frequently credentials or other actionable information. Spearphishing for information frequently involves social engineering techniques, such as posing as a source with a reason to collect information (ex: Impersonation) and/or creating a sense of urgency or alarm for the recipient."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1598.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1598/004> ;
	.

attack:T1599
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Network Boundary Bridging"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may bridge network boundaries by compromising perimeter network devices or internal devices responsible for network segmentation. Breaching these devices may enable an adversary to bypass restrictions on traffic routing that otherwise separate trusted and untrusted networks."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1599" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1599> ;
	.

attack:T1599.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1599 ;
	rdfs:label "Network Boundary Bridging: Network Address Translation Traversal"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may bridge network boundaries by modifying a network device’s Network Address Translation (NAT) configuration. Malicious modifications to NAT may enable an adversary to bypass restrictions on traffic routing that otherwise separate trusted and untrusted networks."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1599.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1599/001> ;
	.

attack:T1600
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Weaken Encryption"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may compromise a network device’s encryption capability in order to bypass encryption that would otherwise protect data communications."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1600" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1600> ;
	.

attack:T1600.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1600 ;
	rdfs:label "Weaken Encryption: Reduce Key Space"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may reduce the level of effort required to decrypt data transmitted over the network by reducing the cipher strength of encrypted communications."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1600.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1600/001> ;
	.

attack:T1600.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1600 ;
	rdfs:label "Weaken Encryption: Disable Crypto Hardware"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries disable a network device’s dedicated hardware encryption, which may enable them to leverage weaknesses in software encryption in order to reduce the effort involved in collecting, manipulating, and exfiltrating transmitted data."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1600.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1600/002> ;
	.

attack:T1601
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Modify System Image"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may make changes to the operating system of embedded network devices to weaken defenses and provide new capabilities for themselves. On such devices, the operating systems are typically monolithic and most of the device functionality and capabilities are contained within a single file."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1601" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1601> ;
	.

attack:T1601.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1601 ;
	rdfs:label "Modify System Image: Patch System Image"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify the operating system of a network device to introduce new capabilities or weaken existing defenses. Some network devices are built with a monolithic architecture, where the entire operating system and most of the functionality of the device is contained within a single file. Adversaries may change this file in storage, to be loaded in a future boot, or in memory during runtime."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1601.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1601/001> ;
	.

attack:T1601.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1601 ;
	rdfs:label "Modify System Image: Downgrade System Image"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may install an older version of the operating system of a network device to weaken security. Older operating system versions on network devices often have weaker encryption ciphers and, in general, fewer/less updated defensive features."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1601.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1601/002> ;
	.

attack:T1602
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Data from Configuration Repository"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may collect data related to managed devices from configuration repositories. Configuration repositories are used by management systems in order to configure, manage, and control data on remote systems. Configuration repositories may also facilitate remote access and administration of devices."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1602" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0009 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1602> ;
	.

attack:T1602.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1602 ;
	rdfs:label "Data from Configuration Repository: SNMP (MIB Dump)"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may target the Management Information Base (MIB) to collect and/or mine valuable information in a network managed using Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1602.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0009 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1602/001> ;
	.

attack:T1602.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1602 ;
	rdfs:label "Data from Configuration Repository: Network Device Configuration Dump"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may access network configuration files to collect sensitive data about the device and the network. The network configuration is a file containing parameters that determine the operation of the device. The device typically stores an in-memory copy of the configuration while operating, and a separate configuration on non-volatile storage to load after device reset."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1602.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0009 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1602/002> ;
	.

attack:T1603
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Scheduled Task/Job"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse task scheduling functionality to facilitate initial or recurring execution of malicious code. On Android and iOS, APIs and libraries exist to facilitate scheduling tasks to execute at a specified date, time, or interval."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1603" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0041 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0028 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1603> ;
	.

attack:T1604
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Proxy Through Victim"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use a compromised device as a proxy server to the Internet. By utilizing a proxy, adversaries hide the true IP address of their C2 server and associated infrastructure from the destination of the network traffic. This masquerades an adversary’s traffic as legitimate traffic originating from the compromised device, which can evade IP-based restrictions and alerts on certain services, such as bank accounts and social media websites."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1604" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1604> ;
	.

attack:T1605
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Command-Line Interface"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use built-in command-line interfaces to interact with the device and execute commands. Android provides a bash shell that can be interacted with over the Android Debug Bridge (ADB) or programmatically using Java’s `Runtime` package. On iOS, adversaries can interact with the underlying runtime shell if the device has been jailbroken. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1623.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1605" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0041 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1623.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1605> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1606
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Forge Web Credentials"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may forge credential materials that can be used to gain access to web applications or Internet services. Web applications and services (hosted in cloud SaaS environments or on-premise servers) often use session cookies, tokens, or other materials to authenticate and authorize user access."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1606" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1606> ;
	.

attack:T1606.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1606 ;
	rdfs:label "Forge Web Credentials: Web Cookies"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may forge web cookies that can be used to gain access to web applications or Internet services. Web applications and services (hosted in cloud SaaS environments or on-premise servers) often use session cookies to authenticate and authorize user access."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1606.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1606/001> ;
	.

attack:T1606.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1606 ;
	rdfs:label "Forge Web Credentials: SAML Tokens"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may forge SAML tokens with any permissions claims and lifetimes if they possess a valid SAML token-signing certificate. The default lifetime of a SAML token is one hour, but the validity period can be specified in the NotOnOrAfter value of the conditions ... element in a token. This value can be changed using the AccessTokenLifetime in a LifetimeTokenPolicy. Forged SAML tokens enable adversaries to authenticate across services that use SAML 2.0 as an SSO (single sign-on) mechanism."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1606.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1606/002> ;
	.

attack:T1608
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Stage Capabilities"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may upload, install, or otherwise set up capabilities that can be used during targeting. To support their operations, an adversary may need to take capabilities they developed (Develop Capabilities) or obtained (Obtain Capabilities) and stage them on infrastructure under their control. These capabilities may be staged on infrastructure that was previously purchased/rented by the adversary (Acquire Infrastructure) or was otherwise compromised by them (Compromise Infrastructure)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1608" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1608> ;
	.

attack:T1608.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1608 ;
	rdfs:label "Stage Capabilities: Upload Malware"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may upload malware to third-party or adversary controlled infrastructure to make it accessible during targeting. Malicious software can include payloads, droppers, post-compromise tools, backdoors, and a variety of other malicious content. Adversaries may upload malware to support their operations, such as making a payload available to a victim network to enable Ingress Tool Transfer by placing it on an Internet accessible web server."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1608.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1608/001> ;
	.

attack:T1608.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1608 ;
	rdfs:label "Stage Capabilities: Upload Tool"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may upload tools to third-party or adversary controlled infrastructure to make it accessible during targeting. Tools can be open or closed source, free or commercial. Tools can be used for malicious purposes by an adversary, but (unlike malware) were not intended to be used for those purposes (ex: PsExec)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1608.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1608/002> ;
	.

attack:T1608.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1608 ;
	rdfs:label "Stage Capabilities: Install Digital Certificate"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may install SSL/TLS certificates that can be used during targeting. SSL/TLS certificates are files that can be installed on servers to enable secure communications between systems. Digital certificates include information about the key, information about its owner's identity, and the digital signature of an entity that has verified the certificate's contents are correct."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1608.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1608/003> ;
	.

attack:T1608.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1608 ;
	rdfs:label "Stage Capabilities: Drive-by Target"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may prepare an operational environment to infect systems that visit a website over the normal course of browsing. Endpoint systems may be compromised through browsing to adversary controlled sites, as in Drive-by Compromise. In such cases, the user's web browser is typically targeted for exploitation (often not requiring any extra user interaction once landing on the site), but adversaries may also set up websites for non-exploitation behavior such as Application Access Token."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1608.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1608/004> ;
	.

attack:T1608.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1608 ;
	rdfs:label "Stage Capabilities: Link Target"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may put in place resources that are referenced by a link that can be used during targeting. An adversary may rely upon a user clicking a malicious link in order to divulge information (including credentials) or to gain execution, as in Malicious Link. Links can be used for spearphishing, such as sending an email accompanied by social engineering text to coax the user to actively click or copy and paste a URL into a browser."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1608.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1608/005> ;
	.

attack:T1608.006
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1608 ;
	rdfs:label "Stage Capabilities: SEO Poisoning"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may poison mechanisms that influence search engine optimization (SEO) to further lure staged capabilities towards potential victims. Search engines typically display results to users based on purchased ads as well as the site’s ranking/score/reputation calculated by their web crawlers and algorithms."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1608.006" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1608/006> ;
	.

attack:T1609
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Container Administration Command"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse a container administration service to execute commands within a container. A container administration service such as the Docker daemon, the Kubernetes API server, or the kubelet may allow remote management of containers within an environment."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1609" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1609> ;
	.

attack:T1610
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Deploy Container"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may deploy a container into an environment to facilitate execution or evade defenses. In some cases, adversaries may deploy a new container to execute processes associated with a particular image or deployment, such as processes that execute or download malware. In others, an adversary may deploy a new container configured without network rules, user limitations, etc. to bypass existing defenses within the environment."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1610" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1610> ;
	.

attack:T1611
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Escape to Host"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may break out of a container or virtualized environment to gain access to the underlying host. This can allow an adversary access to other containerized or virtualized resources from the host level or to the host itself. In principle, containerized / virtualized resources should provide a clear separation of application functionality and be isolated from the host environment."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1611" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0004 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1611> ;
	.

attack:T1612
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Build Image on Host"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may build a container image directly on a host to bypass defenses that monitor for the retrieval of malicious images from a public registry. A remote build request may be sent to the Docker API that includes a Dockerfile that pulls a vanilla base image, such as alpine, from a public or local registry and then builds a custom image upon it."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1612" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1612> ;
	.

attack:T1613
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Container and Resource Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to discover containers and other resources that are available within a containers environment. Other resources may include images, deployments, pods, nodes, and other information such as the status of a cluster."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1613" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1613> ;
	.

attack:T1614
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "System Location Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather information in an attempt to calculate the geographical location of a victim host. Adversaries may use the information from System Location Discovery during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not the adversary fully infects the target and/or attempts specific actions."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1614" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1614> ;
	.

attack:T1614.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1614 ;
	rdfs:label "System Location Discovery: System Language Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to gather information about the system language of a victim in order to infer the geographical location of that host. This information may be used to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether the adversary infects the target and/or attempts specific actions. This decision may be employed by malware developers and operators to reduce their risk of attracting the attention of specific law enforcement agencies or prosecution/scrutiny from other entities."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1614.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1614/001> ;
	.

attack:T1615
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Group Policy Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gather information on Group Policy settings to identify paths for privilege escalation, security measures applied within a domain, and to discover patterns in domain objects that can be manipulated or used to blend in the environment. Group Policy allows for centralized management of user and computer settings in Active Directory (AD)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1615" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1615> ;
	.

attack:T1616
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Call Control"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may make, forward, or block phone calls without user authorization. This could be used for adversary goals such as audio surveillance, blocking or forwarding calls from the device owner, or C2 communication."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1616" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0035 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0034 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0037 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1616> ;
	.

attack:T1617
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Hooking"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may utilize hooking to hide the presence of artifacts associated with their behaviors to evade detection. Hooking can be used to modify return values or data structures of system APIs and function calls. This process typically involves using 3rd party root frameworks, such as Xposed or Magisk, with either a system exploit or pre-existing root access."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1617" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1617> ;
	.

attack:T1618
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "User Evasion"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to avoid detection by hiding malicious behavior from the user. By doing this, an adversary’s modifications would most likely remain installed on the device for longer, allowing the adversary to continue to operate on that device. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1628.002."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1618" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1628.002 ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1618> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1619
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Cloud Storage Object Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may enumerate objects in cloud storage infrastructure. Adversaries may use this information during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including requesting all or specific objects from cloud storage. Similar to File and Directory Discovery on a local host, after identifying available storage services (i.e. Cloud Infrastructure Discovery) adversaries may access the contents/objects stored in cloud infrastructure."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1619" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1619> ;
	.

attack:T1620
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Reflective Code Loading"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may reflectively load code into a process in order to conceal the execution of malicious payloads. Reflective loading involves allocating then executing payloads directly within the memory of the process, vice creating a thread or process backed by a file path on disk (e.g., Shared Modules)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1620" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1620> ;
	.

attack:T1621
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Multi-Factor Authentication Request Generation"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to bypass multi-factor authentication (MFA) mechanisms and gain access to accounts by generating MFA requests sent to users."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1621" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1621> ;
	.

attack:T1622
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Debugger Evasion"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may employ various means to detect and avoid debuggers. Debuggers are typically used by defenders to trace and/or analyze the execution of potential malware payloads."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1622" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1622> ;
	.

attack:T1623
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Command and Scripting Interpreter"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse command and script interpreters to execute commands, scripts, or binaries. These interfaces and languages provide ways of interacting with computer systems and are a common feature across many different platforms. Most systems come with some built-in command-line interface and scripting capabilities, for example, Android is a UNIX-like OS and includes a basic Unix Shell that can be accessed via the Android Debug Bridge (ADB) or Java’s `Runtime` package."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1623" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0041 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1623> ;
	.

attack:T1623.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1623 ;
	rdfs:label "Command and Scripting Interpreter: Unix Shell"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse Unix shell commands and scripts for execution. Unix shells are the underlying command prompts on Android and iOS devices. Unix shells can control every aspect of a system, with certain commands requiring elevated privileges that are only accessible if the device has been rooted or jailbroken."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1623.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0041 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1623/001> ;
	.

attack:T1624
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Event Triggered Execution"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may establish persistence using system mechanisms that trigger execution based on specific events. Mobile operating systems have means to subscribe to events such as receiving an SMS message, device boot completion, or other device activities."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1624" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0028 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1624> ;
	.

attack:T1624.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1624 ;
	rdfs:label "Event Triggered Execution: Broadcast Receivers"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may establish persistence using system mechanisms that trigger execution based on specific events. Mobile operating systems have means to subscribe to events such as receiving an SMS message, device boot completion, or other device activities."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1624.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0028 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1624/001> ;
	.

attack:T1625
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Hijack Execution Flow"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may execute their own malicious payloads by hijacking the way operating systems run applications. Hijacking execution flow can be for the purposes of persistence since this hijacked execution may reoccur over time."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1625" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0028 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1625> ;
	.

attack:T1625.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1625 ;
	rdfs:label "Hijack Execution Flow: System Runtime API Hijacking"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may execute their own malicious payloads by hijacking the way an operating system runs applications. Hijacking execution flow can be for the purposes of persistence since this hijacked execution may reoccur at later points in time."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1625.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0028 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1625/001> ;
	.

attack:T1626
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Abuse Elevation Control Mechanism"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may circumvent mechanisms designed to control elevated privileges to gain higher-level permissions. Most modern systems contain native elevation control mechanisms that are intended to limit privileges that a user can gain on a machine. Authorization has to be granted to specific users in order to perform tasks that are designated as higher risk. An adversary can use several methods to take advantage of built-in control mechanisms in order to escalate privileges on a system."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1626" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0029 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1626> ;
	.

attack:T1626.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1626 ;
	rdfs:label "Abuse Elevation Control Mechanism: Device Administrator Permissions"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse Android’s device administration API to obtain a higher degree of control over the device. By abusing the API, adversaries can perform several nefarious actions, such as resetting the device’s password for Endpoint Denial of Service, factory resetting the device for File Deletion and to delete any traces of the malware, disabling all the device’s cameras, or to make it more difficult to uninstall the app."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1626.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0029 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1626/001> ;
	.

attack:T1627
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Execution Guardrails"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use execution guardrails to constrain execution or actions based on adversary supplied and environment specific conditions that are expected to be present on the target. Guardrails ensure that a payload only executes against an intended target and reduces collateral damage from an adversary’s campaign. Values an adversary can provide about a target system or environment to use as guardrails may include environment information such as location."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1627" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1627> ;
	.

attack:T1627.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1627 ;
	rdfs:label "Execution Guardrails: Geofencing"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use a device’s geographical location to limit certain malicious behaviors. For example, malware operators may limit the distribution of a second stage payload to certain geographic regions."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1627.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1627/001> ;
	.

attack:T1628
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Hide Artifacts"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to hide artifacts associated with their behaviors to evade detection. Mobile operating systems have features and developer APIs to hide various artifacts, such as an application’s launcher icon. These APIs have legitimate usages, such as hiding an icon to avoid application drawer clutter when an application does not have a usable interface. Adversaries may abuse these features and APIs to hide artifacts from the user to evade detection."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1628" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1628> ;
	.

attack:T1628.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1628 ;
	rdfs:label "Hide Artifacts: Suppress Application Icon"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "A malicious application could suppress its icon from being displayed to the user in the application launcher. This hides the fact that it is installed, and can make it more difficult for the user to uninstall the application. Hiding the application's icon programmatically does not require any special permissions."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1628.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1628/001> ;
	.

attack:T1628.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1628 ;
	rdfs:label "Hide Artifacts: User Evasion"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to avoid detection by hiding malicious behavior from the user. By doing this, an adversary’s modifications would most likely remain installed on the device for longer, allowing the adversary to continue to operate on that device."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1628.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1628/002> ;
	.

attack:T1628.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1628 ;
	rdfs:label "Hide Artifacts: Conceal Multimedia Files"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to hide multimedia files from the user. By doing so, adversaries may conceal captured files, such as pictures, videos and/or screenshots, then later exfiltrate those files."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1628.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2024-04-17"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1628/003> ;
	.

attack:T1629
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Impair Defenses"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may maliciously modify components of a victim environment in order to hinder or disable defensive mechanisms. This not only involves impairing preventative defenses, such as anti-virus, but also detection capabilities that defenders can use to audit activity and identify malicious behavior. This may span both native defenses as well as supplemental capabilities installed by users or mobile endpoint administrators."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1629" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1629> ;
	.

attack:T1629.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1629 ;
	rdfs:label "Impair Defenses: Prevent Application Removal"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse the Android device administration API to prevent the user from uninstalling a target application. In earlier versions of Android, device administrator applications needed their administration capabilities explicitly deactivated by the user before the application could be uninstalled. This was later updated so the user could deactivate and uninstall the administrator application in one step."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1629.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1629/001> ;
	.

attack:T1629.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1629 ;
	rdfs:label "Impair Defenses: Device Lockout"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may seek to inhibit user interaction by locking the legitimate user out of the device. This is typically accomplished by requesting device administrator permissions and then locking the screen using `DevicePolicyManager.lockNow()`. Other novel techniques for locking the user out of the device have been observed, such as showing a persistent overlay, using carefully crafted “call” notification screens, and locking HTML pages in the foreground."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1629.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1629/002> ;
	.

attack:T1629.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1629 ;
	rdfs:label "Impair Defenses: Disable or Modify Tools"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may disable security tools to avoid potential detection of their tools and activities. This can take the form of disabling security software, modifying SELinux configuration, or other methods to interfere with security tools scanning or reporting information. This is typically done by abusing device administrator permissions or using system exploits to gain root access to the device to modify protected system files."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1629.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1629/003> ;
	.

attack:T1630
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Indicator Removal on Host"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may delete, alter, or hide generated artifacts on a device, including files, jailbreak status, or the malicious application itself. These actions may interfere with event collection, reporting, or other notifications used to detect intrusion activity. This may compromise the integrity of mobile security solutions by causing notable events or information to go unreported."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1630" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1630> ;
	.

attack:T1630.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1630 ;
	rdfs:label "Indicator Removal on Host: Uninstall Malicious Application"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may include functionality in malware that uninstalls the malicious application from the device. This can be achieved by:"@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1630.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1630/001> ;
	.

attack:T1630.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1630 ;
	rdfs:label "Indicator Removal on Host: File Deletion"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may wipe a device or delete individual files in order to manipulate external outcomes or hide activity. An application must have administrator access to fully wipe the device, while individual files may not require special permissions to delete depending on their storage location."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1630.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1630/002> ;
	.

attack:T1630.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1630 ;
	rdfs:label "Indicator Removal on Host: Disguise Root/Jailbreak Indicators"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary could use knowledge of the techniques used by security software to evade detection. For example, some mobile security products perform compromised device detection by searching for particular artifacts such as an installed \"su\" binary, but that check could be evaded by naming the binary something else. Similarly, polymorphic code techniques could be used to evade signature-based detection."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1630.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1630/003> ;
	.

attack:T1631
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Process Injection"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may inject code into processes in order to evade process-based defenses or even elevate privileges. Process injection is a method of executing arbitrary code in the address space of a separate live process. Running code in the context of another process may allow access to the process's memory, system/network resources, and possibly elevated privileges."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1631" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0029 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1631> ;
	.

attack:T1631.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1631 ;
	rdfs:label "Process Injection: Ptrace System Calls"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may inject malicious code into processes via ptrace (process trace) system calls in order to evade process-based defenses as well as possibly elevate privileges. Ptrace system call injection is a method of executing arbitrary code in the address space of a separate live process."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1631.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0029 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1631/001> ;
	.

attack:T1632
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Subvert Trust Controls"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may undermine security controls that will either warn users of untrusted activity or prevent execution of untrusted applications. Operating systems and security products may contain mechanisms to identify programs or websites as possessing some level of trust."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1632" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1632> ;
	.

attack:T1632.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1632 ;
	rdfs:label "Subvert Trust Controls: Code Signing Policy Modification"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify code signing policies to enable execution of applications signed with unofficial or unknown keys. Code signing provides a level of authenticity on an app from a developer, guaranteeing that the program has not been tampered with and comes from an official source. Security controls can include enforcement mechanisms to ensure that only valid, signed code can be run on a device."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1632.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1632/001> ;
	.

attack:T1633
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may employ various means to detect and avoid virtualization and analysis environments. This may include changing behaviors after checking for the presence of artifacts indicative of a virtual machine environment (VME) or sandbox. If the adversary detects a VME, they may alter their malware’s behavior to disengage from the victim or conceal the core functions of the payload. They may also search for VME artifacts before dropping further payloads."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1633" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1633> ;
	.

attack:T1633.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1633 ;
	rdfs:label "Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion: System Checks"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may employ various system checks to detect and avoid virtualization and analysis environments. This may include changing behavior after checking for the presence of artifacts indicative of a virtual environment or sandbox. If the adversary detects a virtual environment, they may alter their malware’s behavior to disengage from the victim or conceal the core functions of the implant. They may also search for virtualization artifacts before dropping secondary or additional payloads."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1633.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1633/001> ;
	.

attack:T1634
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Credentials from Password Store"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may search common password storage locations to obtain user credentials. Passwords can be stored in several places on a device, depending on the operating system or application holding the credentials. There are also specific applications that store passwords to make it easier for users to manage and maintain. Once credentials are obtained, they can be used to perform lateral movement and access restricted information."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1634" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0031 ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1634> ;
	.

attack:T1634.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1634 ;
	rdfs:label "Credentials from Password Store: Keychain"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may collect keychain data from an iOS device to acquire credentials. Keychains are the built-in way for iOS to keep track of users' passwords and credentials for many services and features such as Wi-Fi passwords, websites, secure notes, certificates, private keys, and VPN credentials."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1634.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0031 ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1634/001> ;
	.

attack:T1635
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Steal Application Access Token"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries can steal user application access tokens as a means of acquiring credentials to access remote systems and resources. This can occur through social engineering or URI hijacking and typically requires user action to grant access, such as through a system “Open With” dialogue."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1635" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0031 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1635> ;
	.

attack:T1635.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1635 ;
	rdfs:label "Steal Application Access Token: URI Hijacking"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may register Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs) to intercept sensitive data."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1635.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0031 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1635/001> ;
	.

attack:T1636
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Protected User Data"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may utilize standard operating system APIs to collect data from permission-backed data stores on a device, such as the calendar or contact list. These permissions need to be declared ahead of time. On Android, they must be included in the application’s manifest. On iOS, they must be included in the application’s `Info.plist` file."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1636" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0035 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1636> ;
	.

attack:T1636.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1636 ;
	rdfs:label "Protected User Data: Calendar Entries"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may utilize standard operating system APIs to gather calendar entry data. On Android, this can be accomplished using the Calendar Content Provider. On iOS, this can be accomplished using the `EventKit` framework."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1636.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0035 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1636/001> ;
	.

attack:T1636.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1636 ;
	rdfs:label "Protected User Data: Call Log"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may utilize standard operating system APIs to gather call log data. On Android, this can be accomplished using the Call Log Content Provider. iOS provides no standard API to access the call log."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1636.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0035 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1636/002> ;
	.

attack:T1636.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1636 ;
	rdfs:label "Protected User Data: Contact List"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may utilize standard operating system APIs to gather contact list data. On Android, this can be accomplished using the Contacts Content Provider. On iOS, this can be accomplished using the `Contacts` framework."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1636.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0035 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1636/003> ;
	.

attack:T1636.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1636 ;
	rdfs:label "Protected User Data: SMS Messages"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may utilize standard operating system APIs to gather SMS messages. On Android, this can be accomplished using the SMS Content Provider. iOS provides no standard API to access SMS messages."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1636.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0035 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1636/004> ;
	.

attack:T1636.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1636 ;
	rdfs:label "Protected User Data: Accounts"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may utilize standard operating system APIs to gather account data. On Android, this can be accomplished by using the AccountManager API. For example, adversaries may use the `getAccounts()` method to list all accounts. On iOS, this can be accomplished by using the Keychain services."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1636.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0035 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1636/005> ;
	.

attack:T1637
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Dynamic Resolution"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may dynamically establish connections to command and control infrastructure to evade common detections and remediations. This may be achieved by using malware that shares a common algorithm with the infrastructure the adversary uses to receive the malware's communications. This algorithm can be used to dynamically adjust parameters such as the domain name, IP address, or port number the malware uses for command and control."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1637" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0037 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1637> ;
	.

attack:T1637.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1637 ;
	rdfs:label "Dynamic Resolution: Domain Generation Algorithms"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use Domain Generation Algorithms (DGAs) to procedurally generate domain names for uses such as command and control communication or malicious application distribution."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1637.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0037 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1637/001> ;
	.

attack:T1638
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Adversary-in-the-Middle"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to position themselves between two or more networked devices to support follow-on behaviors such as Transmitted Data Manipulation or Endpoint Denial of Service."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1638" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0035 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1638> ;
	.

attack:T1639
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Exfiltration Over Alternative Protocol"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may steal data by exfiltrating it over a different protocol than that of the existing command and control channel. The data may also be sent to an alternate network location from the main command and control server."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1639" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0036 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1639> ;
	.

attack:T1639.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1639 ;
	rdfs:label "Exfiltration Over Alternative Protocol: Exfiltration Over Unencrypted Non-C2 Protocol"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may steal data by exfiltrating it over an un-encrypted network protocol other than that of the existing command and control channel. The data may also be sent to an alternate network location from the main command and control server."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1639.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0036 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1639/001> ;
	.

attack:T1640
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Account Access Removal"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may interrupt availability of system and network resources by inhibiting access to accounts utilized by legitimate users. Accounts may be deleted, locked, or manipulated (ex: credentials changed) to remove access to accounts."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1640" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0034 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1640> ;
	.

attack:T1641
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Data Manipulation"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may insert, delete, or alter data in order to manipulate external outcomes or hide activity. By manipulating data, adversaries may attempt to affect a business process, organizational understanding, or decision making."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1641" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0034 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1641> ;
	.

attack:T1641.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1641 ;
	rdfs:label "Data Manipulation: Transmitted Data Manipulation"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may alter data en route to storage or other systems in order to manipulate external outcomes or hide activity. By manipulating transmitted data, adversaries may attempt to affect a business process, organizational understanding, or decision making."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1641.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0034 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1641/001> ;
	.

attack:T1642
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Endpoint Denial of Service"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may perform Endpoint Denial of Service (DoS) attacks to degrade or block the availability of services to users."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1642" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0034 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1642> ;
	.

attack:T1643
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Generate Traffic from Victim"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may generate outbound traffic from devices. This is typically performed to manipulate external outcomes, such as to achieve carrier billing fraud or to manipulate app store rankings or ratings. Outbound traffic is typically generated as SMS messages or general web traffic, but may take other forms as well."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1643" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0034 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1643> ;
	.

attack:T1644
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Out of Band Data"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may communicate with compromised devices using out of band data streams. This could be done for a variety of reasons, including evading network traffic monitoring, as a backup method of command and control, or for data exfiltration if the device is not connected to any Internet-providing networks (i.e. cellular or Wi-Fi). Several out of band data streams exist, such as SMS messages, NFC, and Bluetooth."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1644" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0037 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1644> ;
	.

attack:T1645
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Compromise Client Software Binary"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify system software binaries to establish persistent access to devices. System software binaries are used by the underlying operating system and users over adb or terminal emulators."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1645" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0028 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1645> ;
	.

attack:T1646
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Exfiltration Over C2 Channel"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may steal data by exfiltrating it over an existing command and control channel. Stolen data is encoded into the normal communications channel using the same protocol as command and control communications."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1646" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0036 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1646> ;
	.

attack:T1647
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Plist File Modification"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may modify property list files (plist files) to enable other malicious activity, while also potentially evading and bypassing system defenses. macOS applications use plist files, such as the info.plist file, to store properties and configuration settings that inform the operating system how to handle the application at runtime. Plist files are structured metadata in key-value pairs formatted in XML based on Apple's Core Foundation DTD."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1647" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1647> ;
	.

attack:T1648
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Serverless Execution"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse serverless computing, integration, and automation services to execute arbitrary code in cloud environments. Many cloud providers offer a variety of serverless resources, including compute engines, application integration services, and web servers."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1648" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1648> ;
	.

attack:T1649
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Steal or Forge Authentication Certificates"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may steal or forge certificates used for authentication to access remote systems or resources. Digital certificates are often used to sign and encrypt messages and/or files. Certificates are also used as authentication material. For example, Entra ID device certificates and Active Directory Certificate Services (AD CS) certificates bind to an identity and can be used as credentials for domain accounts."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1649" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0006 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-08-04"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1649> ;
	.

attack:T1650
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Acquire Access"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may purchase or otherwise acquire an existing access to a target system or network. A variety of online services and initial access broker networks are available to sell access to previously compromised systems. In some cases, adversary groups may form partnerships to share compromised systems with each other."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1650" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1650> ;
	.

attack:T1651
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Cloud Administration Command"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse cloud management services to execute commands within virtual machines. Resources such as AWS Systems Manager, Azure RunCommand, and Runbooks allow users to remotely run scripts in virtual machines by leveraging installed virtual machine agents."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1651" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1651> ;
	.

attack:T1652
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Device Driver Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to enumerate local device drivers on a victim host. Information about device drivers may highlight various insights that shape follow-on behaviors, such as the function/purpose of the host, present security tools (i.e. Security Software Discovery) or other defenses (e.g., Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion), as well as potential exploitable vulnerabilities (e.g., Exploitation for Privilege Escalation)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1652" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1652> ;
	.

attack:T1653
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Power Settings"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may impair a system's ability to hibernate, reboot, or shut down in order to extend access to infected machines. When a computer enters a dormant state, some or all software and hardware may cease to operate which can disrupt malicious activity."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1653" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1653> ;
	.

attack:T1654
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Log Enumeration"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may enumerate system and service logs to find useful data. These logs may highlight various types of valuable insights for an adversary, such as user authentication records (Account Discovery), security or vulnerable software (Software Discovery), or hosts within a compromised network (Remote System Discovery)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1654" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1654> ;
	.

attack:T1655
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Masquerading"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to manipulate features of their artifacts to make them appear legitimate or benign to users and/or security tools. Masquerading occurs when the name, location, or appearance of an object, legitimate or malicious, is manipulated or abused for the sake of evading defenses and observation. This may include manipulating file metadata, tricking users into misidentifying the file type, and giving legitimate task or service names."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1655" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1655> ;
	.

attack:T1655.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1655 ;
	rdfs:label "Masquerading: Match Legitimate Name or Location"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may match or approximate the name or location of legitimate files or resources when naming/placing them. This is done for the sake of evading defenses and observation. This may be done by giving artifacts the name and icon of a legitimate, trusted application (i.e., Settings), or using a package name that matches legitimate, trusted applications (i.e., `com.google.android.gm`)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1655.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1655/001> ;
	.

attack:T1656
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Impersonation"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may impersonate a trusted person or organization in order to persuade and trick a target into performing some action on their behalf. For example, adversaries may communicate with victims (via Phishing for Information, Phishing, or Internal Spearphishing) while impersonating a known sender such as an executive, colleague, or third-party vendor. Established trust can then be leveraged to accomplish an adversary’s ultimate goals, possibly against multiple victims. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1684.001."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1656" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1684.001 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1656> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1657
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Financial Theft"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may steal monetary resources from targets through extortion, social engineering, technical theft, or other methods aimed at their own financial gain at the expense of the availability of these resources for victims. Financial theft is the ultimate objective of several popular campaign types including extortion by ransomware, business email compromise (BEC) and fraud, \"pig butchering,\" bank hacking, and exploiting cryptocurrency networks."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1657" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0040 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1657> ;
	.

attack:T1658
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Exploitation for Client Execution"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may exploit software vulnerabilities in client applications to execute code. Vulnerabilities can exist in software due to insecure coding practices that can lead to unanticipated behavior. Adversaries may take advantage of certain vulnerabilities through targeted exploitation for the purpose of arbitrary code execution."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1658" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0041 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2023-09-28"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1658> ;
	.

attack:T1659
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Content Injection"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gain access and continuously communicate with victims by injecting malicious content into systems through online network traffic. Rather than luring victims to malicious payloads hosted on a compromised website (i.e., Drive-by Target followed by Drive-by Compromise), adversaries may initially access victims through compromised data-transfer channels where they can manipulate traffic and/or inject their own content."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1659" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0001 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1659> ;
	.

attack:T1660
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Phishing"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may send malicious content to users in order to gain access to their mobile devices. All forms of phishing are electronically delivered social engineering."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1660" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0027 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1660> ;
	.

attack:T1661
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Application Versioning"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may push an update to a previously benign application to add malicious code. This can be accomplished by pushing an initially benign, functional application to a trusted application store, such as the Google Play Store or the Apple App Store. This allows the adversary to establish a trusted userbase that may grant permissions to the application prior to the introduction of malicious code. Then, an application update could be pushed to introduce malicious code."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1661" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0027 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2023-09-28"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1661> ;
	.

attack:T1662
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Data Destruction"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may destroy data and files on specific devices or in large numbers to interrupt availability to systems, services, and network resources. Data destruction is likely to render stored data irrecoverable by forensic techniques through overwriting files or data on local and remote drives."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1662" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0034 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1662> ;
	.

attack:T1663
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Remote Access Software"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use legitimate remote access software, such as `VNC`, `TeamViewer`, `AirDroid`, `AirMirror`, etc., to establish an interactive command and control channel to target mobile devices."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1663" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0037 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1663> ;
	.

attack:T1664
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Exploitation for Initial Access"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may exploit software vulnerabilities to gain initial access to a mobile device."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1664" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0027 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-02-27"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1664> ;
	.

attack:T1665
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Hide Infrastructure"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may manipulate network traffic in order to hide and evade detection of their C2 infrastructure. This can be accomplished by identifying and filtering traffic from defensive tools, masking malicious domains to obfuscate the true destination from both automated scanning tools and security researchers, and otherwise hiding malicious artifacts to delay discovery and prolong the effectiveness of adversary infrastructure that could otherwise be identified, blocked, or taken down entirely."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1665" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0011 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1665> ;
	.

attack:T1666
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Cloud Resource Hierarchy"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may attempt to modify hierarchical structures in infrastructure-as-a-service (IaaS) environments in order to evade defenses."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1666" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1666> ;
	.

attack:T1667
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Email Bombing"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may flood targeted email addresses with an overwhelming volume of messages. This may bury legitimate emails in a flood of spam and disrupt business operations."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1667" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0040 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1667> ;
	.

attack:T1668
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Exclusive Control"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries who successfully compromise a system may attempt to maintain persistence by “closing the door” behind them – in other words, by preventing other threat actors from initially accessing or maintaining a foothold on the same system."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1668" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1668> ;
	.

attack:T1669
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Wi-Fi Networks"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may gain initial access to target systems by connecting to wireless networks. They may accomplish this by exploiting open Wi-Fi networks used by target devices or by accessing secured Wi-Fi networks — requiring Valid Accounts — belonging to a target organization. Establishing a connection to a Wi-Fi access point requires a certain level of proximity to both discover and maintain a stable network connection."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1669" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0001 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1669> ;
	.

attack:T1670
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Virtualization Solution"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may carry out malicious operations using virtualization solutions to escape from Android sandboxes and to avoid detection. Android uses sandboxes to separate resources and code execution between applications and the operating system. There are a few virtualization solutions available on Android, such as the Android Virtualization Framework (AVF)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1670" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0030 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-03-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1670> ;
	.

attack:T1671
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Cloud Application Integration"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may achieve persistence by leveraging OAuth application integrations in a software-as-a-service environment. Adversaries may create a custom application, add a legitimate application into the environment, or even co-opt an existing integration to achieve malicious ends."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1671" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0003 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1671> ;
	.

attack:T1672
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Email Spoofing"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may fake, or spoof, a sender’s identity by modifying the value of relevant email headers in order to establish contact with victims under false pretenses. In addition to actual email content, email headers (such as the FROM header, which contains the email address of the sender) may also be modified. Email clients display these headers when emails appear in a victim's inbox, which may cause modified emails to appear as if they were from the spoofed entity. Revoked by MITRE ATT&CK; superseded by T1684.002."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1672" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:isReplacedBy attack:T1684.002 ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1672> ;
	owl:deprecated true ;
	.

attack:T1673
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Virtual Machine Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may attempt to enumerate running virtual machines (VMs) after gaining access to a host or hypervisor. For example, adversaries may enumerate a list of VMs on an ESXi hypervisor using a Hypervisor CLI such as `esxcli` or `vim-cmd` (e.g. `esxcli vm process list or vim-cmd vmsvc/getallvms`). Adversaries may also directly leverage a graphical user interface, such as VMware vCenter, in order to view virtual machines on a host."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1673" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1673> ;
	.

attack:T1674
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Input Injection"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may simulate keystrokes on a victim’s computer by various means to perform any type of action on behalf of the user, such as launching the command interpreter using keyboard shortcuts, typing an inline script to be executed, or interacting directly with a GUI-based application. These actions can be preprogrammed into adversary tooling or executed through physical devices such as Human Interface Devices (HIDs)."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1674" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1674> ;
	.

attack:T1675
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "ESXi Administration Command"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse ESXi administration services to execute commands on guest machines hosted within an ESXi virtual environment. Persistent background services on ESXi-hosted VMs, such as the VMware Tools Daemon Service, allow for remote management from the ESXi server. The tools daemon service runs as `vmtoolsd.exe` on Windows guest operating systems, `vmware-tools-daemon` on macOS, and `vmtoolsd ` on Linux."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1675" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1675> ;
	.

attack:T1676
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Linked Devices"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse the “linked devices” feature on messaging applications, such as Signal and WhatsApp, to register the user’s account to an adversary-controlled device. By abusing the “linked devices” feature, adversaries may achieve and maintain persistence through the user’s account, may collect information, such as the user’s messages and contacts list, and may send future messages from the linked device."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1676" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0035 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0028 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Android" ;
	dcterms:coverage "iOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/mobile/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-05-19"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1676> ;
	.

attack:T1677
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Poisoned Pipeline Execution"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may manipulate continuous integration / continuous development (CI/CD) processes by injecting malicious code into the build process. There are several mechanisms for poisoning pipelines:"@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1677" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0002 ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1677> ;
	.

attack:T1678
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Delay Execution"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may employ various time-based methods to evade detection and analysis. These techniques often exploit system clocks, delays, or timing mechanisms to obscure malicious activity, blend in with benign activity, and avoid scrutiny. Adversaries can perform this behavior within virtualization/sandbox environments or natively on host systems."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1678" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1678> ;
	.

attack:T1679
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Selective Exclusion"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may intentionally exclude certain files, folders, directories, file types, or system components from encryption or tampering during a ransomware or malicious payload execution. Some file extensions that adversaries may avoid encrypting include `.dll`, `.exe`, and `.lnk`."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1679" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1679> ;
	.

attack:T1680
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Local Storage Discovery"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may enumerate local drives, disks, and/or volumes and their attributes like total or free space and volume serial number. This can be done to prepare for ransomware-related encryption, to perform Lateral Movement, or as a precursor to Direct Volume Access."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1680" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0007 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1680> ;
	.

attack:T1681
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Search Threat Vendor Data"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Threat actors may seek information/indicators from closed or open threat intelligence sources gathered about their own campaigns, as well as those conducted by other adversaries that may align with their target industries, capabilities/objectives, or other operational concerns. These reports may include descriptions of behavior, detailed breakdowns of attacks, atomic indicators such as malware hashes or IP addresses, timelines of a group’s activity, and more."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1681" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1681> ;
	.

attack:T1682
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Query Public AI Services"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may query publicly accessible artificial intelligence (AI) services, such as large language models (LLMs), to support targeting and operations. In addition to searching websites or databases directly (i.e., Search Open Websites/Domains), adversaries may use AI services to synthesize, aggregate, and analyze publicly available information at scale."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1682" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0043 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1682> ;
	.

attack:T1683
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Generate Content"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may create or generate content to support targeting and operations. This content may be used to establish personas, impersonate known individuals or organizations, and support Social Engineering, fraud, or influence activities. Written materials, audio, images, video, or other media may be developed and tailored to the target and objective."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1683" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1683> ;
	.

attack:T1683.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1683 ;
	rdfs:label "Generate Content: Written Content"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may create or tailor written materials to support targeting and malicious operations. Content may include phishing lures, fraudulent financial communications, fabricated job postings, fabricated employment credentials and documentation, decoy documents, social media persona content, and supporting narratives used to sustain fabricated personas over time. Content may be authored manually, commissioned through third parties, or produced using AI-assisted tools."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1683.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1683/001> ;
	.

attack:T1683.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1683 ;
	rdfs:label "Generate Content: Audio-Visual Content"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may create or manipulate audio, image, and video content to support targeting and malicious operations. Adversaries may also use synthetic voice recordings, real-time altered audio or video during live interactions, fabricated profile photos and identity documents, or video content depicting fabricated or impersonated individuals."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1683.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0042 ;
	dcterms:coverage "PRE" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1683/002> ;
	.

attack:T1684
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Social Engineering"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may use social engineering techniques to influence users to take actions that result in unauthorized access, approval of changes, disclosure of sensitive information, or execution of adversary-supplied instructions (i.e., introduction of malicious payloads or software), while minimizing technical indicators."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1684" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1684> ;
	.

attack:T1684.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1684 ;
	rdfs:label "Social Engineering: Impersonation"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may impersonate a trusted person or organization in order to persuade and trick a target into performing some action on their behalf. For example, adversaries may communicate with victims (via Phishing for Information, Phishing, or Internal Spearphishing) while impersonating a known sender such as an executive, colleague, or third-party vendor. Established trust can then be leveraged to accomplish an adversary’s ultimate goals, possibly against multiple victims."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1684.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1684/001> ;
	.

attack:T1684.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1684 ;
	rdfs:label "Social Engineering: Email Spoofing"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may fake, or spoof, a sender’s identity by modifying the value of relevant email headers in order to establish contact with victims under false pretenses. In addition to actual email content, email headers (such as the FROM header, which contains the email address of the sender) may also be modified. Email clients display these headers when emails appear in a victim's inbox, which may cause modified emails to appear as if they were from the spoofed entity."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1684.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0005 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1684/002> ;
	.

attack:T1685
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Disable or Modify Tools"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may disable, degrade, or tamper with security tools or applications (e.g., endpoint detection and response (EDR) tools, intrusion detection systems (IDS), antivirus, logging agents, sensors, etc.) to impair or reduce visibility of defensive capabilities. This may include stopping specific services, killing processes, modifying or deleting tool configuration files and Registry keys, or preventing tools from updating."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1685" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Containers" ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1685> ;
	.

attack:T1685.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1685 ;
	rdfs:label "Disable or Modify Tools: Disable or Modify Windows Event Log"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may disable or modify the Windows Event Log to limit data that can be leveraged for detections and audits. Windows Event Log records user and system activity such as login attempts and process creation. This data is used by security tools and analysts to generate detections."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1685.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1685/001> ;
	.

attack:T1685.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1685 ;
	rdfs:label "Disable or Modify Tools: Disable or Modify Cloud Log"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "An adversary may disable or modify cloud logging capabilities and integrations to limit what data is collected on their activities and avoid detection. Cloud environments allow for collection and analysis of audit and application logs that provide insight into what activities a user does within the environment. If an adversary has sufficient permissions, they can disable or modify logging to avoid detection of their activities."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1685.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Identity Provider" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Office Suite" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1685/002> ;
	.

attack:T1685.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1685 ;
	rdfs:label "Disable or Modify Tools: Modify or Spoof Tool UI"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may spoof or manipulate security tool user interfaces (UIs) to falsely indicate tools are functioning normally and delay detection and response."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1685.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1685/003> ;
	.

attack:T1685.004
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1685 ;
	rdfs:label "Disable or Modify Tools: Disable or Modify Linux Audit System Log"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may disable or modify the Linux Audit system to hide malicious activity and avoid detection. Linux admins use the Linux Audit system to track security-relevant information on a system. The Linux Audit system operates at the kernel-level and maintains event logs on application and system activity such as process, network, file, and login events based on pre-configured rules."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1685.004" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1685/004> ;
	.

attack:T1685.005
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1685 ;
	rdfs:label "Disable or Modify Tools: Clear Windows Event Logs"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may clear Windows Event Logs to hide the activity of an intrusion. Windows Event Logs are a record of a computer's alerts and notifications. There are three system-defined sources of events: System, Application, and Security, with five event types: Error, Warning, Information, Success Audit, and Failure Audit."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1685.005" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1685/005> ;
	.

attack:T1685.006
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1685 ;
	rdfs:label "Disable or Modify Tools: Clear Linux or Mac System Logs"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may clear system logs to hide evidence of an intrusion. macOS and Linux both keep track of system or user-initiated actions via system logs. The majority of native system logging is stored under the `/var/log/` directory. Subfolders in this directory categorize logs by their related functions, such as:"@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1685.006" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1685/006> ;
	.

attack:T1686
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Disable or Modify System Firewall"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may disable or modify host-based or network firewalls to impair defensive mechanisms and enable further action. Once an adversary has gathered sufficient privileges, they can tamper with firewall services, policies, or rule sets to remove restrictions on inbound or outbound traffic."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1686" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1686> ;
	.

attack:T1686.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1686 ;
	rdfs:label "Disable or Modify System Firewall: Cloud Firewall"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may disable or modify a firewall within a cloud environment to bypass controls that limit access to cloud resources."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1686.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1686/001> ;
	.

attack:T1686.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1686 ;
	rdfs:label "Disable or Modify System Firewall: Network Device Firewall"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may disable network device-based firewall mechanisms entirely or add, delete, or modify particular rules in order to bypass controls limiting network usage."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1686.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1686/002> ;
	.

attack:T1686.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1686 ;
	rdfs:label "Disable or Modify System Firewall: Windows Host Firewall"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may disable or modify the Windows host firewall to bypass controls limiting network usage. This can include disabling the Windows host firewall entirely, suppressing specific profiles (domain, private, public), or adding, deleting, and modifying firewall rules to allow or restrict traffic."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1686.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1686/003> ;
	.

attack:T1687
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Exploitation for Defense Impairment"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may exploit vulnerabilities in security software, infrastructure, or defensive components to degrade, disable, or otherwise continue to impair their ability to prevent, detect, or respond to malicious activity."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1687" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "IaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "SaaS" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1687> ;
	.

attack:T1688
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Safe Mode Boot"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may abuse Windows safe mode to disable endpoint defenses. Safe mode starts up the Windows operating system with a limited set of drivers and services. Third-party security software such as endpoint detection and response (EDR) tools may not start after booting Windows in safe mode. There are two versions of safe mode: Safe Mode and Safe Mode with Networking. It is possible to start additional services after a safe mode boot."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1688" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1688> ;
	.

attack:T1689
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Downgrade Attack"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may downgrade or use a version of system features that may be outdated, vulnerable, and/or does not support updated security controls. Downgrade attacks typically take advantage of a system’s backward compatibility to force it into less secure modes of operation."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1689" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1689> ;
	.

attack:T1690
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Prevent Command History Logging"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may impair command history logging to hide commands they run on a compromised system. Various command interpreters keep track of the commands users type in their terminal so that users can retrace what they have done."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1690" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0112 ;
	dcterms:coverage "ESXi" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Linux" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Network Devices" ;
	dcterms:coverage "Windows" ;
	dcterms:coverage "macOS" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1690> ;
	.

attack:T1691
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Block Operational Technology Message"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may block messages between systems and devices in an OT/ICS environment to disrupt processes. Messages typically fall into two categories: (1) reporting messages that contain telemetry data about the current state of systems, devices, and processes and (2) command messages that contain instructions to control systems, devices, and processes."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1691" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0107 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1691> ;
	.

attack:T1691.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1691 ;
	rdfs:label "Block Operational Technology Message: Command Message"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may block a command message from reaching its intended target to prevent command execution. In OT networks, command messages are sent to provide instructions to control system devices. A blocked command message can inhibit response functions from correcting a disruption or unsafe condition."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1691.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0107 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1691/001> ;
	.

attack:T1691.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1691 ;
	rdfs:label "Block Operational Technology Message: Reporting Message"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may block or prevent a reporting message from reaching its intended target. In control systems, reporting messages contain telemetry data (e.g., I/O values) pertaining to the current state of equipment and the industrial process. By blocking these reporting messages, an adversary can potentially hide their actions from an operator."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1691.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0107 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1691/002> ;
	.

attack:T1692
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Unauthorized Message"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may send unauthorized messages to ICS systems and devices to evade defenses or manipulate processes. Unauthorized messages can be categorized as either reporting messages that contain telemetry data about the current state of systems, devices, and processes or as command messages which instruct systems and devices on how to operate."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1692" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0103 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0106 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1692> ;
	.

attack:T1692.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1692 ;
	rdfs:label "Unauthorized Message: Command Message"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may send unauthorized command messages to instruct control system assets to perform actions outside of their intended functionality, or without the logical preconditions to trigger their expected function. Command messages are used in ICS networks to give direct instructions to control systems devices."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1692.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0103 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0106 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1692/001> ;
	.

attack:T1692.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1692 ;
	rdfs:label "Unauthorized Message: Reporting Message"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may spoof reporting messages in control system environments for evasion and to impair process control. In control systems, reporting messages contain telemetry data (e.g., I/O values) pertaining to the current state of equipment and the industrial process. Reporting messages are important for monitoring the normal operation of a system or identifying important events such as deviations from expected values."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1692.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0103 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0106 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1692/002> ;
	.

attack:T1693
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Firmware"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Firmware is low-level software embedded in hardware that enables systems and devices to function properly and is commonly found in ICS environments. Adversaries may modify firmware on a system or device by installing malicious or vulnerable versions that enable them to achieve objectives such as Persistence, Impair Process Control, and Inhibit Response Function."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1693" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0110 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0107 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0106 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1693> ;
	.

attack:T1693.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1693 ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Firmware: System Firmware"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "System firmware on modern assets is often designed with an update feature. Older device firmware may be factory installed and require special reprograming equipment. When available, the firmware update feature enables vendors to remotely patch bugs and perform upgrades. Device firmware updates are often delegated to the user and may be done using a software update package. It may also be possible to perform this task over the network."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1693.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0110 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0107 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0106 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1693/001> ;
	.

attack:T1693.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1693 ;
	rdfs:label "Modify Firmware: Module Firmware"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may install malicious or vulnerable firmware onto modular hardware devices. Control system devices often contain modular hardware devices. These devices may have their own set of firmware that is separate from the firmware of the main control system equipment."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1693.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0110 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0107 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0106 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1693/002> ;
	.

attack:T1694
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Insecure Credentials"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may target insecure credentials as a means to persist on a system or device or move laterally from one system or device to another. Insecure credentials may appear as default credentials which are pre-configured credentials on a system, device, or software that are well-known in documentation or hard-coded credentials which are built into the system, device, or software that cannot be changed or not easily changed because of the impact on control processes."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1694" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0110 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0109 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1694> ;
	.

attack:T1694.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1694 ;
	rdfs:label "Insecure Credentials: Default Credentials"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may leverage manufacturer or supplier set default credentials on control system devices. These default credentials may have administrative permissions and may be necessary for initial configuration of the device. It is general best practice to change the passwords for these accounts as soon as possible, but some manufacturers may have devices that have passwords or usernames that cannot be changed."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1694.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0110 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0109 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1694/001> ;
	.

attack:T1694.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1694 ;
	rdfs:label "Insecure Credentials: Hardcoded Credentials"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may leverage credentials that are hardcoded in software or firmware to gain an unauthorized interactive user session to an asset. Examples credentials that may be hardcoded in an asset include:"@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1694.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0110 ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0109 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1694/002> ;
	.

attack:T1695
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Block Communications"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Operational technology communications occur over serial COM, Ethernet, Wi-Fi, cellular (4G/5G), and satellite mediums. Adversaries may block communications to prevent reporting messages and command messages from reaching their intended target devices disrupting processes, operations, and causing cyber-physical impacts."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1695" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0107 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1695> ;
	.

attack:T1695.001
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1695 ;
	rdfs:label "Block Communications: Serial COM"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may block access to serial COM to prevent instructions or configurations from reaching target devices. Serial Communication ports (COM) allow communication with control system devices. Devices can receive command and configuration messages over such serial COM. Devices also use serial COM to send command and reporting messages. Blocking device serial COM may also block command messages and block reporting messages."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1695.001" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0107 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1695/001> ;
	.

attack:T1695.002
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1695 ;
	rdfs:label "Block Communications: Ethernet"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may block access to Ethernet communications to prevent instructions or configurations messages from reaching target systems and devices. Ethernet connections allow for communications between IT and OT systems and devices. Blocking Ethernet communications may also block command and reporting messages."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1695.002" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0107 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1695/002> ;
	.

attack:T1695.003
	a
		owl:Class ,
		action:Technique
		;
	rdfs:subClassOf attack:T1695 ;
	rdfs:label "Block Communications: Wi-Fi"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Adversaries may block access to Wi-Fi communications to prevent messages from reaching target systems and devices. Wi-Fi connections allow for communications between IT and OT systems and devices. Blocking Wi-Fi communications may also block command and reporting messages."@en ;
	action:techniqueID "T1695.003" ;
	dcterms:subject attack-tactic:TA0107 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf <https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/> ;
	dcterms:modified "2026-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
	rdfs:seeAlso <https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1695/003> ;
	.
